// Generated by scripts/build-knowledge.mjs from docs/*.md. Do not edit; run npm run knowledge.
/** FBRX's guides, one entry per section: d = file, t = section title, x = text. */
export const DOC_SECTIONS: ReadonlyArray<{ d: string; t: string; x: string }> = [{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud)","x":"From 2.0, FBRX Command can run in the cloud for everyone: people make an **FBRX account**, buy Pro or Ultra for\nthemselves, and sign in on any computer or phone, which then runs what the account bought. Organizations run their\nown FBRX Command tenant there (see LICENSING.md, *Prices*). This page is how accounts, buying and organizations'\nplans work."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › How buying works","x":"| Where | How people pay | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| The website and FBRX on a computer | **Stripe Checkout**: cards, and the Apple Pay and Google Pay wallets, which Checkout shows by itself where the person's browser and device have them | One hosted, PCI-compliant page; no card details ever reach FBRX |\n| The iPhone and iPad app | **In-app purchase** (StoreKit 2) | The App Store's rules: an app that unlocks paid features sells them through the App Store |\n| The Android app | **Google Play Billing** | The same rule on Google Play |\n\nEvery purchase, wherever it was made, becomes an **entitlement** on the account (one per subscription item or store\npurchase, kept current by the provider's notifications). The account's edition is the fullest one its valid\nentitlements give, so Ultra bought on a phone signs in on a Mac, a PC or Linux too. An account signs its computers up\nwith an ordinary FBRX license key (Ed25519, verified offline like any other): up to the edition bought, with no\norganization in it, running a few days past the period paid for and never more than 35 days, so computers fetch a fresh\none as the subscription renews and a refund reaches them in time.\n\n**Each computer, up to those the edition covers.** An edition covers as many computers as it includes (2 for Pro,\n3 for Ultra; the operator's price list, LICENSING.md) and the extra ones bought on top. Essentials counts none: it is\nfree on every computer. Each computer signed in to the account gets a key of its own, carrying its install id, which no other computer accepts. Computers count in the order\nthey first signed in, so signing in again keeps a computer's place; one past them runs Essentials and says why\n(**Settings → FBRX account**), with **Add a computer** when the plan was bought on the website or in FBRX: it adds the\nedition's extra-computer item (`fbrx_standard_extra_computer_monthly`, `fbrx_ultra_extra_computer_monthly`, or their\n`_yearly` prices on a yearly plan) to the\nStripe subscription, prorated. The App Store and Google Play sell no extra computers. Signing a computer out frees its\nplace: that computer gives its key back the next time it renews (every six hours while FBRX runs). When a plan moves to\nthe other edition in the Stripe portal, its extra computers move with it, at that edition's price.\n\nBlack Hole Edition is never sold to an account: it is an add-on in an FBRX Command tenant (LICENSING.md)."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › On a computer","x":"**Settings → FBRX account** (or *I have an FBRX account* on the first screen) signs in, or makes a new account. The\ncomputer brings the account's license and turns that edition on by itself (\"whatever the account has\"); it renews the\nlicense every six hours while FBRX runs, and when the account's purchase ends, the computer goes back to what it had\n(Essentials on its own). Signed in, **Get Pro** and **Get Ultra** open the checkout in the browser, monthly or\nyearly as chosen beside them (a year at the price list's months: ten to start), and **Manage\nbilling** opens the Stripe portal; Settings → Edition offers the same on the locked editions. Signing out takes the\naccount's license away again. The account token is kept in the vault, like the computer's FBRX Command credential.\n\nWhich FBRX Command accounts live on is built into the app (`FBRX_ACCOUNT_SERVER` when building, or in the environment);\nwhen none is, the sign-in asks for its address.\n\nA license from an organization (FBRX Command, *managed*) always wins over an account's; between a key typed into\nSettings → License and an account's, the fuller one counts."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › On a computer › Offline accounts (local, no cloud)","x":"A computer that runs FBRX without the cloud — a local-only environment, or the Beta before the account server is live\n— can make an **offline account** instead: **Settings → Account → Make an offline account**. It is a local identity\n(a name, an optional email, an optional start password) kept on the computer; it never reaches a server. It pairs\nwith a license key for its edition: a **Beta key**, or a key from a local FBRX Command (below). An optional start\npassword locks FBRX each time it starts: a lock on the screen, not encryption (to keep saved passwords encrypted\nuntil you type a password, turn on the vault's own password at start, under Credentials). The cloud FBRX account and the offline account are separate; a computer uses\none or the other.\n\n**Beta keys** are ordinary license keys with a tier and an expiry — for example Ultra until the Beta ends. Make them\nfrom FBRX Command → Licenses (set the tier and an expiry date), or offline with `npm run license:issue -- --tier\nultra --expires 2026-12-31 --customer \"Beta tester\"`. The computer activates the key under Settings → License, as any\nother.\n\nA **local FBRX Command** — one an organization runs for itself (`deploy/control-plane`, with sign-up closed) — keeps\nworking as it always has: it issues license keys, enrolls computers and pushes policy, all on the organization's own\nnetwork with no dependence on FBRX's cloud. The cloud account server is for the public, shared FBRX Command; a local\none needs none of it."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › Signing in, and getting back in","x":"An account is an email and a password. FBRX emails a link to **confirm the address** when the account is made\n(*Send it again* in Settings → FBRX account and on the website's account page); an account becomes an organization's\nmember, or starts an organization, only once its email is confirmed, so nobody can claim someone else's address.\n**Forgot your password?** (in FBRX's sign-in, on the website and in the phone app) emails a link to choose a new one:\nit works once, for an hour, and signs the account out everywhere. Both links open the website's account page\n(`FBRX_CP_ACCOUNT_URL`), with the token after `#`, so it never reaches a server's logs.\n\nEvery sign-in says where it is: a **computer** (its name, system, FBRX version and install id), a **phone** (the\napp) or the **website**. Settings → FBRX account → **Signed in on** and the website list them, and sign any of the others\nout: its token stops working at once, and the computer gives back the license it was given the next time it renews (a\ncomputer whose sign-in simply ended keeps it until it runs out).\nThe same install signing in again takes the place of its last sign-in, so each computer is listed once."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › Signing in, and getting back in › Where accounts are kept","x":"In the database of the FBRX Command that runs in the cloud for everyone (`control-plane.db` in its data folder):\n\n| What | How it is kept |\n| --- | --- |\n| Email, name | As typed; the email is unique, whatever its capitals |\n| Password | Never itself: a scrypt hash with its own salt, compared in constant time; ten wrong tries lock the account for a quarter of an hour |\n| Sign-ins | One row each (where, when, last seen). The token the app keeps is signed by FBRX Command and names that row, so ending the row ends the token |\n| Emailed links | Only a SHA-256 hash of each, used once, then expired (a week to confirm, an hour to reset) |\n| Purchases | The provider's ids and states (`entitlements`): never a card number, which stays with Stripe, Apple or Google |\n| License keys | Signed fresh from the account's purchases each time a computer asks; not stored |\n\nDeleting the account deletes all of it (sign-ins, links, memberships and purchases' records go with it)."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › Organizations and members","x":"An organization's FBRX Command gives editions to people, not just computers: a **member** is an FBRX account the\norganization added, with the edition it gives that person (`POST /v1/admin/members` `{ email, tier }`; Essentials\nmembers cost nothing). A member who signs in on any computer sees the organization under **Your organizations** and\n**Join this computer**: the computer enrolls with a one-time token (fifteen minutes) that carries the member's edition,\nand turns it on. A computer that is in the organization already (it joined with a token from **Deploy & enroll** or a\nlicense key) becomes the member's as soon as they sign in on it: it runs their edition and counts as theirs under\n**Members** (another member's computer stays theirs). When the organization changes a member's edition, the computers\nthey joined follow it; removing a member leaves their computers in the organization on Essentials. This is how an admin\nsigns in on any computer and gets Black Hole Edition there.\n\nA member runs their edition up to what the organization's license allows. An organization billed through FBRX Command\nin the cloud has a license that allows everything, so its members' editions decide. One whose licenses are issued by\nhand (Licenses) runs at most the newest license's edition: when someone is given more, **Members** says so, with\n**Issue a … license** at the edition they need. On the computer, **Settings → FBRX account** shows the edition and the\norganization it comes from (what the account bought is under *Bought*). **FBRX accounts** (the operator's list) gives\npeople an edition of their own, as if bought; in an organization, editions come from Members.\n\nFBRX OS accepts an edition only from a license signed with a key it was built to trust. FBRX OS you build yourself\ntrusts your own key; when your FBRX Command runs on another computer, build FBRX OS with FBRX Command's key\n(`Install FBRX OS --signing-key`, see [INSTALL.md](INSTALL.md)). Until then the computer runs Pro, and\n*Settings → Edition* says why."},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › Organizations and members › Inviting someone without an FBRX account (no website or email needed)","x":"**Members → Add a member** takes an email and an edition. When that email has a confirmed FBRX account, it is added\nat once. Otherwise FBRX Command makes an **invitation**: a code like `FBRX-7K3M-Q9TD-2HXP-W4NC`, shown once to the admin\n(with the FBRX Command address to go with it) and emailed too when this FBRX Command sends email. The person, in FBRX\nOS, opens **Settings → Account → I have an invitation**, enters the code (and the FBRX Command address if FBRX asks),\nsees which organization invited them and at what edition, and either chooses a password (a new account) or enters the\npassword of the account that email already has. FBRX Command makes or confirms the account, adds it as a member, and\nthe computer signs in and joins the organization at that edition, all in one step.\n\n* The code works once, for 14 days (the API takes `days`, 1 to 90). Inviting the same email again replaces the old\n  code; **Withdraw** in the Invitations list ends one. Only the code's hash is kept.\n* The invitation counts as confirming the email (the organization gave the code to that person). If someone else had\n  made an account with that email and never confirmed it, the password the invited person chooses replaces theirs and\n  their sign-ins end.\n* Codes work while public sign-up is off, so an FBRX Command can take only the people it invites.\n* A computer already in another organization still signs in; it lists the new organization with its **Join this\n  computer** button.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /v1/admin/members` | The organization's members, their editions and how many computers each joined |\n| `POST /v1/admin/members` `{ email, tier }` | Adds an account (it must exist, with its email confirmed) |\n| `PATCH /v1/admin/members/:accountId` `{ tier }` | Changes a member's edition, on their computers too |\n| `DELETE /v1/admin/members/:accountId` | Removes a member |\n| `GET /v1/admin/invitations` | Invitations not used yet (pending or expired) |\n| `POST /v1/admin/invitations` `{ email, tier, days? }` | Adds the member now, or invites them: `{ kind: 'added', member }` or `{ kind: 'invited', invitation, code, server, emailed }` |\n| `DELETE /v1/admin/invitations/:id` | Withdraws an invitation |\n| `POST /v1/accounts/invitations/preview` `{ code }` | (Anyone with the code) the organization, email and edition it is for, and whether the email has an account |\n| `POST /v1/accounts/invitations/accept` `{ code, password, name?, device }` | Makes or confirms the account, adds it, and signs in: like `login`, plus `tenantId` |\n| `POST /v1/accounts/memberships/:tenantId/enroll` | (The account) a one-time enrollment token for this computer |"},{"d":"docs/ACCOUNTS.md","t":"FBRX accounts and payments (FBRX Command in the cloud) › An organization's FBRX Command in the cloud","x":"Anyone with an FBRX account can start an FBRX Command for their organization (Settings → FBRX account → **Start an\nFBRX Command** in FBRX, or `POST /v1/accounts/tenants` `{ name, kind }`). They own it, sign in to its console with\nthe account's email and password, and are a member on Essentials. Nothing is charged until the owner starts the plan:\n\n* **Billing** (owners pay; admins see it): the owner chooses **Monthly** or **Yearly** (a year at the price list's\n  months, ten to start), and **Start the FBRX Command plan** opens Stripe Checkout for the organization fee at that\n  interval; **Manage billing** opens the Stripe portal. Everything on the plan is paid the same way. A running plan\n  moves to the other with **Pay yearly** or **Pay monthly**: every item goes to its price at the new interval in one\n  update, and Stripe starts the new period that day, crediting what is left of the old one. The page shows what a month\n  or a year comes to: the plan;\n  each member at the edition the organization gives them, once whatever number of its computers they join, up to\n  those their edition includes (2 Pro, 5 Ultra, 15 Black Hole Edition), and each further computer of theirs at its extra\n  price; and each paid computer no paying member uses (shared, lab and student computers) at its edition's price.\n  The prices are the operator's price list (LICENSING.md); Essentials is free. Student and child computers (only ever\n  in an FBRX Command) run the learner edition and count as Pro. **Members** shows who uses more computers than\n  their edition includes.\n* The plan's per-person and per-computer items in Stripe follow by themselves (a few seconds after a change, and every\n  quarter of an hour), at the plan's interval, prorated by Stripe.\n* While the plan runs, FBRX Command holds one license for the organization that allows everything; each computer runs\n  what it was given, and **Essentials when nobody gave it anything**. Without the plan (not started, or ended), every\n  computer runs Essentials.\n* **Members** gives editions to people (above); **FBRX Server is coming** shows on Billing and the Overview: not on\n  sale yet.\n\nOrganizations the operator sets up by hand (Tenants, `billing = manual`) keep working as before, with the licenses\nthe operator issues. The operator sees every account under **FBRX accounts**."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console)","x":"FBRX Command is the team tenant controller: the console served by the control plane at its public URL. It is the place\nto watch, configure, update and support every FBRX Endpoint computer in real time, with RMM-style tooling (remote\ncommands, packages, credentials, updates, backups and audit). A license issued here can enroll computers by itself:\npaste the key into FBRX Endpoint and it joins the tenant (see [LICENSING.md](LICENSING.md#joining-fbrx-command)).\n\nTo run it on a computer of your own (a family, a school, a small office), double-click **Install FBRX Command** in\nthe FBRX folder; it opens the first-run page when it is done. It looks for new versions of itself and shows them on\n**Releases**, where **Update now** installs one in place\n([DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#fbrx-command-on-a-computer-of-your-own)). Hosted setups:\n[DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#2-control-plane)."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Work, School or Home","x":"Every tenant has a **kind**, picked before anything else, both at first-run setup and in **Tenants → New tenant**:\n\n| Kind | For | Computers | Quick setup makes | Help goes to |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Work** | A business, nonprofit or team | Staff up to FBRX Endpoint Pro, IT up to Endpoint Ultra | **Staff** (up to Pro) and **IT** | The IT computer you mark as a receiver |\n| **School** | A school or co-op | Teachers up to Pro in classroom mode, IT up to Ultra, students on **FBRX OS Education** | **Teachers**, **IT** and **Students** | The IT computer you mark as a receiver |\n| **Home** | A family | Parents on FBRX Endpoint, children on **FBRX OS Home** | **Parents** and **Children** | Every parent's computer, automatically |\n\nThe kind sets the wording in FBRX Command and on the computers, the groups **quick setup** makes (each with its own\nenrollment token, shown once with a provisioning file to download), who a computer can be used by, and the defaults\nfor learner computers. One FBRX Command can run tenants of every kind side by side; the tenant switcher shows each\none's kind. Change a tenant's kind later under **Profiles & groups → Organization defaults** or **Tenants**; issuing a\nlicense with a kind also sets it. Unattended installs can pick the first tenant's kind with `FBRX_CP_ORGANIZATION_KIND`\n(`work`, `school` or `home`; see DEPLOYMENT.md). Families: see [HOME.md](HOME.md). Schools: [EDUCATION.md](EDUCATION.md)."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Pages","x":"| Section | Page | What it is for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Fleet | **Overview** | Online/offline/alerting devices, agent runs, tool calls, policy denials and errors over 24 h, version and platform mix, recent alerts |\n| | **Devices** | Search and filter the fleet (student and child computers and help desk receivers are marked); select devices for bulk commands (sync, update, back up, restart service, notify, …); **Update everyone now**. A device's **Configuration** tab sets who uses it, whether it receives help desk tickets, its **Edition** (*Essentials only*, *Up to Pro*, *Up to Ultra*, or its group's: Ultra only ever comes from here) and its overrides; its header shows the edition it runs |\n| | Device detail | Live health and services, metrics charts, installed plugins, versions, audit chain head; run any command and watch its result; move to a group; set update channel or pin; who uses it (staff or student; parent or child at home); whether it receives help desk tickets; per-device overrides; its backups; retire |\n| | **Alerts & events** | Service failures, circuit breaks, policy denials, student and child safety alerts and other device events; acknowledge them |\n| | **Help desk** | Every ticket sent from the organization's computers, its conversation and the computer's details; answer, take, change status and priority; which computers receive tickets |\n| | **Messages** | The *Everyone* conversation every computer sees in Messages: post to it, remove what does not belong ([MESSAGES.md](MESSAGES.md)) |\n| Configuration | **Profiles & groups** | Organization defaults: the kind (Work, School, Home), *New versions* (install automatically, tell people, off), the help desk on or off, Messages on or off, default profile and channel. **Quick setup** for the tenant's kind (Set up for work, for a school, for a family) until its groups exist. Profiles hold managed settings, governance policy and *locks* (paths users cannot change). Groups attach a profile, who uses the computers (staff or student; parent or child), the edition (*Essentials only*, *Up to Pro* or *Up to Ultra*: computers open in Essentials and their people switch up to it), *New versions*, an update channel and a pinned version. **FBRX Mesh**: the organization's computers mesh together without pairing codes, and the **hive** (which groups may use the AI of servers with the ai role), with the servers in it ([MESH.md](MESH.md#organizations)) |\n| | **Governance** | What the AI may do on the organization's computers: each profile's policy (tool rules, the folders it may use, the web, the shell, AI providers, approvals) as JSON, checked before it is saved and pushed within seconds. Computers show it read-only |\n| | **Deploy & enroll** | The address computers join at (and, for FBRX Command with its own certificate, the fingerprint they check); download the latest installers (the newest stable release uploaded under Releases, or the current FBRX OS from FBRX's downloads when nothing as new was uploaded); create enrollment tokens (group, who uses the computers, usage limit, expiry, template snapshot); download the matching `fbrx-provision.json`, a **Mac profile** (.mobileconfig for Jamf, Mosyle, Kandji, Intune…) and a **Windows script** (Intune Win32 app) |\n| | **Credentials** | Organization secrets scoped to the tenant, a group or a single device; pushed into device vaults as read-only managed secrets; rotate or delete centrally |\n| | **Plugins** | Upload plugin packages (manifest and permissions shown), deploy to devices or groups |\n| | **Backups** | Snapshots uploaded by devices; download, mark as an enrollment template, delete |\n| Platform | **Releases** | FBRX Command's own version and new versions of it (**Update now**, **Skip this version**, automatic checks on or off); upload FBRX OS installers (or let CI do it), publish to `stable`/`beta`/`dev`, staged rollout percentage |\n| | **Licenses** | Issue and revoke signed licenses per tenant: the most the computers may run (up to Endpoint Pro or Ultra), the kind (Work, School or Home, which also sets the tenant's kind), seats and expiry, and whether computers that activate the key join the tenant automatically; copy keys for offline activation |\n| | **Tenants** | (superadmin) Create tenants (pick Work, School or Home first, then run its quick setup), see each one's kind, change it, suspend them |\n| | **FBRX accounts** | (superadmin) Everyone with an FBRX account here, the edition their purchases give them and their organizations; give one an edition (support, a promotion), disable one ([ACCOUNTS.md](ACCOUNTS.md)) |\n| | **Cloud AI** | (superadmin) FBRX Cloud AI: the month so far against the service's budget, each edition's tokens a month, every account's use (its own limit, extra tokens, the limit lifted for the month, turned off) and promo codes ([CLOUD_AI.md](CLOUD_AI.md)) |\n| | **Pricing** | (superadmin) The price list ([LICENSING.md](LICENSING.md#prices)): Pro and Ultra on their own; the organization fee,"},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Pages (2)","x":" with another for larger organizations from a size and for Home ones; each person's price in an organization (it also covers a shared computer nobody pays for), how many computers each edition includes and each further one's price; the volume discount in steps (from so many seats, so much off); what a year costs paying yearly, in months' price; example bills, a month and a year, before saving. **Stripe** shows, for every lookup key, whether Stripe charges the same, and **Update Stripe** brings it in step. The MDM add-on: on sale or not, its price per managed computer (the same whatever the computer's edition), and what it earns after card fees and the cost of a managed computer. Organizations' bills follow at once (**Billing**); organizations turn MDM on per group (**Profiles & groups → Device management**). Every change is in the audit log |\n| Access | **Users & API keys** | Invite users with a role, reset access, create API keys for automation |\n| | **Webhooks** | Signed outgoing webhooks for fleet events |\n| | **Audit log** | Every admin action and device enrollment, hash-chained and verifiable |\n| | Account (your name, top right) | Change your password (signs out your other sessions) and enable TOTP MFA |"},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Pages › Building a profile without JSON","x":"A profile (and a single device's overrides) is built from:\n\n* **Ready-made** bundles, one click each and one click to take out again: **No Fun Extras** (no easter eggs, jokes,\n  goose or start-up sound), **Private by default** (AI on the computer, chats start offline, no usage statistics or\n  clipboard history), **Locked down** (no mesh, no Local API, no sharing AI), **No chatting** (Messages off),\n  **Strong protection**, **Daily backups**,\n  **Meeting-room safe**, **Calm and quick** and **Share AI across computers**. They combine; the profiles table shows\n  which ones each profile carries.\n* **The usual settings**, by area (AI, fun and look, privacy, screens and meetings, messages, mesh, backups, protection,\n  updates, everyday), each with an **(i)** saying what it does: *Not set / On / Off* for switches, a list for choices,\n  and **Lock** so people cannot change it on their computer. *Find a setting* searches names and explanations.\n* **Advanced: everything as text**: the same profile as JSON (any FBRX OS setting) and the locked paths, one per line,\n  for settings the switches do not cover. Both views are the same profile.\n\n**Antivirus:** device detail shows each computer's antivirus and its state (the Health column flags *Antivirus at\nrisk* and *Antivirus needs attention*). To choose it for a group, put `{ \"protection\": { \"provider\": \"shield\" } }` (or\n`defender`, `product:sophos`, `auto`…) in a profile's settings and lock `protection.provider`. See [SHIELD.md](SHIELD.md).\n\n**Calendars for everyone:** to let computers sign in to Outlook / Microsoft 365 calendars, put your Microsoft app\n(client) ID in a profile's settings, `{ \"calendar\": { \"microsoft\": { \"clientId\": \"…\", \"tenant\": \"yourdomain\" } } }`, and\nlock `calendar.microsoft` if people should not change it. See [CALENDAR.md](CALENDAR.md).\n\nChanges to profiles, groups, credentials and licenses are pushed to online devices over their WebSocket within\nseconds; offline devices pick them up when they reconnect. Device detail and the overview update live."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Pages › Help desk routing","x":"A computer sends a ticket to FBRX Command over its authenticated device connection; FBRX Command stores it, numbers it\n(#1, #2, … per organization), and pushes it at once to every **receiver** computer and to open consoles. Replies and\nstatus changes travel the same way back to the computer that asked. Receivers that are off get the queue when they\nnext connect. The `ticket.created` and `ticket.updated` webhooks hand tickets to another system if you have one."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Pages › Automatic updates","x":"*New versions* (organization or group): **Install automatically** sets each computer's update settings so it installs\nnew versions by itself when nobody is using it, from FBRX Command's published releases (electron-updater) or from the\nGitHub repository it was installed from; **Tell people** offers them; **Off** stops the checks. **Devices → Update\neveryone now** sends `update.install` to every active computer (online ones start at once)."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Roles","x":"| Permission | superadmin | owner | admin | operator | viewer |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| See devices, events, plugins, releases | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Send standard commands (ping, sync, update check, diagnostics, notify, service restart) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | |\n| Manage devices (groups, channel, pin, retire) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | |\n| Privileged commands (agent run, plugin install/uninstall, update install, back up, lock vault, restart app) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | | |\n| Profiles, policy, credentials, enrollment, plugins, backups, webhooks, audit | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | | |\n| Users and API keys | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | | |\n| Set up sign in with Google or Microsoft | ✔ | ✔ | | | |\n| Read help desk tickets | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Answer help desk tickets | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | |\n| Read the organization's *Everyone* messages | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Post to *Everyone* and remove messages | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | |\n| Read licenses | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | | |\n| Tenants, releases, issuing licenses | ✔ | | | | |"},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Sign in with Google or Microsoft","x":"Staff can sign in to FBRX Command with their school or work account instead of a separate password. Each\norganization (tenant) adds its own connection under **Users & API keys → Sign in with Google or Microsoft**; only\nits owners (and the platform operator) can. The form walks through the provider side and shows the **redirect\naddress** to paste there: `https:///v1/auth/sso/callback`, so `FBRX_CP_PUBLIC_URL` must be the\nreal https:// address people use.\n\n**Google Workspace** (about five minutes, with a Workspace admin account)\n\n1. Google Cloud console → pick or create a project → **APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen**: *Internal*, app\n   name *FBRX Command*.\n2. **Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application**; add the redirect address under\n   *Authorized redirect URIs*.\n3. Paste the **Client ID** and **Client secret** into FBRX Command, with your Workspace domain (`school.org`).\n\n**Microsoft 365** (about five minutes, in the Microsoft Entra admin center)\n\n1. **App registrations → New registration**: *FBRX Command*, *Accounts in this organizational directory only*,\n   Redirect URI type *Web* with the redirect address.\n2. Copy the **Application (client) ID** and the **Directory (tenant) ID**.\n3. **Certificates & secrets → New client secret**, copy its **Value** (note the expiry date and renew it in time).\n4. Paste them into FBRX Command with your email domain. Optional: *Token configuration → Add optional claim → ID →\n   email* (without it, the sign-in name is used).\n\nThen decide:\n\n* **Invite only** (default): add people under *Users* with their email and no password; they use the button.\n  Or **let new people in on their first sign-in** with a role you choose (viewer, operator or admin).\n* **Require single sign-on**: passwords stop working for everyone in the organization except owners, who keep\n  password + two-step codes as the way back in if the provider has a problem.\n* **Test sign-in** opens the provider in a new tab to check the setup.\n\nOn the sign-in page the **Sign in with Google / Microsoft** buttons appear once any organization has a connection.\nIf several organizations use the same provider, people type their email first so FBRX Command picks the right one.\nA connection only ever signs people into its own organization: someone who belongs to another organization, and the\nplatform operator, are never signed in through it. Google and Microsoft handle passwords and two-step verification\nfor these sign-ins.\n\n`superadmin` is the platform operator (you) and is not bound to a tenant — use the tenant switcher in the top bar.\nEveryone else belongs to exactly one tenant and only ever sees that tenant's data. Users can only grant roles at or\nbelow their own."},{"d":"docs/ADMIN_CONSOLE.md","t":"FBRX Command (admin console) › Automation","x":"Everything in the console is a JSON API under `/v1/admin/*`, authenticated with `Authorization: Bearer `\n(a session or an API key, `fbrx_ak_…`). Superadmins select a tenant with the `x-fbrx-tenant` header.\n\n```bash\nKEY=fbrx_ak_...\n# All devices that are offline\ncurl -s https://fleet.example.com/v1/admin/devices -H \"authorization: Bearer $KEY\" | jq '.[] | select(.online == false) | .name'\n# Ask every device in a group to back up\ncurl -s https://fleet.example.com/v1/admin/commands/bulk -H \"authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"type\":\"backup.create\",\"payload\":{\"upload\":true},\"groupId\":\"grp_...\"}'\n```\n\nReal-time admin events (device status, command results, audit entries) stream over `wss://…/v1/admin/ws`."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves","x":"FBRX OS is built so a workstation can be lost, replaced or duplicated without losing anything: settings, policy,\nvault secrets, conversations, agent memory, plugins and their data, connectors, the workspace and (optionally)\ndownloaded models all live under one data folder and travel together in one encrypted snapshot.\n\n> This page is about FBRX's own data. An image of the **whole computer** (Windows, the apps, every file), made on a\n> schedule to a drive, a network folder or an FBRX Server and checked by starting it, is **FBRX Time**\n> (*Backup & restore → Whole computer*, [TIME.md](TIME.md))."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Snapshots","x":"A snapshot is a single `.fbrxsnap` file:\n\n```\n\"FBRXSNAP\" | u32 header length | header JSON | AES-256-GCM( tar.gz ) | 16-byte GCM tag\n```\n\n* The key is derived from your **snapshot passphrase** with scrypt; the header (app version, schema version,\n  hostname, creation time, KDF parameters) is authenticated as GCM additional data, so it cannot be altered.\n* Inside: a consistent copy of the database (`VACUUM INTO`, safe while running), `plugins/`, `plugin-data/`,\n  `workspace/`, optionally `models/`, and a manifest with the SHA-256 of every file (checked on restore).\n* The manifest also carries the vault's data key, so the restored machine can read every secret even though its OS\n  keychain is different. That is why the passphrase matters: **anyone with the file and the passphrase has your\n  secrets.** Use a long passphrase and keep it in your password manager.\n* Machine-bound items are never captured: logs, the downloaded runtime, temp files, the local keychain fallback."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Snapshots › Creating snapshots","x":"| How | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| On demand | Desktop: **Backup → Create snapshot**. Headless: `fbrx-headless backup \"\" --label before-upgrade` |\n| On a schedule | **Backup → Schedule**: interval, retention, folder (point it at a synced or network folder for off-machine copies), include models |\n| Remotely | Admin console: device → **Back up now** (or the `backup.create` command, also in bulk). The snapshot is uploaded to the control plane, which keeps the latest N per device |\n\nScheduled and remote backups run unattended, so they take the passphrase from the vault secret named in\n`backup.passphraseSecret` (default `FBRX_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE`). Set it on the workstation, or — better for fleets —\ncreate it once as a **tenant secret** in the admin console so every device gets it and you can restore any of them."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Restoring","x":"Restores are staged: the snapshot is decrypted and verified into `.restore-staging/`, the vault key is re-bound to\nthis machine's keychain, and the app restarts. On the next boot the staged data is swapped in *before* anything is\nopened. The data it replaced is kept in `.pre-restore//` (the three most recent are retained), so a\nmistaken restore can be undone by hand.\n\nSnapshots from older versions restore into newer ones (the database migrates forward on boot). A snapshot from a\nnewer version is refused until the target is updated."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Restoring › Migrate — \"as if nothing happened\"","x":"Use **migrate** when the new machine *replaces* the old one (hardware refresh, lost laptop, reinstall):\n\n* Everything comes back, including the fleet identity: same device record in the console, same group, managed\n  configuration, license and secrets. The device simply reconnects from new hardware.\n* The audit log continues on the same hash chain and records the restore.\n* Retire or wipe the old machine afterwards — two machines with one identity will fight over the same device record.\n\nDesktop: **Backup → Restore from file → Migrate**. Headless: `fbrx-headless restore snapshot.fbrxsnap \"\"`."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Restoring › Clone — a new machine from a template","x":"Use **clone** to stand up *additional* machines that start from the same state (a golden image):\n\n* Keeps settings, local policy, the user's own vault secrets, plugins, connectors, workspace and memory.\n* Drops the fleet identity, the managed layers (settings, policy, secrets, license) and queued commands, so the copy\n  enrolls as a new device and receives its own managed configuration.\n* Drops the mesh identity and paired devices, so each copy pairs its own phone and computers.\n\nDesktop: **Restore from file → Clone**. Headless: `fbrx-headless restore snapshot.fbrxsnap \"\" --clone`.\nFleet: pick the snapshot as the **template** on an enrollment token — every machine provisioned with that token\nrestores it as a clone before enrolling (see [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#4-rolling-out-to-workstations))."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › Restoring › Moving to a new computer, step by step","x":"To keep projects, tasks, notes, snippets and the FBRX calendar the same on two computers you go on using, rather than\nmoving everything to a new one, use **FBRX Sync** instead ([MESH.md](MESH.md#fbrx-sync-the-same-projects-tasks-notes-and-calendar-on-your-computers)).\n\n1. Old machine: **Backup → Create snapshot** (tick *include models* to avoid re-downloading them), or take one\n   remotely from the console.\n2. Copy the `.fbrxsnap` file over (USB, network share), or download it from the console (**Backups**).\n3. New machine: install FBRX OS (same or newer version). On the first screen choose **Restore from a backup**\n   (or later: **Backup → Restore from file**), pick the file, enter the passphrase, choose **Migrate**.\n4. The app restarts on the restored state. Re-download the local runtime if it isn't bundled (Local AI page)."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › If the OS keychain is lost","x":"Normally the vault's data key is protected by Windows DPAPI (tied to the user account) or, on a Mac, by a key file\nin the data folder that only your user can read. If the account is rebuilt or the key lost, FBRX OS starts with the\nvault **locked**. Unlock it\nwith the **recovery passphrase** (set it in **Vault → Recovery**; strongly recommended) — or restore a snapshot,\nwhich carries the key itself."},{"d":"docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md","t":"Backup, restore and machine moves › The control plane","x":"Everything the control plane knows is in its data directory (`/data` in Docker):\n\n| Item | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| `control-plane.db` (+ `-wal`, `-shm`) | Tenants, users, devices, configuration, secrets (sealed), licenses, audit |\n| `keys/` | `jwt.key`, `master.key` (seals secrets), `license-signing.pem`. Unless supplied via environment variables, **losing these is unrecoverable**: sealed secrets become unreadable and licenses can no longer be issued under the same key |\n| `releases/`, `snapshots/`, `packages/` | Uploaded files |\n\nBack it up with the volume stopped, or take a consistent online copy of the database:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f deploy/control-plane/docker-compose.yml exec control-plane \\\n  node -e \"const {DatabaseSync}=require('node:sqlite');new DatabaseSync('/data/control-plane.db').exec(\\\"VACUUM INTO '/data/backup.db'\\\")\"\n```\n\n…then copy `backup.db`, `keys/` and the file folders off the host. To move the control plane, restore the data\ndirectory on the new host (rename `backup.db` to `control-plane.db`) and point DNS at it; devices reconnect on\ntheir own because they address it by URL."},{"d":"docs/BROWSER.md","t":"FBRX Browser","x":"A web browser inside FBRX OS, in its own window. It is a real browser — tabs, an address bar, back and forward,\nbookmarks, history, downloads — that also works with the rest of FBRX: the local AI reads and explains the page, the\nVault fills in your sign-ins and offers to save new ones, Notes keeps a page, FBRX Shield checks what you download, and\nan organization's FBRX Command decides which sites may open.\n\nOpen it from the sidebar (**Browser**), the tray (**Open FBRX Browser**), or Settings → Browser. It opens in a window\nof its own, beside FBRX. It comes with Endpoint Pro and up, and on students' and children's computers (Endpoint\nEssentials is the AI on its own)."},{"d":"docs/BROWSER.md","t":"FBRX Browser › What it does","x":"| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Tabs** | New tab (Ctrl/⌘+T), close (Ctrl/⌘+W), a private tab (nothing kept once it closes). Ctrl/⌘+click or a middle click on a link opens it in a new tab behind this one. |\n| **Right-click** | On a link: *Open link in new tab* (behind this one), *in new private tab*, *in your default browser*, *Copy link address* and *Copy link text*. On a picture: open it in a new tab, save, copy it or its address. On selected text: *Copy* and *Search the web for* it. In a box you type in: spelling suggestions, undo, cut, copy, paste. Anywhere else: back, forward, reload and print. A private tab's links open in private tabs. |\n| **Address bar** | Type a web address, or a search. The search engine is yours to pick (Settings → Browser): DuckDuckGo (the default), Startpage, Google, Bing or Brave. A lock shows a secure (https) connection. |\n| **Fabrix** | The side panel (the ✶ button) asks the **local AI** about the page — *Summarize*, *Key points*, *Explain simply*, or your own question. It reads the page here on the computer; nothing is sent away. A private tab is never read. |\n| **Bookmarks and history** | The tag in the address bar keeps a page; the menu opens Bookmarks and History, each searchable, with a way to clear history. |\n| **Sign-ins** | The Vault is built in: click into a sign-in box and your sign-ins for that site show beside it, to fill in with a click; a sign-up form gets **Use a strong password**; after you sign in with something new, FBRX offers to save it (or update the password it has). The key in the address bar and the menu's *Saved sign-ins* fill in, copy or add one. See [VAULT.md](VAULT.md#fbrx-browser-fills-them-in). |\n| **Downloads** | Every download is checked by **FBRX Shield** when it finishes, as if it had landed in Downloads: malware goes to quarantine (when automatic quarantine is on) and Shield raises its alert; a suspicious file waits for your decision in FBRX Shield. With nothing found, you get one \"Download finished\". This needs FBRX Shield as the antivirus. |\n| **Keep a page** | The menu saves a page to **Notes**, copies its link, or opens it in the system browser. |\n| **Find and zoom** | Ctrl/⌘+F finds on the page; Ctrl/⌘+ +/−/0 zooms. |"},{"d":"docs/BROWSER.md","t":"FBRX Browser › Safe and separate","x":"- **Its own storage.** Web pages load in locked-down views with their own cookies and sign-ins\n  (`persist:fbrx-web`), kept apart from FBRX's own pages. A private tab uses a session that keeps nothing.\n- **Web permissions are off.** Pages cannot use the camera, the microphone, your location, notifications and the\n  like; only full screen and copying to the clipboard are allowed.\n- **Downloads are checked**, as above.\n- **Do Not Track.** FBRX Browser sends the Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control signals by default (Settings →\n  Browser turns this off)."},{"d":"docs/BROWSER.md","t":"FBRX Browser › For an organization (FBRX Command)","x":"An organization can decide which sites open in FBRX Browser, pushed as managed settings (the person cannot change\nthem):\n\n- **Allowed sites** (`browser.allowed`): when set, only addresses matching one of these may open — an allow-list.\n- **Blocked sites** (`browser.blocked`): addresses matching one of these are blocked.\n- **Safe browsing** (`browser.safeBrowsing`): searches on Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo and Brave always use strict\n  SafeSearch, and YouTube runs in Restricted Mode. It is on by itself on a student's or child's computer. It does not\n  judge other sites' content: for that, use the allow-list, or the blocked list for sites you know.\n\nThe lists apply to every page and frame the browser loads — a link, a redirect or an embedded frame included — not\nonly to what is typed. A blocked page shows a plain \"This page is blocked\" with the reason. The person can add their\nown blocks too (Settings → Browser), and remove only those.\n\nHost patterns match a site and its subdomains: `example.com` matches `www.example.com` and `shop.example.com`; `*`\nmatches everything."},{"d":"docs/BROWSER.md","t":"FBRX Browser › Settings","x":"Settings → Browser: the home page, the search engine, Do Not Track, whether new tabs open private, whether Fabrix may\nread pages, your own blocked sites, and clearing history. What an organization sets is shown there too, and cannot be\nchanged on the computer."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar","x":"**Studio → Calendar** is your **FBRX calendar**, kept on this computer, with shared calendars beside it as add-ons\n(Outlook / Microsoft 365, and calendar links from Google, Apple, a school or a team). See it as an agenda, a week or a\nmonth. FBRX reminds you before events, today's events run along the top of Dash, and Fabrix can tell you what is on,\nfind a free hour and (with your approval) add an event. It is in every edition, including student and child computers\n(a school timetable link, for example)."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › The FBRX calendar","x":"It is always there and needs no account: **New event** (or double-click an hour in Week, a day in Month) adds one.\n\n* **Repeats:** every day, every weekday, every week, every month (a month without that day, like the 31st, is\n  skipped) or every year, until a day or for good. Editing or deleting a repeating event changes all of it.\n* **About:** an event can be about a **project**, a **note**, a **task** or an **automation**. *Open project* (or note,\n  task, automation) in the event jumps to it.\n* **Color coding:** an event takes the color of what it is about, so a project's work reads at a glance:\n  1. a color you pick for the event itself;\n  2. else its **project's** color (the project it is about, or the project its note or task is in);\n  3. else the color of what it is: **automations** violet, **notes** amber, **tasks** teal, and what **Fabrix** added\n     in honey;\n  4. else the calendar's color.\n\n  **Colors** beside the calendar says what each color on screen stands for.\n* **What FBRX knows of your days** is on it too, each a calendar you can hide under *Calendars*: open **tasks** by their\n  due date, active **projects** by their deadline, and when your **automations** run (every run of a daily one; the\n  next run of one that repeats every few minutes or hours). These show as free time: they never block a meeting or\n  bring a reminder.\n* Events are kept on this computer (and in its backups); hide the whole calendar with its switch."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › Shared calendars (add-ons)","x":"Two kinds, under *Calendars → Add a shared calendar*:\n\n| | Outlook / Microsoft 365 | Calendar link |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What it is | Sign in with a work, school or personal Microsoft account in your browser | An iCalendar (`.ics` / `webcal://`) address from Outlook.com, Google, Apple iCloud, a school or a team site |\n| Shows | Every calendar in the account (turn each one on or off) | That one calendar |\n| Add and delete events from FBRX | Yes (unless the account is read-only) | No: links are read-only |\n| Online meeting links | From Outlook (*Join* buttons) | Found in the event's location, description or link |\n| Needs | A Microsoft app (client) ID: built in, set by your organization, or your own (below) | Nothing |\n\n**Where to find a calendar link**\n\n* **Outlook on the web / Outlook.com:** Settings → Calendar → Shared calendars → *Publish a calendar* → pick the\n  calendar and \"Can view all details\" → Publish → copy the **ICS** link.\n* **Google Calendar:** Settings → your calendar → Integrate calendar → **Secret address in iCal format**.\n* **Apple iCloud:** Calendar → the share button next to a calendar → **Public Calendar** → copy the link.\n\nA link is a credential (anyone with it can read the calendar), so FBRX keeps it in the vault, not in its settings."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › Using it","x":"* **Agenda** lists two weeks from today, grouped by day; **Week** is a time grid with all-day events on top,\n  overlapping meetings side by side, tentative ones striped and a line at the current time; **Month** shows six weeks\n  with each day's first events (your automations' runs last), and a day's number opens its week. Double-click an empty\n  hour in Week, or a day in Month, to add an event there.\n* Click an event for what it is about, how it repeats, where, who organized it, which calendar, how it shows (busy,\n  tentative, free, away), and **Edit** (FBRX events), **Open project** (or note, task, automation), **Join meeting**,\n  **Open in Outlook**, **Delete** (FBRX events, and Outlook events you can edit) and **Prepare with Fabrix**.\n* **Calendars** (beside the view): show or hide the FBRX calendar and each of its four (your events, tasks due,\n  project deadlines, automations), each shared account and each Outlook calendar; rename and recolor; update now;\n  remove a shared one. A calendar that cannot be updated says why; when Microsoft ended the sign-in (a new password,\n  access removed) it offers **Sign in again**.\n* **Calendar settings:** remind me (never, 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes before), update every 5–60 minutes, and the\n  Microsoft sign-in details.\n* **Dash** shows what is left today (with *Join* when a meeting is about to start), the split-flap board\n  announces the next one, and *Plan my day* fits your tasks around your meetings.\n\nFBRX keeps the last week and the next two months on the computer. Repeating events are expanded (Outlook does it for\nMicrosoft accounts; FBRX does it for links, with skipped and moved dates, in the feed's own time zones, including\nOutlook's Windows zone names)."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › The agent","x":"| Tool | Risk | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `calendar.agenda` | read | What is on, day by day, optionally filtered by a word |\n| `calendar.free_time` | read | Open stretches during working hours (events marked free and all-day events do not block time) |\n| `calendar.create_event` | write | Adds an event to the FBRX calendar (or an Outlook calendar the person names); it can repeat and be about a project, whose color it takes. Never invites anyone. Goes through approvals like any change |\n\nWhat Fabrix adds is marked as Fabrix's (honey, unless it is about a project). The tools are there unless every calendar\nis hidden. Sign-in tokens and links are never visible to the agent."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › Plugins","x":"A plugin that declares the `calendar` permission can read events with `ctx.calendar.events(from, to)` (at most 62\ndays at a time): title, start, end, all day, place, how it shows, cancelled and the calendar's name. No meeting links\nand no organizers. Each read is in the audit log. See [PLUGINS.md](PLUGINS.md)."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › Microsoft sign-in: the app (client) ID","x":"Microsoft only lets an app sign people in when it has an app registration. Pick one:\n\n1. **Built in.** Copies built with `FBRX_MS_CLIENT_ID` set (a repository variable or secret in the release workflow)\n   have it already. Nothing to do.\n2. **Your organization.** In FBRX Command → Profiles & groups, add to a profile's managed settings, and lock it if you\n   like (`calendar.microsoft`):\n\n   ```json\n   { \"calendar\": { \"microsoft\": { \"clientId\": \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\", \"tenant\": \"yourschool.org\" } } }\n   ```\n\n   `tenant` is `common` (work, school and personal accounts), `organizations`, `consumers`, or your tenant ID or\n   domain. `\"readOnly\": true` asks Microsoft for read access only.\n3. **Your own.** In the Microsoft Entra admin center (entra.microsoft.com) → Applications → App registrations → New\n   registration:\n   * Supported accounts: include *personal Microsoft accounts* if you use Outlook.com.\n   * Redirect URI: **Public client/native (mobile & desktop)**, `http://localhost`.\n   * API permissions → Microsoft Graph → Delegated: `Calendars.ReadWrite` (or `Calendars.Read`), `User.Read`,\n     `offline_access`. An organization can grant admin consent once for everyone.\n   * Paste the **Application (client) ID** into Calendar → Add calendar (or Calendar settings → Microsoft sign-in).\n\nNational clouds set `FBRX_MS_AUTHORITY` (for example `https://login.microsoftonline.us`) and `FBRX_MS_GRAPH`\n(`https://graph.microsoft.us/v1.0`)."},{"d":"docs/CALENDAR.md","t":"Calendar › How sign-in works (and what FBRX keeps)","x":"* FBRX is a public desktop client: your browser opens Microsoft's own sign-in page (authorization code with PKCE),\n  and Microsoft sends you back to a one-time listener on this computer (`http://localhost:`, loopback only,\n  closed as soon as it answers or after 10 minutes). FBRX never sees your password.\n* FBRX keeps only the refresh token, as an internal vault secret (`fbrx.calendar.`): never listed, never readable\n  by the agent, plugins, the Local API or remote commands. Access tokens stay in memory. Microsoft rotates the refresh\n  token and FBRX stores the new one.\n* While the vault is locked, calendars are not updated (they say so) and new ones cannot be added.\n* Calendars need internet access; a policy that blocks it stops them, with a message.\n* Adding, changing, removing, signing in, deleting events and reading events through the API are reserved for the\n  person at the computer; `calendar.status` (accounts and settings, no events) is open to the Local API.\n* Removing a calendar deletes its events from FBRX and forgets the token or link; nothing changes in the calendar\n  itself."},{"d":"docs/CLOUD_AI.md","t":"FBRX Cloud AI","x":"FBRX's models in the cloud, for FBRX accounts. A computer signed in to an FBRX account chats with them through the\naccount server (FBRX Command in the cloud): it checks the account, counts its tokens and passes\nthe chat on to the model provider whose key only it holds. It is a cloud FBRX Server for the AI alone: the agent's\ntools, its memory, approvals and files stay on the computer, as with any AI it thinks with."},{"d":"docs/CLOUD_AI.md","t":"FBRX Cloud AI › On a computer","x":"- **AI models → FBRX Cloud AI** (signed in to an FBRX account whose server offers it): this month's tokens, the extra\n  tokens, when the month renews, **Use for chats**, and a field for a promo code. **Settings → FBRX account** shows the\n  month in one line and takes promo codes too.\n- It is a cloud provider like any other: the governance policy's *allow cloud providers* decides whether it may be\n  used, and only the general part of the memory goes with a chat. It is off on learners' computers (students' and\n  children's, in an organization).\n- It is ready only while the account may chat; otherwise the AI models page says why (the month's tokens are used,\n  it was turned off), and so does a chat that runs into it."},{"d":"docs/CLOUD_AI.md","t":"FBRX Cloud AI › How it is counted","x":"- **Tokens** read and written, together, times the model's weight (a large model's count for more), by the month\n  (UTC).\n- **Each edition's allowance a month**, until the operator changes it: Essentials none, Pro 1M, Ultra 3M, Black\n  Hole 5M (`CLOUD_AI_ALLOWANCE`). The edition is the account's own or the fullest one an organization gives it.\n  Essentials' allowance, when there is one, waits for the account's email to be confirmed.\n- **Extra tokens** (from a promo code or the operator) are spent once the month's allowance is used, and do not run\n  out at the end of the month. With none left, the account waits for the next month.\n- **A stopped answer** counts what was read and written up to there (about four characters to a token, when the\n  provider did not get to say).\n- **Each chat**: answers of up to 8,192 tokens, four chats going at once per account, sixty a minute per sign-in.\n- **Kept**: per account and month, the tokens read, written and counted and the number of chats. Never what was said:\n  a chat passes through and is not stored or logged, and nothing about the person goes to the provider (only the\n  chat's messages, tools and settings)."},{"d":"docs/CLOUD_AI.md","t":"FBRX Cloud AI › The operator (FBRX Command → Cloud AI)","x":"The platform operator (superadmin) sees the month so far (everyone's tokens against the service's budget, the chats,\nthe models, the promo codes in use) and:\n\n- **Allowances**: each edition's tokens a month, and the **service budget**, the most FBRX Cloud AI counts in a month\n  for everyone together. Past it, it takes no new chats until the month ends: a ceiling no bug or abuse gets through.\n- **Accounts** (this month's, most used first; or any account, by search). **Manage** one: its own monthly limit\n  instead of its edition's, extra tokens to add (or take back), **lift its limit until the month ends** (it goes on,\n  still counted, without spending extra tokens), **turn FBRX Cloud AI off** for it, a note, and its last twelve months.\n- **Promo codes**: a code (or one FBRX makes up, FBRX-XXXX-XXXX) that gives extra tokens, Pro or Ultra for some\n  days (as a grant, like *Give an edition*), or both; for how many accounts and until when. Each account uses a code\n  once, in FBRX OS, on the website or in the phone apps. Turn one off or back on, and see who used it.\n\nEvery change is in the audit log."},{"d":"docs/CLOUD_AI.md","t":"FBRX Cloud AI › What a refused chat says","x":"| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `402 AI_LIMIT` | The month's allowance and the extra tokens are used (or the edition comes with none) |\n| `403 AI_OFF` | The operator turned FBRX Cloud AI off for the account |\n| `503 AI_BUSY` | The service reached its budget, or the provider is busy |\n| `503 AI_UNAVAILABLE` | This FBRX Command has no FBRX Cloud AI |\n| `429` | Four chats going already, or sixty in the last minute |\n| `400` | What the provider said was wrong with the chat (too long for the model, say) |\n| `502 AI_UPSTREAM` | The provider could not be reached or did not answer |"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center","x":"Everything a person uses every day lives in the desktop app next to the agent. The agent is called **Fabrix** by\ndefault (rename it in **Settings → Agent**); every feature below is also available to it as a governed tool.\n\nWindows is the primary platform for these features. Pages that depend on Windows (Security, Updates, Bug catcher,\nVirtual lab, printers, adapters) say so on other systems; the rest works everywhere."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel","x":"**Settings → Appearance** picks the look, in every edition: the themes and an accent color, with the sidebar icon, layout and behavior options below. Textures, and a color of your own for the sidebar icons, come with FBRX Endpoint Ultra:\n\n| Option | Choices |\n| --- | --- |\n| Look | A calm finish in the spirit of Windows 11 and macOS, with a little bounce: the system font (Segoe UI Variable on Windows 11), big page titles, rounded corners, hairline borders, soft shadows, capsule buttons that give a little when pressed, switches whose knob squishes while held, and a pill marker for the current page |\n| Theme | Tropical (FBRX's own and the default: honey running into red on calm, neutral pages; buttons, the current page, switches and the health ring wear the honey-to-red gradient; the FBRX logo stays white on black), Honey (FBRX's golden honey, with a honey glow and a woven texture), Neon Grid (deep night blue with cyan light lines, glowing cards and a grid floor that runs to the horizon), Ember, Midnight, Graphite, Ocean, Forest, Orchid, Paper, High contrast. Each has a light and a dark version; *Mode* follows Windows or is fixed. Every theme except High contrast tints the sidebar, top bar and page with its own gradient glow, and charts use gradient area fills |\n| Accent color | Any color; text on buttons switches between dark and light ink automatically |\n| Sidebar icons | **Theme color** (every section's icon in the theme's accent, for a calm one-color look: mango with Tropical, honey with Honey, blue with Graphite…; the default), **A color for each section**, **One color you pick** (Ultra) or **Plain**, and a **Tint behind the icons** slider from none (just the colored icon) to a rich colored tile |\n| Density | Compact, comfortable, spacious |\n| Corners | Sharp, rounded, soft |\n| Texture | None (the default), the theme's own (palms for Tropical, weave for Honey, a neon grid for Neon Grid, grain for Ember, dots for Midnight, carbon fiber for Graphite, waves for Ocean, linen for Forest…), or pick weave (a small basket weave), linen, grain, grid (glowing lines in the accent color), dots, carbon fiber, waves or palms. A **Strength** slider sets how much it shows, from 0 to 100% in steps of 5 (new installs start at a soft 50%; even 100% stays quieter than the old *bold*). Drawn as sharp SVG patterns in the theme's ink on the sidebar, the top bar and the page behind the cards (never behind text in cards) |\n| Text and interface size | 85–130 % |\n| Motion and start-up | Reduce motion; start-up animation (the FBRX logo stitched in) and start-up sound (from the FBRX intro, on by default, with a Listen button) |\n| Effects | Auto, Full or Lite. Lite turns off frosted glass, glows and decorative animation; Auto picks Lite on computers with four or fewer processor threads or 4 GB of memory |\n| Fun extras | Every edition, once unlocked (see [EASTER_EGGS.md](EASTER_EGGS.md), spoilers): easter eggs and jokes, the Silly Goose's occasional visits, a *Release the goose* button and the **Trophy case**. An organization can lock fun extras off |\n\n**Dark mode: a cool page, color on the elements.** In dark mode every theme keeps the page itself cool and quiet\n(slate neutrals, also in the warm themes: Tropical, Honey, Ember and Paper keep their accent and glow but not a\nbrown page) and puts the color on what you use: the sidebar's icons and the current page's marker (in the theme's\naccent, or with *A color for each section*: Bridge blue, Studio violet, Pit Stop amber, Workbench teal, Orbit sky,\nStronghold green, Lab pink, Control slate; in Essentials, Your AI blue, Help & settings slate),\ncards carry a breath of the theme's accent, and table heads, buttons, badges and tabs pick it up too. The page's glow\nis softer than the sidebar's. The sidebar colors show in light mode as well; High contrast stays flat.\n\nChart series and status colors stay on the validated design-system palette in every theme, so charts remain\nreadable for color-blind users. Organizations can lock any appearance setting from the admin console."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › The sidebar","x":"The sidebar is grouped into sections, each with an FBRX name, an icon and a line saying what is inside. Click a\nsection to open its pages (one at a time; the section of the page you are on opens by itself):\n\n| Section | Pages |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Glass** | Dash, Fabrix, Alerts |\n| **Studio** | Calendar, Tasks, Notes, Projects, Snippets |\n| **Pit Stop** | Storage, Security, Updates, Bug catcher, Network Center |\n| **Workbench** | Files, Task Manager, Clipboard, Copy & migrate, Toolbox, Library |\n| **Orbit** | Mesh & phone, FBRX VPN, AI models, AI coordination, Connections, Tools & plugins |\n| **Stronghold** | FBRX Shield (antivirus), Approvals, Credentials, Governance, Backup & restore |\n| **Lab** | Terminal, Virtual lab (Endpoint Ultra), FBRX Server (Black Hole Edition) |\n| **Control** | Organization, Settings |\n\n**Collapse** at the bottom folds the sidebar down to the section icons (remembered on this computer); click an icon\nand its pages slide out beside it. Counts (unread alerts, waiting approvals) show on the section too."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Version names","x":"FBRX 2.x is the Beta (1.x was the Alpha): versions read **FBRX Endpoint Ultra Beta 2.3.2** (or Essentials, Pro or\nBlack Hole Edition). FBRX is made by **Fox Huntress Labs**, as **Settings → Logs & about** says under the version. Versions also carry a codename from cloth, fiber to fabric, shown in **Settings → Logs & about**: 1.8\n*Spindle*, 1.9 *Bobbin*, 2.0 *Loom*, 2.1 *Warp*, and 2.2 *Weft*, which stays for the rest of 2.x. From now on a\ncodename changes only with a major version: 3.0 *Selvedge*, 4.0 *Tapestry*."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › FBRX Endpoint Essentials, Pro, Ultra and Black Hole Edition","x":"The desktop app comes in four editions. Every new computer opens in **Essentials**, free: your own AI and nothing\nelse, ready on the first screen (see *The first start* below). **Settings → Edition** switches between them (nothing\nsaved goes away), up to what the computer may run. Prices are in US dollars, per month (see LICENSING.md, *Prices*):\n\n| | **Essentials** | **Pro** | **Ultra** | **Black Hole Edition** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price | Free, and free in an organization | $4.99 after a free 7-day trial, or $3.99 through FBRX Command | $11.99, or $9.59 through FBRX Command | $24.99 per computer, only through FBRX Command |\n| How you get it | Where every new computer starts | Bought on your FBRX account or with a license key, or through your organization's FBRX Command | The same | Only as an add-on in an organization's FBRX Command: for the people who run it and its FBRX Servers |\n| Pages | The AI on its own: Fabrix (the chat, with its memory and voice), AI models, Governance, the help desk (in an organization) and Settings. **Home** is the split-flap board and a few notes from FBRX: tips, the AI models, and what Pro and Ultra add (no dashboard) | Everything in Essentials, plus Dash, alerts, messages, notes, tasks, calendar, projects, snippets, storage, FBRX Shield, security, updates, Network Center, backup, files, clipboard (with Ctrl+Alt+Z history), Toolbox, How-tos (the Library), approvals, credentials and macros, in the branded sidebar sections | Everything in Pro | Everything in Ultra, plus the FBRX Server page |\n| How much the AI does on a task | **Thinking**: Quick, Everyday or Thorough (see *Thinking* below), and whether it shows what it is doing | The same, each part on its own (steps per task, longest answer, how much the built-in and Ollama models keep in mind), and unloading the local model when idle | The same, and the temperature | The same |\n| IT and expert tool sets | — | — | Task Manager and Event Viewer, bug catcher, Copy & migrate, Terminal and code lab, virtual lab, FBRX/1, disks and partitions, Defender settings, Network Center's ping and trace, device scans, tools and adapters, device consoles, the developer tools in the Toolbox, logs, update channels, the Local API | Everything in Ultra, plus **network environments** (your network controller) in Network Center and the whole Library: **The Lab** and the **FBRX command library** (FBRX/1, FBRX Server and FBRX Gate commands, also in Terminal → FBRX/1), always there |\n| Connect | AI models | AI models | Mesh & phone, FBRX VPN, AI coordination, connections, tools and plugins | The same |\n| Governance | What the AI may do on its own and what it asks first, the folders it may use, and the audit log | The same | The same | The same, and a second AI model reviewing risky calls |\n| Look | The themes (light, dark or follow the computer) and an accent color | The same | The same, plus textures (with a strength slider) and a sidebar icon color of your own | The same |\n| Fun | Easter eggs, the Silly Goose and the trophy case, once unlocked with the secret key (see EASTER_EGGS.md, spoilers) | The same | The same | The same |\n\n**The walkthrough.** On a new computer, after setup, Essentials dims the window and lights up one page at a time:\nFabrix, AI models and Settings (memory, voice, and the edition for when you want more), ending with the Spotlight\nshortcut. Enter or → goes on, ← back, Esc skips; *Take the tour again* is in Settings → Edition.\n\n**Notifications in Essentials** are only the ones about the AI and the computer's safety: approvals, the AI,\nthe watchdog, messages from your organization and the help desk, and new FBRX versions. Pro and up show the\nrest (alerts, storage, FBRX Shield and so on) as set in **Settings → Notifications**.\n\n**What may be unlocked.** On its own, a computer runs Essentials for free. Pro and Ultra are bought: on the\nperson's FBRX account, or with a license key (Settings → License); a license that belongs to an organization (it\nnames its FBRX Command) counts only in that organization. Black Hole Edition comes only through FBRX Command. In an\norganization, the most a computer may run is what the license allows (the edition it goes up to: Pro, Ultra\nor Black Hole Edition), held lower by its group in FBRX Command (*Profiles & groups →\nEdition*) or by the computer itself (*Devices → a device → Configuration → Edition*): **Essentials only**, **Up to\nPro**, **Up to Ultra** or **Up to Black Hole Edition**. A page from a higher edition opens a short card that says what it\ndoes and how to get it: *Switch to Pro* in one click when it is allowed, *Turn on Ultra* (or Black Hole Edition) when it\nis allowed, and otherwise its price and *Join your organization*. Student computers run **FBRX OS Education** (see\nEDUCATION.md). The edition shows beside the name in the sidebar, at the top of **Settings**, in the window title,\nunder **Settings → Logs & about**, and in FBRX Command next to each device's version.\n\n**FBRX a"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › FBRX Endpoint Essentials, Pro, Ultra and Black Hole Edition (2)","x":"ccounts.** Settings → **FBRX account** signs in to an FBRX account (or makes one): the computer turns on what\nthe account bought, wherever it was bought, and a member of an organization can join the computer to it at the\nedition the organization gave them. Signed in, the locked editions in Settings → Edition have a **Get** button that\nopens the checkout in the browser. See ACCOUNTS.md. Where the account's server offers it, **AI models → FBRX Cloud AI**\nshows the month's tokens and **Use for chats** makes FBRX's cloud models the agent's; both there and in Settings →\nFBRX account, **Have a promo code?** takes a code from FBRX (extra tokens, an edition for some days). See CLOUD_AI.md.\n\n**Try Pro free for 7 days.** A computer on its own with nothing bought may try Pro free, once, for 7 days,\nno card needed: **Try free for 7 days** in Settings → Edition, on a page that comes with Pro, or in the note on\nEssentials Home. Settings → Edition says until when; then it runs Essentials unless Pro or Ultra is bought (and\nturns Pro back on by itself once it is). Until 2.0, Pro was free on a computer on its own: a computer set up\nbefore starts its trial by itself the first time it starts 2.0. Computers in an organization have no trial: FBRX\nCommand decides what they run. A computer set up before Essentials existed keeps the edition it had\n(up to what it may run now). Developers can try the lighter editions in a development build (which otherwise runs\nBlack Hole Edition) with `FBRX_EDITION=essentials`, `standard` or `ultra`."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › The first start: straight to your AI","x":"A new computer's first experience is a conversation, not a setup form:\n\n- **Install FBRX OS** (the from-source installer) asks nothing. While it builds the app it downloads the starter\n  model, **Qwen2.5 1.5B Instruct** (about 1 GB, Apache-2.0), into the app's models folder, and builds the AI runtime\n  (llama.cpp's `llama-server`) into the app. When FBRX OS opens, the model is picked up and the AI starts.\n- **The ready-made installers** (the release's Setup.exe and .dmg, or a shared installer) download the same model\n  when FBRX OS first opens; the chat shows the progress (\"Getting your AI ready\") and a *Try again* button if the\n  internet drops.\n- **One screen** then asks what Fabrix should call you (optional), **Simple** or **Advanced**, and the look, and opens\n  the chat with **Start chatting**. *Simple* keeps a short menu: Fabrix, AI models, Governance, help and Settings.\n  *Advanced* shows every page the edition has, in sections; on a computer on its own it starts the free 7-day Pro\n  trial, which is where the everyday tools are. **Settings → General → Menu** changes it later. The look (light, dark\n  or the computer's, and a theme) changes as you pick it. Moving from another computer (*Restore its backup*) and\n  *Join my organization* are links on the same screen. The rest waits until you want it: a vault recovery passphrase\n  (Credentials), another AI (AI models), the computer's name (Settings).\n\nThe starter is only ever set up by itself on a new computer where nobody chose an AI: picking another model or AI is\nnever overruled, a removed starter is not downloaded again by itself, and a computer set up before this existed is not\nsent a gigabyte unasked (*AI models* has it, one click). `Install FBRX OS --no-ai` leaves the model out entirely."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Your name","x":"FBRX asks what it should call you on the first screen (or once on Dash, for computers set up before it asked):\n\"Samuel\", called \"Sam\" or \"Sir\". Dash greets you by it and Fabrix addresses you that way. Change it in\n**Settings → General → You**."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Ask the agent about anything","x":"Pages carry **Analyze** buttons (the sparkle icon) that hand what you are looking at to the agent in a new chat:\nan alert or all of them, a process or the busiest ones, the drives and what is using space, partitions, a Defender\nthreat, a listening port, a startup item, a link or file check, a network device or the whole scan, a trace route,\nspeed results, app / Windows / driver updates, a file's contents, terminal output, the event log and FBRX's own logs,\nthe dashboard health check and your tasks for the day. Long content is trimmed to keep requests fast on local models."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Talk to Fabrix","x":"Press the **microphone** next to Send and speak; when you pause (or click it again) what you said is written into the\nbox and sent. The **speaker** button reads replies aloud as they stream in, the **headset** starts a hands-free\nconversation (listen, answer out loud, listen again, until a quiet moment), and every answer has **Read aloud**.\n\nSpeech becomes text on this computer with Whisper, so nothing you say leaves it. **Settings → Voice** picks the\nspeech model (downloaded once: *Fastest* 41 MB, *Recommended* 78 MB, *Most accurate* 249 MB, all English, or *Many\nlanguages* 78 MB), the microphone (with a test that shows what it heard) and whether to send right away. Fabrix's\nvoice is one of the **natural voices** or one of the computer's own:\n\n* **Natural voices** are lifelike voices made on this computer by the Kokoro model (one download of about 107 MB,\n  then they work offline): British voices George (an older gentleman: *the butler*), Fable, Lewis, Daniel, Emma,\n  Isabella, Alice and Lily, and American voices such as Heart, Bella, Michael and Fenrir. The presets **The butler**,\n  **The assistant** and **The storyteller** pick a voice and a speed in one click; the play button on each voice lets\n  you hear it. How quickly they are made depends on the processor; on a slow computer there can be a short pause\n  between sentences.\n* **Voices on this computer** come from Windows (Settings → Time & language → Speech; add *English (United Kingdom)*\n  for George and Hazel) or macOS (System Settings → Accessibility → Spoken Content → Manage Voices; the Premium voices\n  such as Jamie sound best). **Get more voices** opens that page; restart FBRX OS to see new ones.\n\nSet the speed in words per minute (120 to 360, 210 by default, a natural speaking pace) and, for the computer's own\nvoices, the pitch, and press **Hear it**.\n\nWhen you speak to Fabrix it says it heard you (\"Got it. Let me look into that.\" — a butler voice says \"Very good.\nAllow me a moment to look into it.\") and plays a soft thinking sound, a low hum with quiet data blips, until the\nanswer starts. Both can be turned off in Settings → Voice."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Watch Fabrix work","x":"While Fabrix works, the conversation shows what it is doing right now (asking the model, thinking, the tool it is\nusing and on what, waiting for your approval, writing the answer), the step it is on out of how many it may take,\nthe time so far and the tokens used, a list of the finished steps, and its thinking as it streams in, for models that\nshare it (recent Claude models, and local models with a reasoning mode such as DeepSeek R1, Qwen 3 or gpt-oss, through llama.cpp,\nOllama, vLLM or OpenRouter). The thinking stays folded under each answer as **How Fabrix thought about it**. It is\nfor you to read (it is not added to the conversation the model sees as text) and can be turned off in **Settings → Agent → Thinking**.\n\nFabrix keeps working when you go to another page. The chats it is working in say **Working** in the history,\n**Fabrix working** in the top bar opens the chat, and coming back to Fabrix opens the chat you left, with everything it\nhas done so far (FBRX keeps each task's progress until it finishes, so the chat picks up exactly where it is). A chat\nopened in its own window shows it too."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Your chat history","x":"The **⋯** next to a chat renames it, adds it to a project (or a new one), takes it out again, or deletes it. **Select**\npicks several chats to add to a project or delete together, and **Clean up** deletes chats you have not touched for\na week, a month, three months, a year, or all of them; it shows how many first, and keeps chats in projects unless\nyou include them. The list above the chats shows all of them, those in no project, or one project's; a new chat\nstarted while a project is shown goes into it, and **Projects → Chats** opens a project's chats. Deleting a project\nnever deletes its chats: they go back to the main history. Only the person at the computer can delete chats in bulk\n(not the Local API or FBRX Command)."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › What Fabrix remembers","x":"Fabrix keeps a memory of you **on this computer only**, so it knows you whichever AI model you pick (a local one, a\ncloud one, or a different one tomorrow). **Settings → Memory** shows all of it.\n\n* **Short-term memory** is the last 24 hours of chats: what you asked and what it answered. It ages out by itself\n  after a day and goes only to a local AI.\n* **Long-term memory** is lasting facts about you (your name, where you work, the town you live in, what you prefer)\n  and your past chats, which it can search when you ask \"what did we say about the printer last month?\". It learns\n  facts from what you say (\"I live in…\", \"my email is…\"), keeps what you or Fabrix choose to remember, and a newer fact\n  replaces an older one with the same meaning. A fact is found by what it is about, not only by its wording: word\n  forms (\"print\" finds printer and printing) and related words (\"my Synology\" finds what you said about the NAS).\n* **How you like things done** is learned from instructions: \"Always answer in bullet points\", \"From now on, use\n  metric\", \"Never use emojis\", \"I like it when you keep it short\". These go with every request, first.\n* **Earlier work** that relates to what you ask (chats of the last 30 days sharing its words or related ones, the\n  closest three) goes along too, so Fabrix picks up where you left off (\"the VPN keeps dropping\" brings back the chat\n  where you set it up). Like short-term memory, it goes only to a local AI.\n\nYou can add a fact, change or forget any of them (right-click one), **Forget everything**, and turn each part off.\nStudent and child computers keep no memory at all.\n\nMemory is not the model's own working memory: that is its **context window** (*What a local model keeps in mind*,\nunder Thinking), the conversation and instructions it reads before each answer, much as a computer's RAM. FBRX's memory\nis a notebook it keeps for the model: before each answer, the facts that fit the question go with your message, inside\nthat window. It helps the model know you; it does not make it reason better. Nor is it prompt caching, which is the AI\nservice reusing what it already read when a request starts the same way as the last one, so it answers sooner.\n\n**Never kept**: Social Security and other national ID numbers, driver's license and passport numbers, tax IDs, bank\naccount and routing numbers, full payment card numbers and their security codes, and passwords and PINs. FBRX finds\nthem by their shape and the words around them and takes them out before anything is saved, whoever asked.\n\n**Private details** (addresses, the town you live in, your email and phone number, your birthday, and payment cards\nby their **last four digits only**, off by default) are kept only when you allow each kind. They go to a local AI\nonly: a cloud AI gets the general facts with your message (or nothing, if you choose *Nothing*), never private details\nand never the last 24 hours.\n\n**Nothing personal goes onto the internet.** If Fabrix is about to put an identity or money number into a web search\nor any other online tool (\"why does my SSN start with 818?\" as a search), FBRX refuses the call before anything is\nsent. A private detail it remembers (your address, your phone number) in an online tool needs your approval first."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Fabrix knows FBRX","x":"Fabrix has a database of FBRX itself on the computer (`knowledge.db`): this guide and the other FBRX documents,\ntroubleshooting playbooks and the FBRX/1 commands. It is rebuilt when FBRX updates. Ask \"why is FBRX slow?\" or \"why\ndoes the AI keep stopping?\" and Fabrix runs a **check-up** (services, the local model and its memory use, the\ndatabase, scans in progress, disk space, FBRX Shield) and explains what it found. Where there is a fix it offers it,\nand it asks you before applying one:\n\n| Fix | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| Restart a service | Restarts one FBRX service that has stopped or keeps failing |\n| Stop the scan | Stops a FBRX Shield scan that is slowing the computer |\n| Check the list again | Re-checks FBRX Shield's findings (clears ones that no longer apply) |\n| Unload the model | Gives the local model's memory back to the computer |\n| Smaller conversation memory | Lowers the local model's context size when it does not fit |\n| Tidy the database | Compacts FBRX's database |"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Look and feel › Folders your AI can use","x":"**Governance → Folders your AI can use** lists the only folders Fabrix may read or work in (your home folder and the\nFBRX workspace to begin with). **Add a folder…** picks any other one with the folder picker, such as another drive or a\nnetwork share; the trash button takes one away, and **Read only** lets it look at files but never change them. System\nfolders (Windows, Program Files, `/System`, `/etc`…) and FBRX's own data folder can never be added.\n\nWhen Fabrix needs a folder it does not have, it asks: a window shows which folder and why, and you **Allow this\nfolder**, **Choose another folder…** or say **Don't allow**. Nothing is opened until you answer. When FBRX Command\nmanages the computer, the folders come from the organization's policy and this card is read-only. The policy as JSON\nis edited in FBRX Command (Governance), not on the computer."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday","x":"| Page | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Dash** | The dashboard, in frosted glass: a split-flap board that rotates through status messages, a health score out of 100 with what costs points, what is left on today's calendar (with *Join* when a meeting is about to start), insight tiles (your next task, internet speed, storage, Fabrix, backups and the vault, alerts and approvals, a tip), then live processor, memory, network and battery (sampled every 2 s), today's tasks, drives, recent alerts, this computer's hardware, and service health |\n| **Fabrix** | The agent. Six starter cards (check my PC, plan my day, free up space, slow internet, is this link safe, explain crashes). Each chat is **Offline** or **Online**: new chats start offline (Settings → Agent); the first internet tool in an offline chat asks you to put that chat online. Attach files and pictures, and ask for documents and pictures: see [FABRIX.md](FABRIX.md) |\n| **FBRX Shield** | The antivirus: what protects this computer (FBRX Shield, Microsoft Defender or an antivirus already installed, such as Sophos), its status, scans with live progress, what was found, quarantine, and FBRX Shield's settings. See [SHIELD.md](SHIELD.md) |\n| **Calendar** | The FBRX calendar on this computer: events you add (repeating, about a project, note, task or automation, in its color), with your tasks' and projects' due dates and your automations' runs on it; shared calendars as add-ons (Outlook / Microsoft 365, calendar links from Outlook.com, Google, Apple, a school). As an agenda, a week or a month, with a color legend, *Join* for online meetings, reminders before events, and *Prepare my day*. See [CALENDAR.md](CALENDAR.md) |\n| **Tasks** | Board (drag between To do / In progress / Done) and list views, priorities, due dates, projects, quick add |\n| **Notes** | Markdown notes with preview, tags, pinning, projects and autosave |\n| **Projects** | Progress, overdue tasks, milestones, linked notes, snippets and chats |\n| **Snippets** | Reusable commands and text; press Enter in Spotlight to copy one |\n| **Automations** | Jobs Fabrix does by itself on a schedule, when FBRX starts or with a shortcut (watch listings, check stocks, a price drop, a morning brief), telling you every time or only when something changed. See [FABRIX.md](FABRIX.md#automations) |\n| **Snapshot** | A picture of the screen (an area, a window or all of it; Ctrl+Alt+S anywhere), marked up with pen, highlighter, arrows, boxes, text, blur and crop, then copied, saved or handed to Fabrix. See [FABRIX.md](FABRIX.md#fbrx-snapshot) |\n| **Files** | Places and drives, folder search, previews (text and images), a plain-text editor |\n| **Task Manager** | Three tabs. **Processes**: live list by CPU or memory with search, details and End task (core Windows processes are protected); *What is this?* on any process, and *What's using my PC?* / *Anything suspicious?* in the Fabrix panel beside it. **Performance**: live processor, memory and network graphs, every core, uptime, battery and temperature. **Event Viewer**: see below |\n| **Clipboard** | A copy-and-paste processor. Paste text (or type `/` for snippets), add steps — trim, remove blank or duplicate lines, sort, straighten quotes, strip HTML or terminal colors, change case (UPPER, Title, camelCase, snake_case…), find and replace (plain or regular expression), keep or drop matching lines, extract emails, web addresses, IP or MAC addresses and numbers, join or split, wrap each line, number lines, JSON format/minify, CSV or Excel cells → Markdown table, Base64 and URL encode/decode — and the result updates as you go. **Save as transform** keeps the steps; ▶ next to a saved transform runs it straight on the clipboard (copy, click, paste). Optional **History** keeps the last 50 things you copied while FBRX runs (see Security), and **Ctrl+Alt+Z** opens it anywhere (see Macros below) |\n| **Copy & migrate** | Copy, back up, mirror or move folders between drives, computers and network shares with **Robocopy** (Windows), **rsync** (Mac and Linux) or FBRX's own copier. Pick the folders, the tool and what to do (copy; new and changed only; mirror; move), skip clutter, retries, files at once, files and folders to leave out; the exact command is shown before it runs. **Preview** lists what would happen without changing anything; mirror and move ask first. Live output, counts and a summary, with *Explain this result*. Recipes: back up Documents, mirror to a NAS, copy a USB stick, move a project, and **Move my user folders to a new PC** (Desktop, Documents, Pictures, Music, Videos and Downloads, one after another). Guard rails refuse mirroring onto a drive root or a system or home folder, copying a folder into itself, and moving a whole drive or home folder away |\n| **Terminal** (Ultra) | **What if?** next to Run has Fabrix explain what the command in the box would do — what it reads, changes, deletes or downloads, whether it needs admin rights, and a verdict — without running i"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday (2)","x":"t. Runs commands with streaming output in **PowerShell 7** when it is installed, otherwise **Windows PowerShell 5.1**, or Command Prompt (picker in the header). A **reference panel** puts ready-made commands (network, system, disks, processes, repair, security, power, printing; admin-only ones marked), your snippets, the code in your notes, and each project's snippets and notes one click away; ▶ runs one (commands that change something ask first). Save any command you ran as a snippet. **Ctrl+C** stops the command that is running (the newest, if several are), as in any terminal; with text selected it copies, and with nothing running it clears the line. Start a line with `?` to ask Fabrix for a command. Every command is in the audit log. The **Code** tab is the code lab (below), the **Device consoles** tab holds SSH and Telnet sessions (see Network Center), and **FBRX/1** is the management console for FBRX itself (below) |\n| **Toolbox** | Passwords, QR codes (guest Wi-Fi codes phones join by scanning, links, text), text tools, compare text, sizes and numbers (why a 1 TB drive shows 931 GB; decimal, hex, binary), decision maker, colors. Ultra: JSON, Base64, URL, hashes, UUIDs, timestamps, regex, JWT, subnet calculator, MAC vendor lookup, port reference (with risky ports flagged) and the command library. Every tool has *Try* examples, and the top row recommends common jobs — all offline |\n| **Library** | 61 short how-tos (Windows basics, files, Wi-Fi, security, speed, devices, troubleshooting) with buttons that open the right Windows setting or ask Fabrix. In Endpoint Black Hole Edition, **The Lab** adds power-user how-tos (install USBs, dual boot, a malware lab, device consoles, blue screen dumps, repairs, verifying downloads) and official download pages for Windows, Linux, security distributions and power tools, plus your computer maker's driver page. Safety goggles recommended (sold separately). **FBRX commands** (Black Hole Edition, always there) is the library of FBRX's own command lines: FBRX/1 on this computer (*Try it* opens FBRX/1 with the command typed in), FBRX Server and FBRX Gate (copy, then run in a device console), and how-tos such as copying settings to another computer or changing a gate without locking yourself out. Talking to the search like a switch (`show commands`, `enable`, `configure terminal`) opens it, and with fun extras on earns *Operator Mode* in any edition |"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Snippets and the / menu","x":"A **snippet** is text you paste often. Type **/** in the Fabrix chat, the Terminal, FBRX/1, the Clipboard editor or any\nFabrix side panel and your quick snippets pop up; ↑ ↓ and Enter paste one. **/snip** lists the snippet library from\n**Studio → Snippets** (keep typing to search: `/snip dns`), **/clip** pastes the clipboard, **/date**, **/time** and\n**/now** insert the date and time, **/snippets** opens the editor and **/macros** the keyboard shortcuts. Program your\nown in **Settings → Snippets**: the word you type, a description, where it works (everywhere, chat only, or Terminal\nand FBRX/1 only) and the text it pastes, with placeholders `{date}`, `{time}`, `{datetime}`, `{isodate}`, `{name}`,\n`{callme}`, `{host}`, `{clipboard}` and `{cursor}` (where the cursor lands). Three come built in: `/sig` (sign-off with\nyour name), `/stamp` (date and time) and `/flushdns` (terminal). The same page lists your clipboard transforms."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Macros: keyboard shortcuts","x":"A **macro** is a quick key that works anywhere, even while FBRX is in the background. **Settings → Macros** lists them;\nclick one and press new keys to change it, or turn it off:\n\n| Macro | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Clipboard history | **Ctrl+Alt+Z** | Opens your recent copies near the pointer, like Windows' Win+V |\n| Spotlight | **Alt+Space** | Search apps, files, settings and quick answers (Ctrl+K inside FBRX) |\n| Presenter-safe mode | **Ctrl+Alt+P** | On and off before you share your screen |\n\n**Your macros** (Settings → Macros) are shortcuts you make: press one in any app and FBRX types its command or text\nwhere you are (it puts the text on the clipboard, presses paste once you let go of the keys, and puts back what was on\nthe clipboard; on a Mac it stays on the clipboard for ⌘V). The quickest way to make one is **Add to macros** on a\ncommand: the ⚡ button or the right-click menu on commands in Library → FBRX commands, the Terminal's reference panel\nand the FBRX/1 library, or on any text you pick. The dialog takes a name, the text (placeholders as in snippets), the\nkeys, and can also offer it as a `/name` snippet in the chat, the Terminal and FBRX/1. A shortcut already used by\nanother macro is refused."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › The right-click menu","x":"Right-click works everywhere in FBRX. In a text box: Undo, Cut, Copy, Paste and Select all. On text you picked: Copy,\nAsk Fabrix about it, and Add to macros. On a command: Copy, Add to macros, Put it in the Terminal (Ultra), and on the\ncommand libraries Run, Type it into an open server console, and Ask Fabrix to explain it. On a page in the sidebar:\nOpen, and Open in a new window. Anywhere else, **What's this?** asks Fabrix what that part of FBRX is for, in a few\nplain words (handy in Endpoint Essentials), and **Open this page in a new window** puts the page beside FBRX. Shift and\nright-click skips the menu."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Pages in their own window","x":"**Pop out** (Terminal) and the ⧉ button by Conversations (the chat) open the page in a window of its own, beside the\nmain window, so you can work side by side; right-click any page in the sidebar for the same. The window has **Side by\nside** (FBRX on the left, this window on the right), **Keep on top**, and a way back to the main window. Links to other\npages open in the main window. In **Settings → General → Windows** the Terminal (Ultra) and the chat can always open in\ntheir own window.\n\n**The clipboard history** shows what you copied while FBRX was running, text and pictures (with a preview), newest\nfirst. Text shows exactly as copied: a password ending in `|>` stays `|>`, never an arrow.\n\n| Key | Does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **↑ / ↓** | Move up and down the list |\n| **Tab** / **~** | Slide right and left through ways to paste the chosen text: *As copied*, *Clean*, *One line*, *Comma list*, *UPPERCASE*, *lowercase*, *Title Case*, *Quoted string*, then your saved clipboard transforms (formats that would not change it are skipped; a preview shows the result) |\n| Type anything | Search your copies, from the first letter (*picture* finds the pictures) |\n| **Enter** | Paste it into the app you were in (a picture pastes as a picture) (Windows; on a Mac it goes on the clipboard and you press ⌘V). Double-click works too |\n| **Delete** · **Ctrl+P** | Remove it · pin it (pinned copies stay when you clear the history) |\n| **Esc** | Clear the search, or close |\n\nThe history is off until you turn it on (the first Ctrl+Alt+Z offers it, or **Settings → Macros**), lives in memory\nonly and skips copies that password managers mark as secret. *Paste right away* can be turned off so a pick only goes\non the clipboard."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Code lab","x":"**Terminal → Code** is a small IDE with Fabrix beside you: a real code editor (syntax colors, line numbers, brackets,\nfolding, search, undo) for PowerShell, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, C#, Java, Go, Rust, C, C++, Ruby, Bash, Lua,\nPHP, Kotlin, Swift and batch files. **Quick start** opens Rock, Paper, Scissors in every one of those languages, all\nplaying the same game the same way so they are easy to compare.\n\n* **Fabrix panel**: *Explain*, *Fix* (finds bugs and gives a corrected file), *Comment*, *Convert to…* another language,\n  *What if?*, or ask anything (select lines first to ask about just those). Code in an answer can be inserted at the\n  cursor, replace the file (Ctrl+Z undoes it) or open as a new file in its language.\n* **What if?** summarizes what the file would do before you run it anywhere: step by step, what it touches (files,\n  network, settings, admin rights) and a verdict: *Safe to run*, *Changes things* or *Risky*.\n* **Run** (JavaScript): runs right in FBRX, completely sandboxed; answers for `prompt()` come from the Input box.\n  **Run in Sandbox** (PowerShell and batch, Windows Pro/Enterprise): opens the file inside Windows Sandbox with no\n  network and the code folder read-only. Other languages are never run by FBRX.\n* **Run it yourself** shows, for the language in the editor, where to get the tools and the exact commands\n  (`python rps.py`, `go run rps.go`, `java Rps.java`…), plus where the files are. **VS Code**, **PowerShell ISE** and\n  **Editor** open the file in those apps (shown when installed). Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Enter runs."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Event Viewer","x":"**Task Manager → Event Viewer** reads the Windows event logs (Application, System, Setup, Security with admin\nrights, Microsoft Defender, PowerShell, Task Scheduler, Wi-Fi), the systemd journal on Linux, or the unified log on a\nMac (errors and faults, last six hours). Filter by level (counts on each), time range (an hour to 30 days), source\nand event ID, then search within the results. Click an event for the full message and details, *Explain this\nevent*, copy it or search the web. The Fabrix panel's *Analyze these events* groups repeats, separates what matters\nfrom harmless noise and suggests what to fix first. **Bug catcher → Show logs → All logs** opens it too."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › FBRX/1","x":"**Terminal → FBRX/1** manages FBRX OS from a command line in the style of a network operating system:\n`show` reads, `request` acts, and `configure` edits settings as a candidate that only takes effect on `commit`.\n\n```\nroot@PC> show system status\nroot@PC> show services | except running\nroot@PC> show log 200 | match error\nroot@PC> request ai stop\nroot@PC> configure\n[edit]\nroot@PC# set appearance preset neon\nroot@PC# show | compare\n+ set appearance preset neon\nroot@PC# commit check\nconfiguration check succeeds\nroot@PC# commit\ncommit complete (1 change)\nroot@PC# exit\n```\n\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `show version`, `show license` | Product, version, build and license |\n| `show system status`, `show system resources` | Services, vault, fleet, agent and emergency stop; processor, memory and disks |\n| `show services`, `show alerts`, `show approvals`, `show tasks`, `show consoles`, `show trophies` | What they say |\n| `show ai providers`, `show ai models ` | AI providers and their models |\n| `show network interfaces`, `show network devices`, `show network vendors` | Adapters, the last scan, the MAC registry in use |\n| `show audit `, `show log ` | The audit log (newest first) and FBRX's log |\n| `show configuration [section]` | Settings in use, as a tree or (`| display set`) as `set` commands |\n| `request service restart `, `request backup now`, `request network scan`, `request alerts mark-read` | Actions |\n| `request ai stop`, `request ai resume` | The emergency stop |\n| `ping  [count n]` | Ping |\n| `configure` → `set`, `delete`, `show [| compare]`, `commit [check]`, `rollback`, `run `, `exit [discard]` | Edit settings safely: `commit check` validates against the schema and your organization's locks first |\n\nUnique prefixes work (`sh sys st`), **Tab** completes, **?** lists what can come next, ↑ / ↓ walk the history, and\noutput can be piped: `| match `, `| except `, `| count`, `| last `, `| display json`,\n`| display set`. The **FBRX/1 library** next to the console has pre-configured commands and multi-line recipes\n(health check, switch theme, offline-first agent, try-before-you-commit); ▶ runs every line in order, and the\nbookmark on any command you ran saves it as an FBRX/1 snippet that appears under *My snippets*. Once the FBRX\ncommands are found in the Library (*Operator Mode* with fun extras on; straight away with them off), the library also\nhas **FBRX Server** and **FBRX Gate**: click one to copy it, or type it into an open server console (Enter stays yours).\nEvery FBRX/1\ncommand, with what it does, is also in **Library → FBRX commands**, next to the FBRX Server and FBRX Gate ones. FBRX/1 is reserved\nfor the person at the computer: it is not available over the Local API, the mesh or remote commands, and every\nchange goes through the same checks and audit log as the rest of FBRX."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Emergency stop","x":"The red **Emergency stop** button (Settings → Agent, the slim pill at the bottom of the conversation list on the agent\npage, the tray menu, or `request ai stop` in FBRX/1) halts every AI action at once: running chats are canceled, tools waiting for\napproval are denied, the local model is stopped, and the agent and AI apps connected over the Local API are refused\nuntil someone resumes. A red **AI on emergency stop** pill with **Resume** stays in the top bar meanwhile. Only the\nperson at the computer can resume (Resume, the tray menu or `request ai resume`); both are in the audit log. You can\nstill use tools directly while the AI is stopped."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Everyday › Spotlight","x":"Press **Alt+Space** anywhere (change it in Settings → Spotlight), or **Ctrl+K** inside FBRX OS. One box finds\nStart menu apps, files (Windows Search index), FBRX pages, notes, tasks, snippets, Windows settings and tools, and\nsystem commands (lock, sleep, restart, shut down, sign out, empty Recycle Bin, flush DNS — the disruptive ones ask\nfor a second Enter). It calculates (`(1250*12)/7`), converts units (`5 km to mi`, `100 f to c`, `1 gib in mb`),\nopens web addresses and searches the web. **Tab** gives a quick answer from Fabrix right in the box.\n\nSpotlight only launches what it found itself: Start menu app ids it listed, a fixed set of Windows tools, and\n`https://`, `ms-settings:` and `windowsdefender:` links."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Alerts","x":"Background rules watch performance (CPU, memory, temperature), storage, battery, the internet connection, new\ndevices on your network, Microsoft Defender (real-time protection, threats, definitions age), the firewall, app\ncrashes and blue screens, printers, due tasks, paired devices and pending approvals. Each rule can be switched off\nand its threshold changed (**Alerts → Rules**).\n\n**Alerts → Delivery** decides where each severity goes:\n\n| Channel | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Inbox | Always |\n| Desktop | Windows notifications |\n| Phone | Paired phones running FBRX Mobile |\n| Organization | The control plane's **Alerts & events** page (enrolled devices) |\n| Webhook | Slack, Microsoft Teams, Discord, ntfy or plain JSON |\n| E-mail | Your SMTP server; the password lives in Credentials (`FBRX_SMTP_PASSWORD`) |\n\nQuiet hours hold back desktop and e-mail alerts that are not critical; a cooldown stops the same alert repeating."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Alerts › Notifications","x":"**Alerts → Notifications** (1.9.8+) covers everything that pops up, not only alerts:\n\n* **Show notifications**, on or off, and a switch for each kind: alerts, approvals, messages, FBRX Mesh, help desk,\n  your organization, calendar reminders, backups, notes from your AI, macros, FBRX itself and plugins. The alerts and\n  messages switches are the same ones as *Delivery → Desktop* and *Settings → Messages*.\n* **Where they show.** Each notification shows in **one place**:\n  * *Automatic* (the default): inside FBRX while you are using it, otherwise in Windows notifications (Notification\n    Center on a Mac).\n  * *Only in FBRX*: nothing pops up over your other apps.\n  * *Only in Windows notifications*.\n  * *Both*: how it worked before 1.9.8, when every notification showed twice.\n\n  Clicking a Windows notification opens FBRX on the page it is about.\n* **Sound:**\n  * *FBRX sound* (the default): FBRX's own chime, made in the app with no sound file. Its two rising notes are\n    FBRX's; a warning falls, a problem knocks twice, and good news finishes the chord. It has its own volume and a\n    *Hear it* row, and the Windows notification stays silent so you hear one sound.\n  * *Windows sound*.\n  * *No sound*.\n\n  Macros never make a sound.\n* **Send a test notification** waits four seconds, so you can switch to another app and see where it lands.\n\nPresenter-safe mode still holds notifications back while you present."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › PC care (Windows)","x":"| Page | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Storage** | Drives with health and BitLocker state; clean temporary files; empty the Recycle Bin. **Space map**: a treemap of any folder or the whole drive, colored by kind of file (videos, pictures, music, documents, archives, apps and models, code); click a folder to go into it. Under it: the space hogs inside (node_modules, Python environments, Xcode, Gradle and Rust build folders, a Photos library, Steam games, the Trash) with **Delete** for build folders, the largest files with **Show** and **Move to Trash**, and how much has not changed for two years. **Space hogs on this computer** checks the usual places (npm, Yarn, pip, Gradle and Homebrew caches, browser caches, Xcode, Docker, Ollama, LM Studio and Hugging Face models, Steam, phone backups, temporary files) and clears caches in one click. Ultra: physical disks with wear and temperature, partitions, optimize / check / rename / extend; deeper scans of whole drives, **duplicate files** (same contents, 1 MB and up) with **Keep one**, and what grew or shrank since you last mapped a folder |\n| **Security** | Microsoft Defender status, quick/full/folder scans, definitions update, threat history and removal; firewall profiles; listening ports (exposed vs local); what starts with Windows and unsigned programs running from user folders; file check (SHA-256, signature, VirusTotal with your key); **link check** (look-alike domains, redirects without running page code, domain age, certificate, VirusTotal); Windows Sandbox for unknown links and files. Ultra: Defender settings and exclusions |\n| **Updates** | App updates through winget (one or all, with live output), pending Windows updates, third-party drivers oldest first, installed updates |\n| **Bug catcher** | Errors from the event log grouped by source, app crashes, blue screens, unexpected shutdowns, problem devices and stopped services — with one-click repairs (SFC, DISM, network reset, Windows Update reset, Explorer restart, icon cache, print queue, Store cache, clock resync, battery and energy reports) and \"explain with Fabrix\" |\n| **Backup & restore → Whole computer** | **FBRX Time** (1.9.8+, every edition): an image of the whole computer, by hand or every day or week at a time you choose, to an external drive, a network folder, or an FBRX Server (paste its connection code). Set up once with one UAC prompt; after that *Back up now* and FBRX Command's *Image backup now* start it without asking, and a computer that was off at the time catches up when it is next on. Shows the last image, the next one and how many the target holds; right-click it for the same actions. Images go back with Windows' *System Image Recovery*; on an FBRX Server each one is also checked by starting it ([TIME.md](TIME.md)) |\n| **Virtual lab** (advanced) | **Room for a VM**: live processor, memory and disk gauges and which VM sizes fit right now (keeping 2 GB and a core for Windows). **My VMs**: cards with state, resources, network (isolate / internet / unplug), console, shut down, save, power off, named checkpoints, revert and delete (optionally keeping the disk). **New safe VM**: templates for Kali Linux, REMnux, FLARE-VM, Parrot Security, Ubuntu, a Windows 11 evaluation machine or your own disc, each with its official download page, a fit check and a setup guide from Fabrix; Generation 2, secure boot, a TPM for Windows guests and a first checkpoint. **Windows Sandbox**: launch it with a link or a read-only shared folder, networking on or off. **Readiness**: edition, firmware virtualization, memory, processors and account, plus turning on Hyper-V, Windows Sandbox, the Virtual Machine Platform and WSL. *Plan a lab* asks Fabrix to design one for this PC |\n| **FBRX Server** (Black Hole Edition) | **Your servers**: FBRX Servers and any other server or device with a web console, saved by address and port, opened inside FBRX in a browser of its own (separate sign-ins and cookies, no access to FBRX). An FBRX Server opens as a **remote desktop**: its console with a command line (SSH) under it, full screen or in a window of its own, through FBRX VPN when you are away. A server's own certificate is trusted once, after comparing its fingerprint with the server's, and only that certificate is accepted at that address from then on. FBRX Servers paired on FBRX Mesh are offered. **Set up a server**: the installer ISO, the bundle for a Debian 13 server you already have, the firmware settings to check first, and the guides (see [SERVER.md](SERVER.md)) |\n\nActions that need administrator rights show the Windows UAC prompt. FBRX passes the script to the elevated\nPowerShell as an encoded command — it is never written to a file that another program could swap before you\napprove. Read-only information never asks for elevation."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Network Center","x":"Overview (this PC, router, internet latency and loss, public address, plain-language verdict), ping and\n**trace route with hop identification** (your router, other local routers / double NAT, your provider, the\ninternet, the destination; names, makers and locations), **device discovery** (ping sweep, ARP, mDNS/Bonjour,\nUPnP, port fingerprints and MAC vendors identify PCs, phones, printers, TVs, cameras, smart-home devices and NAS),\nsaved scans with comparison and CSV export, a **speedometer** speed test (download, upload, latency and jitter, with history), Wi-Fi (signal, channel, band) with\nthe **channel planner**: every network around drawn over the channels it covers (yours in the accent color), how busy\neach channel is (closer and louder neighbors count for more; at 2.4 GHz, overlapping channels count too), the best\nchannel for your network with the reason (at 2.4 GHz only 1, 6 or 11), and **Open router settings** to change it\n(Ultra: the channel width to set, DFS channels with their catch, how busy each channel has been since FBRX started, and\nevery access point with its signal), Bluetooth devices with battery, printers (test page, queue, default, clear, restart spooler), DNS\ncomparison, port checks and device consoles (Ultra). Ultra: adapter configuration (DHCP, static, secondary addresses).\n\nMakers come from the **IEEE MAC address registry** that ships with FBRX OS (MA-L, MA-M and MA-S blocks, about 54,000\nentries, the most specific block wins; randomized phone and laptop addresses are recognized as such). *Update from\nthe IEEE* in Toolbox → MAC vendor lookup downloads the current lists (falling back to the daily published JSON copy\nwhen the IEEE site refuses the download)."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Network Center › Network environments (Black Hole Edition): your UniFi console","x":"**Network Center → Environments** attaches the UniFi console that runs a school's or office's network (Dream Machine,\nCloud Gateway, Cloud Key or UniFi OS Server with UniFi Network 9 or newer):\n\n1. In UniFi Network, **Settings → Control Plane → Integrations → Create API key**. Copy it; UniFi shows it once.\n2. In FBRX, **Attach a UniFi console**: the console's local address (for example `https://192.168.1.1`) and the key.\n3. **Test connection**. UniFi consoles make their own certificate, so FBRX shows its SHA-256 fingerprint; compare it\n   with your browser's certificate details for the same address and **Trust it**. From then on FBRX checks the\n   fingerprint before sending anything, and refuses the connection if the certificate changes.\n\nYou then see every gateway, switch and access point (state, address, model, firmware and pending updates, clients per\ndevice), every client (name, address, MAC, Wi-Fi or wired, which access point or switch it is on, since when), and per\ndevice its uptime, CPU, memory and uplink traffic. **Restart** a device, and make **Guest Wi-Fi codes** (vouchers for a\nhotspot guest network) for visitors, substitutes or parent night. The key is kept in the vault; the agent can read the\nnetwork (*which access points are offline? where is the Room 12 TV connected?*), and asks before it restarts anything\nor makes codes. Essentials and Pro do not show environments."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Network Center › Device consoles (advanced)","x":"A device with SSH (22, or 4118 for WatchGuard) or Telnet (23) open gets a **Connect** button. The console opens in\nTerminal → Device consoles with a full terminal (colors, `--More--` paging, full-screen editors) and, next to it, the\n**device guide** for the maker: how to log in, the commands people use most (click to type one, ▶ to run it;\ncommands that change or restart the device ask first), a button that turns paging off, the maker's official\ndocumentation and a search for more commands. Guides: **FBRX Server** (FBRX Virtual, its roles and libvirt) and\n**FBRX Gate & MiniDome** (picked by themselves when the server's name has fbrx in it), Ubiquiti UniFi / EdgeMAX, Sophos Firewall, Cisco IOS / IOS XE,\nCisco Meraki (no CLI: dashboard pointers), Fortinet FortiGate, Palo Alto PAN-OS, Juniper Junos, HPE Aruba, MikroTik\nRouterOS, pfSense / OPNsense, TP-Link Omada / JetStream, NETGEAR, SonicWall, WatchGuard, Zyxel, Synology, QNAP,\nRaspberry Pi and generic Linux. *Web admin* opens the device's admin page; *Windows Terminal* opens the same SSH\nlogin outside FBRX.\n\n- **Saved connections:** every device you connect to is kept on the left and in the *Saved connections* view\n  (shown when no console is open): name, address, protocol, user, device guide, notes and when you last used it.\n  Edit one to name it and add notes, add one by hand, or untick *Keep in Saved connections* when connecting.\n  A key means its password is saved, so *Connect* logs in with one click. Removing a connection can forget its\n  password too. Passwords stay encrypted in the vault (Credentials); the list itself never holds them.\n- **Identity:** the device's SSH host key is saved the first time (you see its fingerprint) and a changed key is\n  refused with a warning until you confirm it.\n- **Logins:** user name and password (or keyboard-interactive), or your SSH keys in `~/.ssh` when the password is\n  empty. *Remember the password* stores it encrypted in the FBRX vault as an internal secret that plugins, the Local\n  API and the agent cannot read. Old firmware that only offers SHA-1 key exchange or CBC ciphers is retried\n  automatically with those allowed.\n- **Telnet** negotiates the window size and terminal type, fills in the user name and password at the first prompts,\n  and warns that it is unencrypted.\n- **Fabrix:** *Get help from Fabrix* and *Explain the screen* hand the console to the agent. With\n  `device_console.read` it reads what is on screen and with `device_console.send` it types one command at a time —\n  an *execute* tool, so each command waits for your approval with the exact text shown. It cannot open consoles or\n  see saved passwords.\n- Opening, typing into and reading consoles is reserved for the person at the computer (never the Local API, mesh\n  peers or remote commands); connections and disconnections are in the audit log.\n\nDevice scans start from a chosen **network adapter**, and the subnet is filled in from it (limited to /22–/30, at most\n1022 addresses, so a scan takes about a minute). Endpoint Ultra lets you type a different subnet.\n\nA new device found by a scan raises the *Unknown device joined my network* alert when that rule is on."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Presenter-safe mode","x":"For when your screen is on a projector, a classroom TV or shared in a meeting, in every edition (**Settings →\nPresenting**). While it is on:\n\n* notifications stay quiet (an urgent one only says that something needs a look), in the app and from the system;\n* the clipboard history shows dots instead of your copies (they still paste);\n* chat history, alerts, credentials and help desk tickets are blurred;\n* the Silly Goose stays home and approvals wait in the top bar.\n\nTurn it on with **Ctrl+Alt+P** (a macro you can change), the tray menu, or **Turn on now**; the top bar says\n**Presenting** while it is on (click it to turn it off). **Turn on by itself while a projector or second screen is\nconnected** does what it says (mirrored displays can look like one screen, so the shortcut is the sure way); turning it\noff by hand then keeps it off until the extra screen is unplugged. Education organizations turn the automatic switch\non for staff computers."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Help desk","x":"Once a computer belongs to an organization (FBRX Command), a **Help desk** tab appears in the sidebar. **New ticket**\nsends a problem to IT: a short subject, what it is about (this computer, Wi-Fi, printer, projector or sound, sign-in,\nan app), the details, *urgent* when a class or meeting cannot go on, and (unless you untick it) a short summary of the\ncomputer: name, system, free space, memory, network address and any failing FBRX services. **My tickets** and\n**History** show where each one stands and the conversation; you are told when IT answers, and can reply, say *It's\nfixed*, or open it again. The agent can write a ticket for you too (you approve it before it goes).\n\nComputers marked as **receivers** in FBRX Command (Devices → a device → *Receives help desk tickets*) get a **Queue**\ntab: every open ticket, newest and most urgent first, delivered within a second of being sent. Take a ticket, answer,\nchange its status and priority. Admins can answer from FBRX Command's Help desk page as well."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Help desk › Working on the computer that asked","x":"Under a ticket, a receiver has **Work on** *that computer*:\n\n| | What happens on the computer that asked |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Check it** (Run diagnostics) | A fresh snapshot comes back: processor, memory, disk, network (address, gateway, DNS), protection, problems and the busiest apps. Nothing is changed. **Explain** hands it to your own agent. |\n| **Ask its AI** | Its agent answers a question about that computer, looking things up with its own tools; anything that would change the computer asks the person there. |\n| **Run a command** | The command runs in its shell (PowerShell on Windows) and the output comes back, with **Explain**. |\n\nThe person at that computer is asked once per ticket (*Let … from the help desk work on this computer*, under\nApprovals, with a notification); a yes lasts 2 hours. Every command asks them again, by itself, and a no comes back as\n*Not run*. Each request is noted in the ticket (*Alex checked how Milo laptop is doing*), and FBRX Command's audit\nlog keeps it. A computer that is off gets the request when it is back (within 30 minutes).\n\nSettings → Messages → **Help desk** on each computer (or `helpdesk.assist` in a profile, *The help desk works on the\ncomputer*) chooses: **Ask me once per ticket** (the default), **Without asking** (commands then follow the governance\npolicy, like any remote request), or **Never**.\n\nTickets travel through FBRX Command rather than the mesh: Command keeps every ticket and its conversation (so nothing\nis lost while the IT computer is off or at home), works across networks, and pushes each change instantly over the\nencrypted connection every computer already keeps with it. The mesh stays for one person's own devices."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Mesh and FBRX Mobile","x":"Pair your other FBRX computers and your phone in **Mesh & phone**. See [MESH.md](MESH.md) for how it works and its\nsecurity model."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › AI coordination","x":"**AI coordination** finds other AI apps on the computer from the Start menu, the installed-programs list, running\nprocesses and their folders: Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, VS Code with GitHub Copilot, Hermes Agent,\nGemini CLI, LM Studio, Perplexity, Comet, Grok, ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Msty, AnythingLLM, Codex CLI, Ollama, Jan and\nGPT4All. Apps that support MCP (Claude Desktop and Code, Cursor, Windsurf, VS Code, Hermes Agent, Gemini CLI, LM Studio)\nconnect with one click and then see FBRX tools plus `ask_fabrix` (apps that still call it by its old name, `ask_fabric`, keep working). Apps without MCP on Windows (Perplexity, Grok, ChatGPT) can be\nopened from FBRX, and their makers' APIs can be added as models for second opinions (*Add as a model*; you need an\nAPI key from them). **Look again** re-scans and reports what it found.\n\nThe bridge (`fbrx-mcp.mjs`, started by the AI app with the FBRX executable in Node mode) talks to the Local API\nwith the **agent-scoped** token, read at start-up from `localapi-agent.json` in the data folder (never written into\nthe other app's configuration). Every call goes through the same governance as Fabrix's own: policy, guardian,\napprovals in FBRX, and the audit log. The Local API must be on.\n\n**Test** (next to a connected app) starts the bridge exactly the way the app will and checks that it lists the FBRX\ntools, so you know the link works before you switch apps. For Claude Desktop, FBRX writes the configuration for both\nthe regular installer and the Microsoft Store version (which keeps its settings under\n`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Packages\\Claude_…\\LocalCache\\Roaming\\Claude`); quit Claude completely from its tray icon and start it\nagain to load it. Then, in Claude, ask it to use FBRX (\"use FBRX to check my disk space\", \"ask Fabrix …\").\n\n**Hermes Agent** keeps its MCP servers in `~/.hermes/config.yaml` under `mcp_servers`. Connect adds one `fbrx` line\nthere and leaves every other line as it was, comments included (the old file is kept as `config.yaml.fbrx-backup`);\nDisconnect takes the line out again. Run `/reload-mcp` in Hermes, or start it again, and the FBRX tools appear named\n`mcp_fbrx_…`. A `mcp_servers` list written on one line (`mcp_servers: {…}`) is left alone: put each server on its own\nline, then connect again. FBRX finds Hermes on Linux and macOS, where it keeps its settings in your home folder (inside\nWSL on Windows, its settings are out of FBRX's reach).\n\n**Second opinion** asks another configured model the same question without tools. Claude Code, when installed, is\none of the choices: FBRX runs `claude -p` with the question and shows the answer, which is how to ask Claude from\nFBRX without an API key (an Anthropic API key, added in AI models, works too). FBRX cannot type into the Claude\nDesktop app itself."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Agent tools added","x":"| Tool | Risk |\n| --- | --- |\n| `workspace.list_tasks`, `workspace.search_notes`, `workspace.list_projects`, `workspace.search_snippets` | read |\n| `workspace.add_task`, `workspace.update_task`, `workspace.create_note`, `workspace.append_note`, `workspace.save_snippet` | write (allowed by the built-in `builtin-workspace` rule: they only touch FBRX's own data) |\n| `pc.live_status`, `pc.storage_report`, `pc.space_hogs`, `alerts.recent`, `net.wifi_channels`, `skills.list`; Windows: `pc.security_status`, `pc.event_errors`, `pc.pending_updates` | read |\n| `skills.save` | write (asks the person, so every learned skill is one they approved) |\n| `pc.clean_temp` | write |\n| `pc.run_fix` (Windows) | execute — asks by default |\n| `net.ping`, `net.traceroute`, `net.dns_lookup`, `net.port_check`, `net.scan_lan`, `net.check_link`, `net.speed_test` | network — offline chats ask to go online first |\n| `device_console.sessions`, `device_console.read` | read |\n| `device_console.send` | execute — asks by default, showing the exact command |\n| `calendar.agenda`, `calendar.free_time` (when a calendar is connected) | read |\n| `calendar.create_event` | write — adds to an Outlook calendar, never invites anyone |\n| `shield.status`, `shield.scan` (report only) | read |\n| `shield.quarantine` | write |\n| `memory.remember`, `memory.forget` | write (FBRX's own data; identity and money numbers are refused) |\n| `memory.recall`, `memory.search_chats` | read |\n| `fbrx.help`, `fbrx.doctor` | read: FBRX's knowledge base and the check-up |\n| `fbrx.fix` | write — asks first |\n| `files.request_access` | read: asks the person for a folder |"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Local AI without the stutter","x":"A local model left to itself takes every processor core at normal priority, which makes the whole computer stutter\nwhile it thinks. **Settings → Agent → How hard a local model may work this PC** sets it:\n\n| Setting | Built-in runtime and Ollama |\n| --- | --- |\n| Light | A quarter of the processor threads, lowest priority; Ollama unloads the model after 2 minutes |\n| Balanced (default) | Half the threads, below-normal priority; Ollama keeps it for 5 minutes |\n| Full speed | All threads but one, normal priority; Ollama keeps it for 30 minutes |\n\nThe built-in runtime also takes one conversation at a time (so the whole context goes to it), refuses a model that\nwould not fit in memory, loads only when a chat needs it (or at start-up when it is the default provider), and is\nunloaded after 20 idle minutes (**Unload the local model when idle**: 5 minutes to never). Answers stream into the\nwindow in small batches rather than token by token, and the system monitor samples every 10 seconds instead of 2\nwhile the window is hidden."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › New versions","x":"FBRX OS looks for new versions in its online repository a minute and a half after it starts and every six hours\n(**Settings → Updates → New versions**, where *Check now* and the automatic check live). When there is one, an alert\nsays so, an **Update to 1.x.y** pill appears in the top bar, and the dialog shows what is new. Updating is always your\nchoice:\n\n* **Update now** downloads the new version into the folder FBRX was installed from and starts the installer there in\n  its own window (Terminal on a Mac: press Return). FBRX closes, updates and opens again; your data, settings,\n  license and signing keys stay. Installs made before 1.8.1 don't know their folder yet: **Download** gets the new\n  zip; unzip it over your FBRX folder and run the installer once, and from then on Update now works.\n* **Later** keeps the pill; **Skip this version** stops the reminders for that version.\n* **Install new versions by itself** (or your organization's *Install automatically*) installs a new version without\n  asking, waiting until nobody is using the computer, nobody is presenting and the agent is idle. FBRX Command can also\n  send **Update now** to every computer at once (see ADMIN_CONSOLE.md).\n\nWindows copies signed by a publisher only install updates signed by the same publisher."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Thinking","x":"**Settings → Agent → Thinking** sets how much Fabrix does on one task, in every edition:\n\n| Level | For | Steps per task | What a local model keeps in mind | A local model thinks first |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Quick** | Small jobs and quick answers; it checks in with you sooner | 10 | 8K tokens | No |\n| **Everyday** (where every computer starts) | Everyday tasks | 30 | 8K tokens (Ollama: its own setting) | No |\n| **Thorough** | Big jobs and long documents | 100, the most there is | 16K tokens (about 1 GB more memory) | Yes |\n\nEvery level leaves the length of an answer to the model. A step is one round of thinking and using tools; when Fabrix\nuses them all it says so, and *continue* goes on. The steps are kept in the policy (with up to three tool calls a\nstep), so an organization can set them; when it sets any part, the levels are locked. **Show what Fabrix is thinking\nand doing** turns the live activity panel and the thinking notes on or off.\n\n**Pro and up** also set each part on its own, starting from the level's values; changing one makes the level\n**Custom** (picking a level puts it all back):\n\n| Setting | What it does | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Steps per task | 5 to 100. | 30 |\n| Longest answer | The most the model may write in one reply. A cut-off answer says so. | Automatic (64K tokens for Claude, the server's own limit for other models) |\n| Memory of the built-in model | The built-in runtime's context window: **Auto** (as much as this computer holds comfortably, up to 64K tokens, shown next to it; when the model runs on the graphics card, as much as the card has left, so the model stays on it) or 4K to 128K tokens, with the memory it needs. Kept in 8 bits with flash attention (`runtime.compactContext`), about half the memory. FBRX Server goes up to 128K on Auto. | Auto |\n| Memory of Ollama models | Ollama's context window (`num_ctx`); Ollama's own setting is often 4K. | Ollama's setting |\n| A local model thinks before it answers | Lets a model that can reason first (Qwen3, DeepSeek R1 and the like) do so on this computer. Better on hard questions, much slower: it writes hundreds of words of reasoning before the answer. | Off (on for Thorough) |"},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Thinking › Why a model on this computer is slower than a cloud AI","x":"FBRX is the banks of the river, not the river: every AI you pick gets the same tools, memory and governance, and\nFBRX helps a small one more and gets out of a capable one's way. A cloud AI sees every tool and decides how long it\nthinks; a model on this computer gets the help below.\n\nA cloud AI runs on accelerators built for it, with many times the memory speed of a computer's processor and RAM:\nwriting each word means reading the whole model from memory, so a laptop writes 5 to 15 words a second where a cloud\nAI writes 50 to 100, and it reads a long request in a moment where a laptop takes many seconds. FBRX gives a local\nmodel less to read and write:\n\n* **A short list of tools.** An AI that holds a long conversation (about 32K tokens or more: a cloud AI, a big\n  model on a server) is shown every tool (about 13,000 tokens of descriptions). The AI on this computer, however much\n  it holds, and any smaller one get a few it always has\n  (the time, its memory, FBRX's help and check-up) and the tools for what the chat is about, briefly described: the\n  files, the web, the computer, the calendar… A new subject adds its tools at the end. It may still call any tool it\n  is allowed to use. *Tools it is shown* on each provider (AI models → edit) chooses for yourself.\n* **The same start every time.** The instructions and tools stay the same from one request to the next (what Fabrix\n  remembers goes with your message), so the AI reuses what it already read and starts writing sooner: the built-in\n  runtime's prompt cache, and a cloud AI's (Claude's, for one).\n* **Room for the chat.** What the model keeps in mind is shared out after the instructions and the tools, so a long\n  chat is shortened from its start instead of overflowing.\n* **No thinking out loud first** (above), unless the level is Thorough or you turn it on.\n* **It says how far it has read.** On a processor a long request takes a while before the first word: the chat shows\n  how far the AI has read it (*Reading your request: 4,100 of 9,800 tokens*), with no time limit, so a slow computer\n  gets there instead of giving up. The AI on this computer answers one chat at a time: a second chat says it waits\n  for the other. Asking again after stopping does not add the question (and its files) a second time.\n\nOn a computer with 16 GB of memory, a 4B or 8B model is the quick choice; with 32 GB, a mixture-of-experts model\n(Qwen3 30B-A3B, which uses about 3B of its parameters for each word) answers about as fast as a 4B one and better. A\ngraphics card with enough memory for the model is the biggest speed-up of all."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › AI models","x":"**AI models** lists every way to run the agent:\n\n* **Built-in local runtime** (llama.cpp). *Install runtime* downloads the newest official llama.cpp build for this\n  computer: the Vulkan build when there is a graphics card with at least 2 GB of memory, otherwise the CPU build.\n  *Use* on a downloaded model makes it the default. A model that does not all fit in the graphics card's memory (Qwen3\n  14B on a laptop card, say) loads with half of its layers on the card, then none, and the page says how it runs;\n  the next start goes straight to what fit. When a model will not load, the page says why and offers the fix:\n  **Download again** for an incomplete or damaged file, **Update the runtime** for a model newer than the runtime,\n  a smaller model when memory is short, and **Show what the runtime said** for everything else. A download that\n  stops early is kept and continues where it stopped.\n* **Ollama models**: the models Ollama has already downloaded on this computer, read from Ollama, or from its model\n  folder (`OLLAMA_MODELS` or `~/.ollama/models`) while Ollama is not running, with *Start Ollama*. *Use* makes one the\n  default. With no model chosen, Fabrix picks the installed model best suited to tool calling.\n* **Your other computers' AI**: paired computers (your FBRX Server, say) that lend the AI they run themselves. *Use\n  for chats* makes one the default: Fabrix keeps working here, with its tools, memory and approvals, and the model\n  there does the thinking. From away, connect FBRX VPN first. See [MESH.md](MESH.md#think-with-your-fbrx-servers-ai).\n* **Providers**: Ollama, Claude, other OpenAI-compatible endpoints, and other computers' AI. The *Default model* is a\n  list of the provider's models (or *Other model…* to type a name).\n\nThe chat's model list shows the same installed models. If a chat asks Ollama for a model it does not have, the error\nnames the installed ones. Each answer in a chat shows the model that wrote it and how long it took from your question,\nso two AIs are easy to compare."},{"d":"docs/COMMAND_CENTER.md","t":"The FBRX OS command center › Easter eggs","x":"FBRX OS has a playful side, all of it under **Settings → Appearance → Fun extras** (and lockable by an organization).\nFor the full list (spoilers), see [EASTER_EGGS.md](EASTER_EGGS.md)."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment","x":"Running FBRX for an organization: your own FBRX Command, FBRX OS on your computers, and looking after them from there.\n\n```\nlicense signing key ─► FBRX Command ─► tenant ─► license ─► enrollment token ─► fbrx-provision.json ─► computers\n```"},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 1. License signing keys","x":"```bash\nnpm run keys:generate\n```\n\n* `.fbrx-keys/license-signing.pem` — **private**. Give it only to the control plane (`FBRX_LICENSE_PRIVATE_KEY`)\n  and keep an offline backup. It is git-ignored.\n* `apps/desktop/build/license-public-key.pem` — public. Commit it (or store it as the `FBRX_LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY`\n  CI secret); `apps/desktop/scripts/build.mjs` embeds it so installs verify licenses offline.\n\nAlready running a control plane that generated its own key? Export the public half instead:\n\n```bash\nnpm run keys:generate -- --from /path/to/control-plane-data/keys/license-signing.pem\n```\n\nBuilds without an embedded key accept no license key and run the free features (development builds unlock everything)."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane","x":"FBRX Command (the control plane and its console) runs three ways: **on a computer of your own** with the\n`Install FBRX Command` launcher (a family, a school, a small office), in **Docker** with a domain name and an\nautomatic public certificate (a hosted service for many customers), or **from the build** under your own process\nmanager."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane › FBRX Command on a computer of your own","x":"Double-click **`Install FBRX Command.cmd`** (Windows) or **`Install FBRX Command.command`** (macOS; on Linux run it\nwith `bash`) in the unzipped FBRX folder. It uses the same Node.js bootstrap as `Install FBRX OS` and runs\n`scripts/setup/command.mjs`:\n\n| Step | What happens |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dependencies | `npm ci` without Electron's binary (skipped if `Install FBRX OS` already installed them) |\n| Signing key | Copies `~/.fbrx-keys/license-signing.pem` (made by either installer) into FBRX Command's `data/keys/`, so its licenses are the ones your FBRX OS builds trust |\n| Build | The console and the server bundle |\n| Install | `app/` (server, console, launcher and its own Node.js) and `config.json` in FBRX Command's folder: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\FBRX Command`, `~/Library/Application Support/FBRX Command` or `~/.local/share/fbrx-command` |\n| At login | Windows: Start menu, desktop and Startup shortcuts (no console window). macOS: a login item (`~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.fbrx.command.plist`, restarted if it stops) and `~/Applications/FBRX Command.app`. Linux: autostart and applications-menu entries |\n| Start | Starts it and opens the first-run page with the setup token filled in |\n\n**Who connects** (asked, or `--network` / `--local`):\n\n* **Computers on this network** (default): HTTPS on `0.0.0.0:8787` with a certificate FBRX Command makes for itself\n  on first start (`data/tls/`, kept across updates). Computers trust it by its SHA-256 fingerprint: provisioning\n  files, Mac profiles, Windows scripts and license keys made in FBRX Command carry it, and a person joining by hand\n  is shown it to compare with **Deploy & enroll** (see SECURITY.md). The console on the computer itself is\n  `http://127.0.0.1:8788` (local only, no certificate warning). The address given to computers is this computer's\n  network address (`--address` to choose another, such as a DNS name); give the computer a fixed address in your\n  router. Windows and macOS ask once whether Node.js may accept connections: allow it.\n* **Only this computer**: plain HTTP on `127.0.0.1:8787`, to try FBRX Command or join FBRX on the same computer.\n\n**New versions.** FBRX Command carries the version of the FBRX release it was installed from (the same number as\nFBRX OS: *FBRX Command 1.9.8* at the bottom of the console). It checks the FBRX release list by itself a minute and a\nhalf after it starts and every six hours, and shows a newer version on **Releases** (and in a line across the top of\nthe console for the platform operator). **Update now** downloads the new FBRX folder over the one it was installed\nfrom (keeping `.fbrx-keys`, `.fbrx-setup` and `node_modules`), then runs `Install FBRX Command` there on the FBRX\nCommand computer: in its own window on Windows, in Terminal on a Mac (press Return), in the background on Linux\n(`logs/update.log`). FBRX Command stops for a few minutes and starts again with all its data. **Skip this version**\nstops the reminder; the switch on the card (or `FBRX_CP_UPDATE_CHECK=off`) turns the checks off. Only the platform\noperator (the account that set FBRX Command up) checks or installs; administrators see the version. FBRX Command as\nFBRX Server's command role updates with the server instead (`FBRX_CP_MANAGED_BY=fbrx-server`). FBRX Command\nfrom before 1.9.8 cannot update itself yet: update it once by hand (below), and from then on Update now works.\n\nRun the installer again to update by hand (same data, certificate and setup): download and unzip the new FBRX zip and\ndouble-click `Install FBRX Command` in the new folder. `--uninstall` stops FBRX Command and removes\nthe program, icons and login item but keeps `data/`; add `--delete-data` to erase it too. Other options: `--port`\n(default 8787; the local console uses the next port), `--yes`, `--no-open`. The installed launcher also works on\nits own: `/app/node /app/launcher.mjs open | start | stop | status`. Log: `.fbrx-setup/command-setup.log`;\nFBRX Command's own log: `logs/command.log` in its folder.\n\n**The setup token.** The first sign-in asks for a one-time setup token. The installer shows it (*Setup token*, a short\ncode like `4qZy-…`) and opening FBRX Command from its icon fills it in; it is also `setupToken` in `config.json` in\nFBRX Command's folder until setup is done. The long `AB:CD:…` code under *Certificate* is the certificate's\nfingerprint, not the token: the setup page says so if it is pasted there."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane › On FBRX Server","x":"FBRX Server runs FBRX Command as a role (`install.sh --roles …,command`, or *Roles* in the server's console): its own\ncertificate, a setup token, updates with the server, and the server itself joins it. See\n[SERVER.md](SERVER.md#fbrx-command-on-the-server-the-command-role)."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane › Docker (recommended for a hosted service)","x":"On a Linux host with ports 80/443 open and a DNS record (e.g. `fleet.example.com`) pointing at it:\n\n```bash\ncp deploy/control-plane/.env.example deploy/control-plane/.env    # set FBRX_DOMAIN, admin email/password, keys\ndocker compose -f deploy/control-plane/docker-compose.yml --env-file deploy/control-plane/.env up -d --build\n```\n\n* Caddy terminates TLS with an automatic Let's Encrypt certificate and proxies WebSockets.\n* All state is in the `fbrx-data` volume: `control-plane.db`, `keys/`, uploaded releases, snapshots and packages.\n* The image is also published by the Release workflow as `ghcr.io///control-plane:`; set\n  `FBRX_IMAGE` in `.env` and drop `--build` to deploy without building.\n* Behind your own load balancer instead of Caddy: run only the `control-plane` service, set\n  `FBRX_CP_TRUST_PROXY=1` and `FBRX_CP_PUBLIC_URL=https://…`, and allow WebSocket upgrades on `/v1/device/ws` and\n  `/v1/admin/ws` with long idle timeouts."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane › Without Docker","x":"```bash\nnpm ci && npm run build -w @fbrx/admin-console && npm run build -w @fbrx/control-plane\ncd apps/control-plane/dist && FBRX_CP_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/fbrx FBRX_CP_PUBLIC_URL=https://fleet.example.com node server.mjs\n```\n\n`dist/` is self-contained (one bundled `server.mjs` plus the console); copy it anywhere with Node 22.13+.\nRun it under systemd, launchd, NSSM or a PaaS."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 2. Control plane › Configuration","x":"| Variable | Default | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `FBRX_CP_PUBLIC_URL` | `http://localhost:` | External URL written into provisioning files and update feeds |\n| `FBRX_CP_DATA_DIR` | `.fbrx-cp-data` | Database, keys, files |\n| `FBRX_CP_PORT` / `FBRX_CP_HOST` | `8787` / `0.0.0.0` | Listener |\n| `FBRX_CP_TRUST_PROXY` | off | Honor `X-Forwarded-*` from a reverse proxy |\n| `FBRX_CP_TLS` | off | `self-signed`: serve HTTPS with FBRX Command's own certificate (made once in `/tls/`; computers pin its fingerprint) |\n| `FBRX_CP_TLS_CERT` / `FBRX_CP_TLS_KEY` | — | Serve HTTPS with a certificate you have (PEM files) |\n| `FBRX_CP_TLS_NAMES` | — | Extra names and addresses for the self-signed certificate (comma-separated; this computer's are added) |\n| `FBRX_CP_LOCAL_PORT` | — | Also answer on `http://127.0.0.1:`, for the console on the same computer |\n| `FBRX_CP_ADMIN_EMAIL` / `_PASSWORD` / `_NAME`, `FBRX_CP_ORGANIZATION` | — | Bootstrap the first superadmin and tenant on an empty database |\n| `FBRX_CP_ORGANIZATION_KIND` | `work` | Kind of that first tenant: `work`, `school` or `home` (the setup page asks instead when you set up in the browser) |\n| `FBRX_CP_SETUP_TOKEN` | random, printed | Without bootstrap variables, the console's first-run screen asks for this token |\n| `FBRX_CP_JWT_SECRET`, `FBRX_CP_MASTER_KEY`, `FBRX_LICENSE_PRIVATE_KEY` | generated into `keys/` | Supply from a secrets manager to keep key material out of the volume |\n| `FBRX_CP_SESSION_HOURS` | 12 | Admin session lifetime |\n| `FBRX_CP_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS` | 30 | Device heartbeat interval |\n| `FBRX_CP_SNAPSHOT_RETENTION` | 10 | Snapshots kept per device |\n| `FBRX_CP_MAX_UPLOAD_MB` | 4096 | Largest release/snapshot upload |\n| `FBRX_CP_LOG_LEVEL` | info | `fatal` … `trace`, or `silent` |\n\nFirst login: open the console, sign in, **enable MFA** (click your name, top right), then create tenants (one per customer or business\nunit), users and API keys. Health endpoint: `GET /healthz`."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 3. The Windows installer: upgrade, repair and uninstall","x":"`FBRX-OS-Setup-.exe` knows when FBRX OS is already installed. Instead of asking where to install, it opens on a\nmaintenance page:\n\n| Installed version vs. this Setup | Choices (default first) |\n| --- | --- |\n| older | **Upgrade** (keeps data, settings, license, paired devices) · Repair · Uninstall |\n| same | **Repair** (reinstall the program files, clear Chromium caches; data kept) · Uninstall |\n| newer | Downgrade (warns that newer data may not be readable; must be picked explicitly) · Uninstall |\n\n*Uninstall* keeps the data folder (`%APPDATA%\\FBRX OS`) unless *Also delete my data* is ticked. Uninstalling from\nWindows Settings asks the same question, defaulting to keep. Upgrades and repairs reuse the existing install folder\nand mode (just me / all users).\n\n**Closing a running app.** Setup and the uninstaller find every process started from the install folder: the app,\nits helpers, the local AI runtime and bridges that other AI apps start (`apps/desktop/build/fbrx-close.ps1`). The app\nis asked to quit properly first (`\"FBRX OS.exe\" --fbrx-quit` reaches the running copy, which stops the AI runtime and\ncloses its database even when it is minimized to the tray); anything still running after 20 seconds is ended. If\nsomething cannot be ended (for example it runs as administrator), Setup asks the user to quit it and retry. Without\nPowerShell, it falls back to `taskkill`.\n\n**Command line** (IT tools, scripts):\n\n| Switch | Effect | Exit code |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/S` | install, upgrade or reinstall without questions | 0; **3** if a newer version is installed |\n| `/S /ALLOWDOWNGRADE` | also allow replacing a newer version | 0 |\n| `/S /REPAIR` | reinstall and clear cached files | 0 |\n| `/S /UNINSTALL` | uninstall, keep the data | 0 (also when not installed) |\n| `/S /UNINSTALL --delete-app-data` | uninstall and delete the data folder | 0 |\n\n**Licenses.** A key saved as `fbrx-license.key` next to `Setup.exe` is copied to the user's data folder and activated\nwhen FBRX OS next starts. The setup wizard's *Make an installer to send* (`Install FBRX OS.cmd --share --for \"Name\"\n--days 30`) builds the key into the installer instead: it re-packages only the installer with\n`FBRX_SHARE_LICENSE=` set, which `installer.nsh` embeds at build time, and writes the result to `Share\\`.\nWithout `--for` it copies the plain installer there (for updates; the recipient's license is kept).\n\n**Unsigned installers** (a copy you built yourself) show *Windows protected your PC*; recipients click **More info → Run\nanyway**. Installers from a published release are signed, so they do not."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 4. Rolling out to workstations","x":"1. **Admin console → Deploy & enroll → New enrollment token.** Optionally choose a group, a usage limit, an expiry and a\n   **template snapshot** (a golden machine's backup that every new install starts from).\n2. Download **`fbrx-provision.json`** (see `deploy/provisioning/fbrx-provision.example.json`).\n3. Distribute the installer with the provisioning file using your tool of choice (Intune, Jamf, Kandji,\n   SCCM, a shared drive …). FBRX OS looks for it, in order:\n   * `--provision=` on the command line,\n   * `FBRX_PROVISION_FILE`,\n   * next to the executable,\n   * `/provisioning/fbrx-provision.json` (bake it into a customer-specific build by placing it in\n     `apps/desktop/resources/provisioning/` before packaging),\n   * macOS: `/Library/Application Support/FBRX OS/fbrx-provision.json`,\n   * Windows: `%ProgramData%\\FBRX OS\\fbrx-provision.json`,\n   * the data folder root.\n4. On first launch the device restores the template (if any, as a **clone** — fresh identity), enrolls, pulls its\n   managed configuration, license and secrets, and shows up in **Devices**. Users can also enroll by hand in\n   **Fleet** with the server URL and token.\n\nHeadless machines: `fbrx-headless enroll https://fleet.example.com fbrx_enr_… --name LAB-01`."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 4. Rolling out to workstations › Installing with a device manager","x":"FBRX Command makes the files: **Deploy & enroll → New enrollment token**, then in the dialog:\n\n* **Mac profile** (`.mobileconfig`): a configuration profile that sets FBRX's managed preferences (domain\n  `com.fbrx.os`: `ServerURL`, `EnrollmentToken`, `Audience`). Upload it with the FBRX installer to Jamf, Mosyle, Kandji,\n  Intune or another Mac manager. FBRX reads `/Library/Managed Preferences/com.fbrx.os.plist` when it starts and joins.\n* **Windows script** (`Install-FBRX.ps1`): for Intune, package it with `FBRX-OS-Setup-.exe` as a Win32 app\n  (`IntuneWinAppUtil`), install command `powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -NoProfile -File Install-FBRX.ps1`,\n  detection rule *file `%ProgramFiles%\\FBRX OS\\FBRX OS.exe` exists*. It writes\n  `%ProgramData%\\FBRX OS\\fbrx-provision.json` and installs for all users (`/S /allusers`). Without an installer next\n  to it, it downloads the current FBRX OS: the newest stable release uploaded to FBRX Command, or FBRX's own download\n  when that is newer.\n* Without a device manager the provisioning file works next to the installer, in `/Library/Application Support/FBRX OS`\n  (Mac) or `%ProgramData%\\FBRX OS` (Windows).\n\nA token's *used by* (or `\"audience\": \"student\"` in the provisioning file) makes the computers student computers."},{"d":"docs/DEPLOYMENT.md","t":"Deployment › 5. Operating the fleet","x":"* **Profiles** carry managed settings, policy and locks. A device gets its tenant's default profile overlaid by its\n  group's profile; secrets resolve tenant → group → device, most specific wins.\n* **Credentials** created in the console are encrypted at rest, pushed over the device channel, and stored as managed,\n  read-only vault entries; rotating or deleting them updates every device.\n* **Updates**: publish a release, choose a channel per tenant, group or device, pin versions where needed, and\n  raise the rollout percentage gradually. Enrolled devices check shortly after launch, every 4 hours, and on the `update.check` command;\n  `update.install` applies immediately.\n* **Commands** run in real time on online devices and queue for offline ones.\n* **Webhooks** (`device.enrolled`, `device.offline`, `device.alert`, `command.failed`, `snapshot.uploaded`,\n  `license.issued`, `release.published`, …) are HMAC-signed (`x-fbrx-signature: sha256=` over the raw body)\n  for ITSM, SIEM, chat and billing integrations."},{"d":"docs/DIGGER.md","t":"FBRX Digger","x":"FBRX Digger is the FBRX **Network Center on its own**: a small app for anyone who looks after networks, with every\nnetwork tool FBRX has and nothing else. It runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, next to FBRX OS or without it."},{"d":"docs/DIGGER.md","t":"FBRX Digger › What it does","x":"* **Overview**: this computer, the router, internet latency and loss, the public address and a plain-language verdict.\n* **Devices**: scan the network (ping sweep, ARP, mDNS/Bonjour, UPnP, port fingerprints and the IEEE MAC registry\n  name PCs, phones, printers, TVs, cameras, smart-home devices and NAS), save scans, **compare** two scans to see what\n  joined and what left, and export them as CSV.\n* **Ping and trace route** with each hop identified (your router, double NAT, your provider, the internet).\n* **Speed test** with history.\n* **Wi-Fi channel planner**: every network around drawn over the channels it covers, how busy each channel is, the\n  best channel for your network with the reason, the channel width to set, DFS channels, how busy each channel has\n  been, and every access point with its signal.\n* **Tools**: DNS comparison and port checks.\n* **Consoles**: SSH and Telnet to routers, switches, firewalls and servers, straight from a scan or the Consoles tab,\n  with SSH host keys pinned on first use and the session open right there. A refused login says why in the connect\n  box, ready for another try.\n* **Network environments**: attach a network controller (UniFi) to see its sites, devices and clients.\n* A router's or device's own page opens in your computer's browser.\n\nEverything is included, always: Digger has no editions, and its Settings never mention them. It is the network tools of\nFBRX Endpoint, on their own."},{"d":"docs/DIGGER.md","t":"FBRX Digger › On its own","x":"Digger needs nothing else: no FBRX account, no edition and no FBRX OS. It keeps its own data folder and settings, runs\nnone of FBRX Endpoint's background work (no AI of its own, no backups, antivirus or organization), and leaves the\nkeyboard alone (no global shortcuts), so it never gets in the way of FBRX OS on the same computer.\n\n**Fabrix, while FBRX Endpoint runs.** When FBRX Endpoint is running on the same computer, Digger finds its AI by\nitself: **FBRX Endpoint's AI** shows at the top, with **Ask Fabrix**, *Diagnose with Fabrix* and the other ask buttons\non the network pages. Digger's Fabrix thinks with Endpoint's AI model, while its tools, chats and approvals stay in\nDigger; nothing leaves the computer. Close FBRX Endpoint and they go away until it is back. Endpoint lends only an AI it\nruns itself (not a cloud provider), and not while its AI is on emergency stop.\n\nIts icon is a mole in a miner's helmet, lamp lit, drawn in honey and red on black.\n\nDigger is FBRX OS's own Network Center, so every network tool FBRX OS gets, Digger gets too, in the same release."},{"d":"docs/DIGGER.md","t":"FBRX Digger › Install","x":"Download it from the repository's releases: the release named **FBRX Digger** (tagged `digger-v`).\n\n| Computer | File |\n| --- | --- |\n| Windows | `FBRX-Digger-Setup-.exe` |\n| Mac with Apple silicon | `FBRX-Digger--arm64.dmg` |\n| Intel Mac | `FBRX-Digger--x64.dmg` |\n| Linux | `FBRX-Digger--x86_64.AppImage`, or the `.deb` for Debian and Ubuntu |\n\nUnsigned builds show *Windows protected your PC* (More info → Run anyway) or, on a Mac, need System Settings →\nPrivacy & Security → **Open Anyway** the first time. `SHA256SUMS.txt` in the release lists every file's checksum.\n\nDigger tells you when a new version is out (Settings → Updates): it reads the `digger` entry of `release.json` and\nlinks to the new release."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Unlocking them: 418","x":"The easter eggs, the Silly Goose and the trophy case come with every edition of FBRX Endpoint (Essentials, Pro and\nUltra), but they stay hidden until someone enters **418** as a license key (**Settings → License**). It is not a\nlicense: it is HTTP status 418, *I'm a teapot*. The locked trophy case in Settings drops the only hint: \"The key is\nshort and stout.\" Some eggs live on pages a lighter edition does not show (the Terminal's are in Ultra, Dash's in\nPro and Ultra); the rest, the chat's among them, work everywhere. Student computers have no License page, so they\nstay plain.\n\nOnce unlocked, **Settings → Appearance → Fun extras** appears, with a switch to turn them off again. An organization\ncan turn them off and lock the setting from FBRX Command (`appearance.easterEggs`). None of them changes files or\nsettings."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › The trophy case","x":"Every easter egg has a badge in **Settings → Trophy case** (also in Spotlight: \"trophy case\", and the little trophy\ncount in the sidebar footer). Badges you haven't found are greyed out with a hint; found ones are in color with how and\nwhen you earned them. A toast with **See trophy case** pops up the moment you find one.\n\nFind all 26 and you unlock the **Golden Goose**: from then on the goose arrives wearing a golden egg with a red **#1**\nribbon on its back (and says \"HONK! (#1)\"). The record lives on this computer; `show trophies` in FBRX/1 lists it.\n\n| Badge | How to earn it |\n| --- | --- |\n| Goose Wrangler | Release the Silly Goose (see below) |\n| Shoo! | Click the goose three times until it gives up and goes home |\n| Cheat Code | Type the classic cheat code anywhere in FBRX: ↑ ↑ ↓ ↓ ← → ← → B A |\n| Master Weaver | Click the FBRX logo (top left) seven times quickly: the loom spins |\n| Safety First | Open **Library → The Lab** |\n| Storyteller | Search the Library for \"tell me a story\" (or \"story time\", \"once upon a time\") |\n| Operator Mode | Talk to the Library search like a switch: `show commands`, `enable`, `configure terminal` or `fbrx help`. It unlocks **FBRX commands** in the Library (Ultra) |\n| Digital Rain | Type `matrix` in the Terminal |\n| Sandwich Artist | Type `sudo make me a sandwich` in the Terminal |\n| I'm a Teapot | Type `coffee` in the Terminal |\n| On the Edge | Flip coins in **Toolbox → Decision maker** until one lands on its edge |\n| Natural 20 | Roll a 20 on the Decision maker's d20 |\n| Time Traveler | Click **Run test** in **Network Center → Speed** eight times in a row |\n| My Stapler | Ask Fabrix \"Have you seen my stapler?\" (or \"Where is…\", \"Has anyone seen…\") |\n| Synergy Achieved | Keep the jargon manager talking for five answers |\n| Back-Seat Radar | Say \"Talk to me, Goose\" to Fabrix |\n| Wingman for Life | Keep your wingman on the radio for five messages |\n| Loose Screw | Click the loose **Tip** tile on Dash until it falls off |\n| Flap Happy | Run your mouse across twenty letters of the split-flap board on Dash |\n| Nobody Calls Me Chicken | Set \"What should FBRX call you?\" to `chicken` (Settings → General). Bawk. |\n| PC Load Letter | Say \"PC load letter\" (or \"PC load better\") to Fabrix |\n| Home Sweet Ship | Tell Fabrix you're home, calling your furry co-pilot by name (you'll know the line) |\n| Laugh It Up | Say \"Laugh it up, fuzzball\" while Fabrix is growling |\n| Plot Twist | Say \"Nooo\" (three o's or more) while Fabrix is growling |\n| Growl Whisperer | Keep talking to your furry co-pilot for five messages |\n| Who's Who? | On **Mesh & phone**, ping the same computer three times in a row (no other computer? click your own address three times) |\n| **Golden Goose** | Find all of the above |"},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › The Silly Goose","x":"Our own desktop goose, with one signature look: brass-rimmed aviator goggles pushed up on his head. A goose waddles\nacross the screen FBRX OS is on for about a minute and a half, then leaves by itself. It eases into and out of its\nwalks, waddles in step with its feet and turns around with a little pivot. While it is around it:\n\n- honks (a synthesized honk, with a speech bubble),\n- drops a loose feather now and then, which drifts down and fades away,\n- chases your mouse pointer, and when it catches up gives it a friendly boop (\"boop.\" or \"tag. you're it.\") and\n  waddles off. Your pointer always stays yours,\n- runs off the edge of the screen and comes back dragging a sticky note (\"I ate your Wi-Fi password. It was\n  delicious.\") or a framed *Portrait of a Goose, oil on canvas*. Click a note to put it away.\n\nHe dresses for the season where you are (the southern hemisphere is worked out from your time zone), or the one you\npick under *The goose's wardrobe* in Settings → Appearance → Fun extras, which shows him in it:\n\n| Season | What changes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Winter | A red striped scarf whose ends flap as he walks, and green snow boots with fluffy cuffs |\n| Fall | An orange scarf, and a leaf that blew onto his back |\n| Spring | Nothing on him; a butterfly keeps him company |\n| Summer | Now and then he plants a beach umbrella and enjoys the shade (\"ahh. ☀\"), and his trips off screen sometimes come back with a postcard of himself at the beach (\"Wish you were here. The Wi-Fi is terrible. — G\") |\n\nIt lives in a transparent, always-on-top window: clicks go straight through it to your apps, except on the goose and\nits notes. Click the goose and it flees; click it three times and it leaves (\"Fine. FINE.\"). The tray menu has\n**Shoo the goose**.\n\nHow to summon it:\n\n| Where | What |\n| --- | --- |\n| Anywhere in FBRX | The classic cheat code: ↑ ↑ ↓ ↓ ← → ← → B A (\"Cheat code accepted: 30 extra lives. Also, a goose.\") |\n| Spotlight (Alt+Space) | Type `honk` or `goose` → *Release the goose* |\n| Terminal | Type `honk` |\n| Tray menu | *Release the goose* |\n| Settings → Appearance → Fun extras | *Release the goose*, or *Let the goose drop by now and then* (about once every two hours while you are at the computer) |\n| Settings → Trophy case | *Show off the egg* (after the Golden Goose) |\n| April 1 | It drops by on its own, once |"},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Have you seen my stapler?","x":"Ask Fabrix \"Have you seen my stapler?\", \"Where is my stapler?\" or \"Has anyone seen my stapler?\" (or just \"stapler\").\nIt answers at once, without asking a model, with where the stapler went (storage, in the basement, most likely), and\nfrom then on that conversation is run by a middle manager in full office-comedy mode. Like the furry co-pilot below,\nevery answer is made up on the spot from a big pile of corporate jargon (circling back, synergy, bandwidth, cover\nsheets, decks about decks…), has very little to do with what you asked, and ends with \"That would be great.\" Keep him\ntalking for five answers for a trophy. Say \"PC load letter\" (or \"PC load better\") to get the normal Fabrix back and\nanother trophy; \"I quit\", \"normal mode\" or \"no more jargon\" work too. New conversations are always normal."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Talk to me, Goose","x":"Say \"Talk to me, Goose\" to Fabrix and your wingman gets on the radio from the back seat (and the Silly Goose flies in\non your screen). For the rest of that conversation every answer is radio chatter: bogeys that turn out to be clouds,\nvolleyball at 1600 hours, a very firm \"no\" from the tower. Random, and mostly unrelated to what you said. Say \"wheels\ndown\", \"That's a copy\", \"return to base\", \"land the plane\" or \"normal mode\" to land. Two trophies: one for getting him on the radio,\none for keeping him there for five messages. The Library's Story time has a matching how-to, *How to be a great\nwingman*."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Home sweet ship","x":"Tell Fabrix you're home the way a certain space smuggler tells his furry co-pilot, and Fabrix answers only in\ngrowls (with the occasional stage direction) for the rest of that conversation. Say \"Nooo\" (with at least three o's)\nfor a family secret before it goes back to growling. \"Laugh it up, fuzzball\" brings plain English back. Four trophies\nhide in here."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Nothing is impossible","x":"Type \"nothing is impossible\" into Spotlight or the Library search and it answers with Luke 1:37 (\"For nothing will be\nimpossible with God.\"). No trophy; it's a quiet one."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › 88 miles per hour","x":"In **Network Center → Speed**, click **Run test** eight times in quick succession. The needle swings back and forth,\nclimbs to 88 (mph), the dial lights up blue with lightning, and the readout lands on **1.21 gigawatts**. A few seconds\nlater everything drops back to zero as if nothing happened. (A real test you started keeps running underneath.)"},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Who's who?","x":"On **Mesh & phone**, choose **More → Send a ping** for the same computer three times in a row. The two computers appear side\nby side, pointing at each other, each convinced the other is the copy (\"Wait… you're me?\" \"No. YOU'RE me.\"). No other\ncomputer yet? Click this computer's own address (under *Reachable at*) three times: it pings itself and has the same\ncrisis. Click anywhere to make them stop."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Dash","x":"- **The split-flap board** flips through status messages like a railway departures board. Run your mouse across the\n  letters and they riffle (with a faint clack).\n- **The loose tile.** The **Tip** tile is a little crooked and one of its screws is backing out. Click it: it rattles\n  (\"Hm. That rattled.\" … \"Please stop. It's hanging by one screw.\"). On the sixth click it swings off its last screw\n  and falls, revealing what was behind it: a lost sock, a shiny coin and a note in goose handwriting. **Screw it\n  back on** puts it back.\n- Now and then the board says \"FBRX DASH / NOW 40% MORE SHINY\" or \"HONK / THE GOOSE SAYS HI\"."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Story time","x":"Search the Library for \"tell me a story\" and a **Story time** chip appears (and stays): how-tos nobody needed, each\nwinking at a film, book or game, with a riddle-like hint at the end. They are written in our own words: no titles,\ncharacter names, trademarks or famous lines, so guessing is the fun. *How to fly (sort of)*, *How to wait for a\ndownload that is stuck at 99%*, *How to call home from very far away*, *How not to panic*, *How to get back to the eighties* (which\nhints at the speed test), *How to build a snowman*, *How to get home in three clicks* (home is 127.0.0.1), *How to fix\na printer with a mind trick*, *How to deal with a haunted computer*, *How to take a very long walk to return some\njewelry*, *How to design a superhero outfit* (skip the cape), *How to stop worrying about your computer*, *How to\nshrink things without shrinking the kids*, *How to find what you are looking for*, *How to fix the same problem every\nsingle day*, *How to be a great wingman* (which tells you what to say to Fabrix) and *How to befriend a dragon*. Several end with a button to the FBRX page that actually helps."},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Toolbox → Decision maker","x":"- **Flip a coin.** Now and then (about one flip in a hundred) a coin lands on its edge: \"Edge! Take the rest of the\n  day off.\" Patience pays: by flip 150 it is guaranteed. The flip count shows under the result so you can tell you're\n  getting closer.\n- **Roll a d20.** A 20 is announced as a natural 20 (critical success); a 1 gets a sympathetic \"Oof.\"\n- **Ask the goose.** Type a yes-or-no question and the goose answers like a fortune-telling toy: \"Signs point to honk.\",\n  \"Outlook good, if you back up first.\", \"Yes, but wear safety goggles.\"…"},{"d":"docs/EASTER_EGGS.md","t":"Easter eggs (spoilers) › Elsewhere","x":"| Where | What |\n| --- | --- |\n| Library | **The Lab**: power-user how-tos, a hazard-striped banner and \"Safety goggles recommended (sold separately)\", with a dry remark for every section of downloads |\n| Dash | A line under the board at special moments: after midnight, on Friday afternoons (\"Maybe don't deploy anything\"), after 14 days without a restart (\"Have you tried turning it off and on again?\"), when the battery is nearly empty, on Halloween and on April Fools' Day |\n| Terminal | `matrix` makes it rain green code; `sudo make me a sandwich` → \"Okay.\"; `make me a sandwich` → \"What? Make it yourself.\"; `coffee` → 418 I'm a teapot; `xyzzy` → \"Nothing happens.\"; `hello`; `exit`. And `rm -rf /`, `format c:` and friends are refused: \"Fabrix hid the sharp objects.\" |\n| FBRX/1 | `request goose release` |\n| Toolbox → Base64 | Decode the sample |\n| Settings → Logs & about | Read the small print |"},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school","x":"FBRX Command with an **Education license** runs a school's computers: staff on **FBRX Endpoint** (up to Pro for teachers,\nUltra for IT), students on **FBRX OS Education**, a help desk from every classroom to IT, and updates that install\nthemselves. This guide walks through setting one up, using a K-12 co-op as the example: teachers on MacBooks, a\nnetwork on UniFi, staff accounts in Google Workspace."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › What each computer gets","x":"| | Teachers and staff | IT | Students |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Runs | FBRX Endpoint, up to **Pro** | FBRX Endpoint, up to **Ultra** | **FBRX OS Education** |\n| Agent | Fabrix with every tool, asking before it changes anything | Fabrix with every tool, plus the network | A learning helper that only talks: no files, apps, websites or commands |\n| Pages | The everyday ones (see COMMAND_CENTER.md) | Everything, including Network Center → Environments (UniFi), Task Manager, Terminal | Home, the helper, Get help, Calendar, Tasks, Notes, Toolbox, Library, Settings |\n| Classroom defaults | Presenter-safe mode turns on with a projector, chats start offline, no jokes | Same, changeable | Same, and the fun extras, mesh and local API locked off |\n| Help desk | Sends tickets | Receives everyone's tickets | Sends tickets (\"Get help\") |\n\nThe defaults come first; your own profiles in FBRX Command can change them for staff. Student computers keep their\nlocks."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 1. Make the tenant a school","x":"No FBRX Command yet? On a computer at the school that stays on, double-click **`Install FBRX Command`** in the FBRX\nfolder (computers on the school network join it over HTTPS with its own certificate), or host it with Docker and a\ndomain name (DEPLOYMENT.md).\n\nPick **School** when FBRX Command asks what it will run: on the first-run setup page, or in **Tenants → New tenant**\nfor each further school. An existing tenant changes kind under **Profiles & groups → Organization defaults → Kind**.\n\nThen **Licenses → Issue license** with *School (Education)* as the kind of license (the edition decides Ultra; groups\ncan hold computers to Essentials or Pro). Issuing a license with a kind also sets the tenant's kind. From the command line:\n`npm run license:issue -- … --vertical education`."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 2. Groups and tokens in one click","x":"**Profiles & groups → Set up for a school** (or the step right after **New tenant**) creates three groups, each with\nan enrollment token and a provisioning file to download:\n\n* **Teachers**: staff, up to Endpoint Pro.\n* **IT**: staff, Endpoint Ultra (what the license gives).\n* **Students**: student computers, FBRX OS Education.\n\nCopy the tokens when they are shown. Any group can be edited later: *Used by* (staff or student), *Edition* (Essentials only, up to Pro, or\nwhat the license gives) and *New versions* (see below)."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 3. Put FBRX on the computers","x":"* **Macs with a device manager** (Jamf, Mosyle, Kandji, Intune, or any manager linked to Apple School Manager):\n  **Deploy & enroll → New enrollment token** (pick the group), then **Mac profile** downloads a configuration profile.\n  Upload it to the device manager with the FBRX installer; FBRX reads it from `/Library/Managed Preferences` and joins\n  on its next start. Google's own Mac management may not accept custom profiles in every edition; if yours does not,\n  use one of the next two ways.\n* **Windows with Intune**: **Windows script** downloads `Install-FBRX.ps1`. Package it with `FBRX-OS-Setup-.exe`\n  as a Win32 app (install command and detection rule are at the top of the script); it installs for all users and\n  joins.\n* **No device manager**: put `fbrx-provision.json` next to the installer, or install FBRX and paste the address and\n  token on its **Organization** page. Tick **This computer is for a student or a child** on student laptops: a computer can always\n  make itself a student computer when it joins, but only IT (in FBRX Command) can make a student computer a staff one.\n\nInstallers from a published release are signed and notarized; installers pushed by a device\nmanager also skip the browser download warnings."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 4. Staff sign-in to FBRX Command","x":"Staff who manage FBRX (IT, the office) sign in to FBRX Command with their Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 accounts:\n**Users & API keys → Sign in with Google or Microsoft** (ADMIN_CONSOLE.md). Teachers don't need an FBRX Command account;\ntheir computers belong to the organization."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 5. The help desk","x":"Mark the IT computer as a receiver: **Devices → the IT computer → Configuration → Receives help desk tickets**. Every\ncomputer in the school then has a Help desk tab (Get help on student computers). Tickets reach the IT computer within\na second, with a short summary of the sending computer, and replies go straight back. FBRX Command's **Help desk** page\nshows every ticket and lets admins answer too. Turn the help desk off under Organization defaults if you use another\nticket system (webhooks `ticket.created` and `ticket.updated` can feed it).\n\nFrom a ticket, the IT computer can also **work on the computer that asked**: check how it is doing, ask its AI, or run a\ncommand there. The person at it lets IT in once per ticket and approves each command (a profile can change that with\n*The help desk works on the computer*). See [COMMAND_CENTER.md](COMMAND_CENTER.md#working-on-the-computer-that-asked)."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 6. Updates","x":"**Organization defaults → New versions**:\n\n* **Install automatically**: computers install new versions by themselves when nobody is using them (never while\n  someone is presenting or the agent is busy), from FBRX Command's published releases or from the GitHub repository\n  they were installed from.\n* **Tell people** (default): they are told and choose when.\n* **Off**: no checks; IT updates with **Devices → Update everyone now**.\n\n**Update everyone now** works whatever the setting: every online computer installs the newest version right away;\noffline ones do it when they reconnect."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › 7. The network (IT, Ultra)","x":"On the IT computer, **Network Center → Environments → Attach a UniFi console** shows every gateway, switch, access\npoint and client, lets you restart a device, and makes guest Wi-Fi codes for visitors and events (COMMAND_CENTER.md)."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › Students and safety","x":"* The learning helper on FBRX OS Education keeps answers short, kind and age-appropriate, teaches rather than doing\n  graded work for the student, never asks for personal details, and has no tools at all: it cannot open files, apps or\n  websites, and FBRX refuses any tool call it attempts.\n* If a student writes that they might hurt themselves or that someone is hurting them, the helper answers with care and\n  points to a teacher, the counselor or another trusted adult, 911 in an emergency, and **988** (call or text, the\n  Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or the Childhelp hotline. FBRX Command gets a **Student safety** alert naming the computer\n  and the kind of concern, so a caring adult can check in. **The student's message is not sent**; it stays on that\n  computer.\n* Conversations stay on the student's computer. FBRX Command sees health, version and ticket information, not chats or\n  files.\n* Student computers start with presenter-safe mode on a projector, chats offline first, and easter eggs, the mesh and the\n  local API locked off. Organization **Messages** start off for students; turn them on in a profile for the Students\n  group if you want them, or use the **No chatting** preset to keep any group off ([MESSAGES.md](MESSAGES.md)). Choose which AI model they use in a profile for the Students group (a local model keeps\n  everything on the computer).\n\nSchools have their own duties under student privacy law (FERPA and state student data privacy rules); review the\nsettings above with whoever is responsible for them before rolling FBRX out to students."},{"d":"docs/EDUCATION.md","t":"FBRX at a school › Families","x":"The same design runs a family: pick **Home** instead of School, and children's computers run **FBRX OS Home** with\nthe learning helper and the same protections. See [HOME.md](HOME.md)."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work","x":"Fabrix (the agent; its name is yours to change in Settings → Agent) does more than answer. It reads the files and\npictures you give it, makes documents and pictures, reads the web, does jobs by itself on a schedule, and looks at\nyour screen when you hand it a snapshot. Everything here follows the computer's policy like any other tool: what\nneeds a yes asks for one, and the audit log keeps what was done."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › How Fabrix works","x":"Whatever model answers (on this computer, on your FBRX Server, FBRX Cloud AI or another cloud AI), FBRX gives it the\nsame rules:\n\n- **Build alongside you, and heal what it touches.** It leaves files, settings and systems better than it found them,\n  never worse, and prefers the fix that repairs a problem over one that hides it.\n- **Do no harm, whoever asks.** Being told to is not enough: when a request would damage data, a computer, a network or\n  a person, it says so and offers a safe way to what you need.\n- **Point out what else needs care.** While it works it notices other problems (a failing service, a setting that\n  weakens security, a nearly full disk) and lists them after the task under *Also noticed*, with the fix it recommends.\n  It changes them only when you say yes.\n- **Only claim what a tool confirmed.** If a tool failed, or it has none for what you asked, it says so.\n- **Show the plan.** For a job of three or more steps it shows its plan as a checklist in the chat and ticks steps off\n  as it goes (`plan.update`; only the latest version of the plan shows).\n- **Check its work.** After a change it reads it back when it can (the file it wrote, the task it added, the event it\n  made) before it says it is done.\n- **Ask once, when it matters.** When a request could mean two quite different things, or needs something only you\n  know (which file, which day, which account), it asks one short question first; otherwise it takes the sensible\n  reading, says which, and goes.\n\nIt never runs the same tool with the same input twice in one task: a repeat gets what the first call gave, and after\ntwo it answers with what it has.\n\n**Queue the next thing.** While Fabrix works you can keep typing: **Enter** (or **Queue**) puts the message under *Up\nnext*, and each one is sent when the answer before it is done, in order. Edit one (back into the box) or remove it\nfrom the list. **Stop** pauses the rest until you choose *Go on*.\n\nFourteen everyday requests (packages/core/src/ai/eval/everyday-tasks.ts) are checked with every change: each must give\neven a small model the tools and the skill the job needs."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Its room: the context window and the monthly budget","x":"- **The gauge under the chat** shows how much of the model's context window the conversation takes, such as\n  *Context 9.8K / 16K tokens*: estimated when a chat opens, then as the provider counted each request. Amber past three\n  quarters, red when full. Click it for AI usage.\n- **Fabrix knows it too.** Once its room fills (past half of the context window or of the month's budget, or once\n  older requests are folded), a short note goes with your message: its context window, how full it is, its longest\n  answer, its steps for the task and, with a monthly budget, the month. Near a limit it works in fewer, smaller steps\n  and says so once; at a limit it stops cleanly, with what is done, what is left and how to go on.\n- **The built-in model's window fits the computer.** On **Auto** (the default from 2.2.0; Settings → Agent →\n  Thinking → Memory of the built-in model) FBRX reads from the model file how much memory each token of context takes\n  and gives it as much as the computer holds comfortably, after the model itself and 2 GB for everything else, up to\n  64K tokens and never more than the model was made for. The context's memory is kept in 8 bits with flash attention,\n  about half the size, so the same memory holds twice the conversation; a model or runtime that cannot do that is\n  loaded again with the full-size one.\n- **Long conversations are folded, not cut.** When the older requests no longer fit, they become a short digest: what\n  was asked, what came of it, the tools used and the ids made (a project's, a note's), exactly. Nothing in the digest is\n  made up, because no model is asked to summarize. The gauge says how many requests are folded.\n- **Monthly cloud AI budget** (Settings → Agent): the most cloud AI may read and write on this computer in a month.\n  Models on this computer are not counted. Past three quarters Fabrix works sparingly; at the limit a task stops between\n  steps and new chats with cloud AI wait for next month or a higher budget.\n- **AI usage** (next to AI models): this month's tokens by day and by model, the budget, what the agent's model holds,\n  and your FBRX account's FBRX Cloud AI. Only counts are kept, never what was said."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Soul: who Fabrix is, and your commands","x":"**soul.md** is Fabrix's own file of who it is and how it works on your computer (*Settings → Soul*). It comes with\nFBRX and is yours to change:\n\n* **Who I am, What I care about, How I talk**: its character. Your data stays on the computer, it does no harm\n  whoever asks, it says it did something only when a tool shows it worked, and it starts with the answer.\n* **Options**: how it works for you, one line each, like `length: short`, `emoji: off`, `plan-first: 3 steps`,\n  `ask-before-changes: always`, `also-noticed: on` or `units: your region's`. Change a value and Fabrix follows it from\n  your next message.\n* **Commands**: start a message with one and Fabrix does it. `/help` lists them all, and `/help wifi` the ones about\n  Wi-Fi. There are more than sixty, in groups:\n\n  | Group | For example |\n  | --- | --- |\n  | Getting around | `/status`, `/explain`, `/plan`, `/go`, `/undo`, `/audit` |\n  | This computer | `/checkup`, `/slow`, `/disk`, `/clean`, `/updates`, `/errors`, `/fix` |\n  | Network | `/net`, `/wifi`, `/speed`, `/ip`, `/dns`, `/ping`, `/trace`, `/port`, `/scan`, `/flushdns`, `/renew` |\n  | Security | `/security`, `/scanfile`, `/password`, `/newpassword` |\n  | FBRX Server and Gate | `/server`, `/gate`, `/dome`, `/lan` |\n  | Mesh and devices | `/mesh`, `/askmesh`, `/consoles`, `/sites`, `/guests` |\n  | Work | `/brief`, `/agenda`, `/tasks`, `/todo`, `/note`, `/doc`, `/web`, `/ticket` |\n  | You and me | `/remember`, `/forget`, `/skills`, `/kit`, `/toolkits`, `/soul`, `/tune`, `/short`, `/detail`, `/eli5` |\n\n  Add your own the same way: a line like `` - `/standup` — Read my tasks due today and draft three lines for the team. ``\n* **Toolkits**: what Fabrix works with, by family. Ask it (or type `/toolkits`) and it reads the live list: each tool,\n  whether it works right now, and for FBRX Server and FBRX Gate what you can do on the server itself (its console\n  pages and the `fbrx-server` and `fbrx-gate` commands).\n* **Notes**: how you like it to work, as it learns.\n\nFabrix reads its soul with every message (the commands only when you type one, so a small AI on your computer stays\nquick). Ask it to work differently from now on (or type `/tune` and what to change) and it offers the change to\nsoul.md: you see the change and approve it first. Every change keeps the version before it, so *Undo the last change*\ngoes back; *Use FBRX's* goes back to FBRX's soul.md. Until you change yours, it follows FBRX's newer soul.md when FBRX\nupdates. Student and child computers have the learning helper instead, with no soul or commands."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Skills: jobs it knows how to do","x":"A skill is how Fabrix does a job of several steps, written down so it does it the same good way next time.\n\n- **Starter skills** come with FBRX: *Slow or dropping Wi-Fi* (the channel planner, then ping and a speed test),\n  *Free up disk space* (the storage report and the space hogs, safe things first), *Computer feels slow*, *Security\n  check-up*, *What's on my network*, *Troubleshoot the network* (the internet check, then FBRX Gate and MiniDome on your\n  FBRX Server when it lets Fabrix look: see [SERVER.md](SERVER.md#your-servers-in-fbrx-endpoint)) and *Plan my day*.\n- **Learned skills**: after a job that took three or more tool steps and is likely to come up again, Fabrix saves how\n  it did it (`skills.save`). FBRX asks you first, like any change, so a skill is kept only when you approve it.\n- **Your own**: Settings → Skills → **Add a skill**: a name, when to use it and the steps, one per line.\n- The skills that fit what you ask (by the words of their name, when and keywords) go with your message, and the\n  tools their steps name join a small model's short list of tools, so even a small model follows steps that worked.\n  Settings → Skills (also *AI Toolkit → Skills*) shows each skill's steps and how often it was used, turns any of them\n  off, and edits or deletes yours and the learned ones."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › AI Toolkit: actions it runs for you","x":"*AI Toolkit* (in every edition, Essentials included) holds ready-made actions Fabrix runs for you. Each is a command for\nthis computer (PowerShell on Windows, the shell on macOS and Linux) that Fabrix calls in one step, so even a small model\nanswers \"what is my IP address?\" at once and the same way every time.\n\n- **FBRX's library**, in toolkits:\n  - *Networking*: My IP address, Network adapters, Full network details, DNS servers in use, Look up a name, Routing\n    table, Trace the route, Devices this computer has talked to, Open connections, Wi-Fi connection, Flush the DNS\n    cache, Get a fresh address (DHCP), Set a fixed address, Use an automatic address (DHCP).\n  - *This computer*: About this computer, Time since the last restart, Disk space, Busiest programs.\n  - *Troubleshooting*: Check the internet (the router, then the internet past it, then names), Is a port open.\n- **Built by Fabrix**: when no action fits something you could well ask again and one command does it, Fabrix builds a\n  new action (`toolkit.build`) and runs it. FBRX asks you first, showing the command, so an action is kept only when you\n  approve it. Fabrix's actions that only look run straight away next time; one whose command could change anything\n  asks before each run.\n- **Your own**: **New action**: a name, the toolkit it goes in, when to use it, and the command for each system.\n\n**Make it do it your way.** Every action can be changed: find it (the search looks in names and commands), click the\npencil, and change the command. Fabrix runs it your way from then on. For example, if the action Fabrix built for your\nIP address runs `ipconfig /all` and tells you more than you want, change it to plain `ipconfig`. A library action you\nchanged says\n*Changed by you*, and **Reset to FBRX's** puts the original back. **Try** runs an action right there and shows what it\nprinted. The switch turns an action off: Fabrix stops using it until you turn it back on.\n\n- **Inputs**: write `{{name}}` in a command where something goes each time (a host, an adapter), and add an input of\n  that name: a host or address, an IPv4 address, a number, a name or some text. FBRX checks each value and puts it in\n  quoted, so a value can never become a command of its own.\n- **Ask me before each run**: on for anything that changes settings, files or services. Off only for commands that\n  just look. Either way your policy (Governance) and the guardian check every run, and each run is in the audit log.\n- **Needs administrator rights**: Windows asks through its usual prompt (UAC); macOS and Linux ask for an\n  administrator's password.\n- **Put it back unless I keep the change**: for a change that could cut you off, such as *Set a fixed address*. After\n  it runs, FBRX asks whether to keep it, and runs the \"put it back\" command by itself when you say no or do not\n  answer in time (60 seconds unless you change it). A wrong address can never leave you stranded.\n\nThe actions that fit what you ask go with your message, and Fabrix prefers them to writing a command of its own.\nChanging actions is for the person at the computer: other apps on the Local API can run actions (through governance)\nbut cannot change them."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Attach files and pictures","x":"The paperclip next to the chat box (or drag files onto the chat, or paste a picture) attaches up to 10 files to a\nmessage, 25 MB each.\n\n| What | What Fabrix gets |\n| --- | --- |\n| Text and code (`.txt`, `.md`, `.csv`, `.json`, source files…) | The text itself. |\n| PDF | The text of every page (a scan without text has none: say what is in it, or attach a picture). |\n| Documents (`.docx`, `.xlsx`, `.pptx`, and OpenDocument `.odt`, `.ods`, `.odp`) | Their text: paragraphs, cells row by row, slides in order. |\n| Pictures (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP) | The picture, for models that see: cloud models, and local ones with *vision* or *vl* in their name. Others get its name only, and the chat says so before you send. Big pictures are made smaller first (2000 pixels at most). |\n| Anything else | Its name and size; Fabrix can still open it with its file tools. |\n\nA long file is shortened to fit what the model reads at once (the chat says when). Attached files are kept in the\nworkspace (`Attachments`), where Fabrix's file tools reach them by the path the chat gives; ones never sent go after a\nday, and the rest go with their chat."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Documents and pictures Fabrix makes","x":"Ask for a file and Fabrix makes it: *\"make a PDF report of my open tasks\"*, *\"put that table in a spreadsheet\"*,\n*\"draw a diagram of my home network\"*.\n\n* **Documents:** PDF, `.docx` (for any word processor), web page (`.html`), Markdown, plain text and CSV, written from\n  Markdown: headings, lists, tables, links, quotes and code. PDFs are printed by the app's own engine, with page\n  numbers; on FBRX Server, FBRX writes them itself.\n* **Scenes:** *\"draw a mouse knight facing a giant by a castle\"* becomes a storybook picture made from FBRX's own art:\n  a backdrop (day, sunset, night, forest, sea, desert, snow, space, a castle hall), characters (a person, a giant, a\n  mouse, a cat, a dog, a bird, a dragon) that can wear a helmet, crown, wizard hat, cap or hood and hold a sword,\n  shield, staff, club, flag, lantern or torch, and scenery (trees, mountains, houses, castles, tents, a campfire, a\n  treasure chest, boats, cars, rockets, the sun, the moon, clouds, stars), each recolored and named as asked. Fabrix\n  only says what goes where; FBRX draws every piece, outlined and shaded, so even a small model on a laptop makes a\n  good picture.\n* **Pictures:** other drawings (charts, diagrams, icons, logos) are drawn on the computer, offline, as SVG and PNG.\n  Generated images (photos and paintings from a description) need an image service: an OpenAI-compatible\n  images endpoint, or an image server on your network (Settings → Agent → *Files, pictures and the web*).\n* **Where they go:** the FBRX folder in Documents (Settings → Agent; any folder the AI may use). A new file never\n  replaces one already there: *Report (2).pdf*. Each one shows in the chat with a picture of it, **Open** and **Show in\n  folder**. Making a new file in that folder needs no yes (the policy's *documents* and *pictures* rules)."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › The web: pages, search and quotes","x":"In an online chat (an offline one asks first), Fabrix can:\n\n* **read a page**: its title, text and links, or a PDF on the web (up to 15 MB);\n* **search the web**: a public search engine that needs no key, or your own SearXNG server for privacy (Settings →\n  Agent → *Files, pictures and the web*);\n* **look up a stock quote**: the latest price, the day's open, high, low and volume, from a free public source\n  (delayed; not for trading).\n\nAddresses on this computer and inside your network are refused (unless the policy allows private networks), and\nthe organization's blocked sites apply."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Automations","x":"Automations (Studio → **Automations**, Endpoint Pro and up) are jobs Fabrix does by itself and tells you about.\nEach one is a request, as you would type it in the chat, with:\n\n* **When:** every 15 minutes to 7 days, at a time on chosen days, when FBRX starts, or only when you run it.\n* **Tell me:** every time, only when something changed (Fabrix answers *NO_CHANGE* when nothing is new, and nobody is\n  told), or never (it stays in its chat).\n* **Internet:** whether it may read the web, search and look up quotes.\n* **Shortcut:** an optional key combination that runs it at once from any app.\n\nStart from an idea (watch classified listings, check my stocks, a price drop alert, a morning brief, news on a topic,\na weekly PDF report) and fill in the parts in [brackets]. Each automation keeps its own chat (marked *automation* in\nConversations): open it to read every result and ask follow-up questions. **History** lists its last runs.\n\nNobody is there to approve anything while one runs, so it uses only the tools the policy allows without asking; an\noffline one gets no internet tools at all. Automations run one at a time (a local model answers one question at a\ntime anyway), and a computer that slept runs each missed one once, not over and over. They are not in Endpoint\nEssentials, nor on student computers."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › FBRX Snapshot","x":"A picture of the screen (Workbench → **Snapshot**, or **Ctrl+Alt+S** from any app; Settings there can make the\n**Print Screen** key take one too, or change the shortcut):\n\n1. Every screen is captured at once, so an open menu stays as it was. Then pick: **Area** (drag over it; the size in\n   pixels shows as you go), **Window** (from a list of the open windows, taken at its own size), or **Whole screen**.\n   Esc cancels. From the Snapshot page you can wait 3, 5 or 10 seconds first.\n2. It is saved as a PNG in Pictures → *FBRX Snapshots* (changeable), copied to the clipboard, and opened to mark up\n   (or only announced, as you prefer).\n3. Mark it up: **pen**, **highlighter**, **arrow**, **box**, **text**, **blur** (squares big enough that nothing under\n   them can be read back: for passwords and account numbers) and **crop**, in eight colors and three sizes, with undo and\n   redo. Keys: P, H, A, R, T, B, C for the tools; Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y; Ctrl+C copies; Ctrl+S saves.\n4. Then **Copy**, **Save** (over the snapshot), **Save a copy**, **Show in folder**, **Delete**, or **Ask Fabrix**: a new\n   chat with the picture attached, ready for your question (\"what does this error mean?\").\n5. Or press **Drag out** and drop the picture straight into another app (a chat, an email, a document, a folder), marks\n   and crop included, saved or not. Snapshots in the list drag out the same way.\n\nOn a Mac, the first snapshot asks for **Screen Recording** permission (System Settings → Privacy & Security). On Linux\nwith Wayland, the system may ask which screen to share. Snapshot is in Endpoint Pro and up."},{"d":"docs/FABRIX.md","t":"Fabrix at work › Fabrix on FBRX Server","x":"FBRX Server's console has a **Fabrix** page too: a chat with the server's own AI, which sets itself up with the best\nmodel the server runs well. See [SERVER.md](SERVER.md#fabrix-in-the-console)."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate","x":"**FBRX Gate** turns an FBRX Server into the router and firewall of your network: the internet on one port, your\nnetworks on the others (VLANs on one cable, or a port each), addresses and names for your devices, a VPN for your\nphone and laptop, traffic priority, and **Prefer Mesh**, a fast lane for FBRX computers whose AI helps each other.\n\nIt is the **gate** role of FBRX Server ([SERVER.md](SERVER.md)), managed from the same web console (the *Gate* pages)\nor from a router-style command line on the server (`fbrx-gate`). Add **FBRX MiniDome** ([MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md)) to\nwatch the network through it.\n\n> First stage (FBRX Gate 0.1): IPv4 routing with one internet connection. It is tested as a real router in Linux\n> network namespaces in CI. IPv6 routing, several internet connections and high availability come later."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › How it works: edit, review, commit","x":"Everything the gate does is **one configuration**. You never change the network directly:\n\n1. **Edit** — the console's pages (or `fbrx-gate set …`) change a *candidate* copy. Nothing on the network changes yet;\n   a banner across the console counts the changes waiting.\n2. **Review** — *Changes* lists every difference from what runs, the problems to fix first (a DHCP range outside its\n   network, two networks overlapping, a VLAN bigger than its port, a rule about a network that does not exist…) and\n   things worth a look. *Preview the files* shows exactly what it becomes: the nftables table, dnsmasq's settings, the\n   systemd-networkd files and the traffic-shaping commands.\n3. **Commit** — checked again (`nft -c`, `dnsmasq --test`), then applied. If applying fails, the previous configuration\n   is put back at once and nothing changed.\n\n**What if?** Before you commit, *Changes* works out what the change would do to traffic, from the firewall itself and\nbefore anything changes on the network:\n\n- **Every kind of connection**: each network to the internet, to the other networks and to the gate (this console,\n  SSH, DNS, DHCP), the VPN, and the internet to the console and to each port forward, now and after the change, with\n  the reason (the rule that decides it, or the gate's default). What changes comes first: cut off, then newly opened.\n- **You**: whether you can still reach this console from where you are. When a change would cut you off, it says so in\n  red, and committing asks again (on trial, the old configuration comes back by itself if you cannot reach the console\n  to keep it).\n- **What really happened**: the connections the gate saw in the last hour (FBRX MiniDome keeps them) replayed against\n  the change: \"56 of 56 recent connections would be cut off\" is a change to look at again.\n- **Ask the AI** (with the ai role): this server's AI reads all of the above and explains the change, what could go\n  wrong, and whether to commit it on trial or change something first.\n\n**Trial commits** (on by default, 5 minutes): the commit undoes itself unless you press **Keep it** in time. If a change\ncuts you off (a wrong port, a rule that blocks the console), wait: the old configuration comes back by itself. A gate\nthat restarts during a trial also goes back. Every commit is kept; *Changes → History* goes back to any of them (as a\nnew commit, on trial too)."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Install","x":"On FBRX Server (the ISO installs the virtual and ai roles; add the gate afterwards):\n\n```bash\nsudo /opt/fbrx-installer/install.sh --roles virtual,ai,gate --gate-wan eno1 --gate-lan eno2\n# or, a dedicated gate with MiniDome:\nsudo bash fbrx-server-1.0.0/install.sh --roles gate,minidome,ai --gate-wan eno1 --gate-lan eno2\n```\n\n| Option | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--gate-wan ` | The port your modem or provider's box plugs into. |\n| `--gate-lan ` | The port to your network. It becomes **192.168.1.1/24** and hands out 192.168.1.100–199, unless the internet side already uses 192.168.1.x (a home router in front of the gate, say): then the next free one of 192.168.10.1, 192.168.50.1, 192.168.88.1, 10.10.0.1 and 172.20.0.1. The installer says which. |\n| `--gate-lan-address ` | Your network's addresses, chosen yourself (`192.168.50.1/24`). |\n| `--gate-wan-address ` `--gate-wan-gateway ` | A fixed internet address (`203.0.113.2/29` via `203.0.113.1`). When the internet port already has a fixed address in `/etc/network/interfaces`, the gate keeps it without these. Otherwise DHCP from the modem or router. |\n| `--gate-manage ` | A private network on the internet side that may still reach the console and SSH. By default the network the internet port is on during the install (if private), so installing over it does not shut you out. |\n| `--reboot` | Restart at the end when the takeover waits for it (run interactively, the installer asks instead). |\n\nThe installer adds nftables, dnsmasq, WireGuard tools and systemd-networkd; moves the two ports from the system's own\nnetwork settings (`/etc/network/interfaces`, kept as `interfaces.fbrx-backup`) to systemd-networkd; loads the firewall\nbefore the network comes up at every boot (`fbrx-gate-firewall.service`); points the server's own name lookups at the\ngate; switches systemd-networkd, dnsmasq and the firewall service on for every start; and, at the first start, commits\nthe **starter configuration**: the internet by DHCP (or the fixed address), your network on the LAN port, nothing\nreachable from the internet. On a running server the takeover waits for a restart (the installer offers it).\n\nThen plug a computer into the LAN port: it gets an address. Open `https://:9443` (192.168.1.1, or\nthe one the installer named; the server's login screen shows it) and sign in with the setup code\n(`sudo fbrx-server setup-code`)."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Install › When something does not work","x":"```bash\nsudo fbrx-server doctor          # every service the roles need: on for the next start, and running now\nsudo fbrx-server doctor --fix    # switches on and starts whatever is off\nsudo fbrx-gate status            # the gate's own view, problems last\n```\n\nEvery commit also switches systemd-networkd, dnsmasq and the firewall service on, so a gate keeps working after a\nrestart. The problems `fbrx-gate status` (and the Gate page) name, with what to do:\n\n| Problem | |\n| --- | --- |\n| *The internet port got 192.168.1.37, inside your network lan* | The router in front of the gate hands out the same addresses as your network, so nothing gets through. `sudo fbrx-gate renumber lan 192.168.10.1/24` (or change the address under Networks), then `sudo fbrx-gate commit`. Devices get new addresses as their leases renew (restart them to hurry it). |\n| *DHCP and DNS (dnsmasq) are not running* / *systemd-networkd is not running* | `sudo fbrx-server doctor --fix`; `journalctl -u dnsmasq` says why it stopped. |\n| */etc/network/interfaces still sets up eno1* | The system's own network settings and the gate fight over the port. Put a `#` before its lines (auto, iface, address…) and restart. |\n| *The internet port has no address yet* | Cable, modem, or the provider's DHCP; for a fixed address `sudo fbrx-gate wan static  `. |"},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › The console","x":"| Page | |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Gate** | The internet address, traffic now (and a chart while the page is open), devices, connections, ports, what is switched on, the last commit. |\n| **Networks & VLANs** | The internet port (DHCP or fixed), networks and the ports and VLANs they are on. |\n| **Firewall** | What each network may reach, rules (top to bottom, first match wins) with how often each matched, port forwards. |\n| **DNS & VPN** | Upstream servers, your own domain and names, blocking lists, the question log; the VPN and its devices. |\n| **Traffic & Prefer Mesh** | Fair sharing of the internet line and the priority lane. |\n| **Changes** | Review, commit (with a trial), throw edits away, edit as text, preview, history and going back. |\n\nViewers see everything; administrators change it."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Networks and VLANs","x":"A **network** has a kind, an address for the gate (its size gives the range), what it may **reach**, whether its\ndevices may **manage the gate** (console and SSH), and DHCP with reserved addresses.\n\n| Kind | Reaches | Manages the gate | |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Your network | Everything | Yes | Your own computers. |\n| Guests | The internet only | No | Visitors, kept away from your devices. |\n| Mesh | Everything | Yes | FBRX computers and servers helping each other: Prefer Mesh, jumbo frames where possible. |\n| Servers | Everything | Yes | |\n| Smart devices | The internet only | No | Cameras, TVs, plugs. |\n| Management | Everything | Yes | Switches, iDRACs, access points. |\n\n*Reaches* means: **everything** (the internet and your other networks), **the internet only**, or **nothing outside**\n(only each other, and the gate for addresses and names). Nothing reaches in from the internet unless you forward a\nport; firewall rules add exceptions either way.\n\n**VLANs** carry several networks on one cable: *New network → A VLAN on a port* picks a tag (guests 20, mesh 30, …);\nset the same tag on your switch's ports. A VLAN on the internet port works but is flagged.\n\n**Jumbo frames** (MTU 9000) suit a mesh or storage network whose switch and computers all take them. A VLAN's port must\nbe at least as large, and an untagged network on that port would get jumbo frames too, so the console only offers them\nby default where that cannot happen and says why otherwise."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Networks and VLANs › Virtual machines on your networks","x":"On a server that is both the gate and a hypervisor (the **virtual** role), virtual machines can sit on any of the gate's\nnetworks like computers plugged into its port: turn on **Virtual machines can join this network** for it (your LAN,\na server VLAN, an IoT VLAN…) and commit. The network becomes a **switch**, a Linux bridge called `br-`\n(`br-lan`), holding the port or VLAN, the gate's address, its DHCP and its firewall zone. Any number of machines plug\ninto it and share the port with the server, the way virtual switches on other hypervisors do.\n\n* Machines get addresses from the gate (reserve one like for any device), resolve names through it, and follow that\n  network's rules: *reaches*, *manages the gate*, firewall rules, Prefer Mesh.\n* They see the other devices on the network directly, so a controller for your access points or cameras finds them.\n* VLANs riding on a switched port keep working; a VLAN can be a switch of its own (`br-iot`).\n* A new gate on a hypervisor starts with its LAN as a switch; new networks offer it too.\n* In FBRX Virtual: pick the switch when creating a machine, or move one under *its Settings → Network* (it keeps its\n  MAC address, so a reservation follows it). *Networks* shows every switch, where it leads and who is plugged in.\n\nThe switch keeps the port's MAC address, so devices on the network do not notice the change and machines joining it\nnever alter the gate's address. Turning a switch on or off briefly drops the network while the address moves (the\ntrial commit has you covered).\nFrom the command line: `sudo fbrx-gate set networks lan vmSwitch true`, then `sudo fbrx-gate commit confirmed 5`."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Firewall","x":"The **basics** come from each network's *reaches* and *manages the gate*. **Rules** go between zones: a network's name,\n`wan` (the internet), `vpn`, `gate` (the gate itself) or `any`, with TCP/UDP ports, optional source and destination\naddresses, and *allow*, *refuse* (the sender is told) or *block quietly*. Ready-made rules: printing, screen casting,\nfile sharing, keeping a network out. **Port forwards** send a port on the internet side to one device inside (optionally\nonly from one address on the internet). Each rule and forward shows how often it matched; the *basics* card counts\nwhat was turned away.\n\nThe firewall is one nftables table, `inet fbrx_gate`, replaced in one step at each commit. It filters only the ports\nthe gate runs, so FBRX Virtual's own networks keep working next to it."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › DNS","x":"The gate answers its networks' questions (dnsmasq), asks your **upstream** servers for the rest, knows your devices by\nname (`laptop.lan`, and the names you add), and can **block** domains and blocklists (ads, trackers). The **question\nlog** (on by default) is what FBRX MiniDome reads; it is rotated daily and kept a week."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › VPN","x":"Your phone or laptop reaches home from anywhere (uses WireGuard). *DNS & VPN → Add a device*: the gate makes the\ndevice's keys and settings and shows them **once**, as a QR code for the WireGuard app and as a settings file. The\ndevice's private key is not kept anywhere. Choose whether everything goes through home (safer on public Wi-Fi) or only\nyour networks. Behind another router, forward UDP 51820 to the gate. On an FBRX computer, paste the settings into\n**FBRX VPN** ([VPN.md](VPN.md)): it connects from FBRX, and the gate lets a device with full access reach its console,\nSSH and FBRX Mesh (the server's own AI), as it does for the networks that may manage it."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › Traffic priority and Prefer Mesh","x":"**Traffic priority** shares the internet line fairly: give your upload (and optionally download) speed a little under\nwhat the line really does, and the gate shapes it with four lanes by DSCP mark (cake; HTB with three lanes on kernels\nwithout cake): voice, video, best effort, bulk. A big upload no longer drowns out calls.\n\n**Prefer Mesh** puts traffic between FBRX computers first, so their AI helping each other (Mesh Assist) does not wait\nbehind a backup or a download:\n\n* **Marked**: FBRX Mesh traffic (TCP port 47800) through the gate, and everything from the networks you pick (a mesh\n  VLAN), gets the DSCP mark you choose (AF41 by default: the video lane, below voice calls). The gate counts what it\n  marked.\n* **At both ends**: FBRX computers mark what they send too (*Mesh & phone → Network* in FBRX Endpoint; *Mesh & AI →\n  Network* on a server), and reach each other through the mesh network first. See [MESH.md](MESH.md#prefer-mesh).\n* **TCP, kept open**: mesh connections are TCP, kept alive and sent without delay, so help between computers starts\n  without a new handshake each time.\n* **Jumbo frames** on the mesh VLAN where every switch and computer takes them.\n* **Your switches**: let them trust DSCP on the mesh VLAN's ports (or give that VLAN a higher queue) so the priority\n  holds inside your network too."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › fbrx-gate","x":"The same configuration from the server's command line, as root (`sudo fbrx-gate …`). Paths are words: list items by\nname.\n\n```\nfbrx-gate status                         ports, the internet side, devices, problems\nfbrx-gate show [running] [path…]         the configuration being edited (or what runs), or part of it\nfbrx-gate set networks guest access internet\nfbrx-gate set interfaces eno2.30 mtu 9000\nfbrx-gate add firewall rules '{\"id\":\"ssh-lan\",\"name\":\"SSH from the LAN\",…}'\nfbrx-gate delete firewall forwards web\nfbrx-gate edit                           the whole configuration in $EDITOR\nfbrx-gate check                          problems and warnings\nfbrx-gate compare                        what a commit would change\nfbrx-gate commit confirmed 5 comment Guest VLAN\nfbrx-gate confirm                        keep it\nfbrx-gate rollback now                   undo the commit on trial\nfbrx-gate rollback 12                    go back to commit 12\nfbrx-gate renumber lan 192.168.50.1/24   move a network: its range, reservations, forwards, rules and names follow\nfbrx-gate wan static 203.0.113.2/29 203.0.113.1    a fixed internet address (wan dhcp: back to DHCP)\nfbrx-gate reset                          throw the edits away\nfbrx-gate history | leases | preview nftables|dnsmasq|networkd\n```\n\nIt signs in with a token only root can read, accepted only from the gate itself."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › On disk","x":"| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/var/lib/fbrx-virtual/gate.db` | The candidate, every commit with its configuration |\n| `/etc/fbrx-gate/gate.nft` | The firewall, loaded at boot by `fbrx-gate-firewall.service` |\n| `/etc/fbrx-gate/wg-fbrx.key` | The gate's VPN key |\n| `/etc/systemd/network/10-fbrx-*` | Ports, VLANs, switches (`br-*`), addresses, the VPN interface |\n| `/etc/dnsmasq.d/fbrx-gate.conf` | DHCP and DNS |\n| `/var/lib/fbrx-gate/` | Leases, the downloaded blocklist |\n| `/var/log/fbrx-gate/dnsmasq.log` | The question log |\n\nAll of it is written by FBRX Gate from the configuration: change the configuration, not the files."},{"d":"docs/GATE.md","t":"FBRX Gate › API","x":"Under `/v1` on the console's port (see [SERVER.md](SERVER.md#api)):\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /gate`, `GET /gate/live`, `GET /gate/interfaces` | The state (running, candidate, changes, problems, trial), live status, the server's ports (viewers) |\n| `PUT /gate/candidate`, `POST /gate/candidate/reset`, `GET /gate/preview` | Edit, throw away, preview (administrators) |\n| `GET /gate/whatif`, `POST /gate/whatif/explain` | What the change being edited would do to traffic (viewers); the server's AI explains it (operators) |\n| `POST /gate/commit` `{ comment, confirmMinutes }`, `POST /gate/confirm`, `POST /gate/confirm/undo`, `POST /gate/rollback` `{ to, confirmMinutes }` | Commit, keep, undo the trial, go back |\n| `GET /gate/history`, `GET /gate/history/:id` | Commits |\n| `POST /gate/vpn/peers` `{ name, endpoint?, fullTunnel }` | A new VPN device: its settings and QR code, once |"},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home","x":"FBRX Command can run a family's computers as well as a company's or a school's. Pick **Home** as the kind of tenant\nand parents get FBRX Endpoint, children get **FBRX OS Home** (a simpler, safer computer with a learning helper), and\na child's \"Get help\" goes straight to a parent's computer."},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › What each computer gets","x":"| | Parents | Children |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Runs | FBRX Endpoint (up to Ultra, or Pro if you hold the group to it) | **FBRX OS Home** |\n| Agent | Fabrix with every tool, asking before it changes anything | A learning helper that only talks: no files, apps, websites or commands |\n| Pages | Everything the license gives | Home, the helper, Messages, Calendar, Tasks, Notes, Toolbox, Library, Settings |\n| Defaults | Yours to choose (profiles in FBRX Command) | Presenter-safe mode with a projector, chats offline first; easter eggs, the mesh and the local API locked off |\n| Talking | **Messages** with every computer in the family; with Ultra, the Help desk receives requests for help | **Messages** with the family |"},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › 1. Set up FBRX Command as a Home","x":"On the computer that will look after the family (one that stays on: a desktop, or a small home server), double-click\n**`Install FBRX Command`** in the FBRX folder (`.cmd` on Windows, `.command` on a Mac) and keep the default,\n*Computers on this network*. It installs FBRX Command, starts it whenever you log in, and opens the first-run page\nwith its setup token filled in: pick **Home**, then give the family's name and the parent's account. Give that\ncomputer a fixed address in your router so the children's computers can always find it. (A hosted setup with Docker\nand a domain works too: DEPLOYMENT.md.)\nAlready running FBRX Command for work? **Tenants → New tenant → Home** adds the family next to your other tenants.\n\nUnattended installs: `FBRX_CP_ORGANIZATION_KIND=home` with the other `FBRX_CP_*` bootstrap settings."},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › 2. Parents and Children in one click","x":"**Profiles & groups → Set up for a family** (or the step right after **New tenant**) creates two groups, each with an\nenrollment token and a provisioning file to download:\n\n* **Parents**: FBRX Endpoint; their computers receive the children's requests for help.\n* **Children**: FBRX OS Home with the learning helper.\n\nCopy the tokens when they are shown. **Deploy & enroll** makes more at any time."},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › 3. Put FBRX on the computers","x":"Install FBRX on each computer and, on its **Organization** page (or in the first-run setup), paste the FBRX Command\naddress and the token for its group. FBRX then shows FBRX Command's certificate fingerprint: check that it is the same\nas on **Deploy & enroll** and choose *It matches: trust and connect*. Or put the downloaded `fbrx-provision.json` next to the installer before running\nit. Using a Parents token on a child's computer? Tick **This computer is for a student or a child** and it becomes a\nchild's computer. Only FBRX Command can turn a child's computer back into a parent's."},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › 4. Talking to each other","x":"Every computer in the family has **Messages** in the sidebar: message one computer (\"Can I play for ten more\nminutes?\") or everyone at once (\"Pizza night at 6!\"). Messages arrive within a second, with a pop-up, and wait in\nFBRX Command for a computer that is off. A parent can also write to everyone from FBRX Command's **Messages** page, and\nturn messages off for the family under **Profiles & groups → Organization defaults**. See\n[MESSAGES.md](MESSAGES.md).\n\nThe help desk is there too, for computers on Endpoint Ultra: a parent's Ultra computer receives requests for help\nwithin a second, with a short summary of the computer, and can answer from FBRX Command's **Help desk** page as well.\nFrom a request, the parent's computer can also check how the child's computer is doing, ask its AI, or run a command\nthere; the child (or whoever is at it) says yes first, and again for each command. To stop a parent's computer\nreceiving requests, open it under **Devices → Configuration** and turn off *Receives the family's requests for help*.\nOn Endpoint Essentials and Pro and on FBRX OS Home, Messages takes its place."},{"d":"docs/HOME.md","t":"FBRX at home › Children and safety","x":"* The learning helper on FBRX OS Home keeps answers short, kind and age-appropriate, teaches rather than doing\n  homework for the child, never asks for personal details, and has no tools at all: it cannot open files, apps or\n  websites, and FBRX refuses any tool call it attempts.\n* If a child writes that they might hurt themselves or that someone is hurting them, the helper answers with care and\n  tells them to talk to a parent or another adult they trust right away, 911 in an emergency, and **988** (call or\n  text, the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). FBRX Command gets a **Child safety** alert naming the computer and the kind of\n  concern, so a parent can check in. **The child's message is not sent**; it stays on that computer.\n* Conversations stay on the child's computer. FBRX Command sees health, version and help requests, not chats or files.\n* Choose which AI model the children's computers use in a profile for the Children group (a local model keeps\n  everything on the computer).\n\nFBRX is a helper, not a babysitter: keep talking with your children about what they do online, and use your\noperating system's own family settings (screen time, app limits, content filters) alongside it."},{"d":"docs/INSTALL.md","t":"Installing FBRX","x":"FBRX OS for Windows, Mac and Linux is downloaded from the **FBRX website** (*Download*), never from an app store, and\nkeeps itself up to date (*Settings → Updates*). The FBRX app for iPhone, iPad and Android is the one part in the app\nstores: it pairs with your computer ([MESH.md](MESH.md))."},{"d":"docs/INSTALL.md","t":"Installing FBRX › What to download","x":"| Download | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `FBRX-OS-Setup-.exe` (Windows), `FBRX-OS--arm64.dmg` and `-x64.dmg` (Mac: Apple silicon and Intel), `FBRX-OS--x86_64.AppImage` and `-amd64.deb` (Linux) | **The installer.** Double-click it: there is no terminal and nothing to build, and FBRX OS opens straight into a chat with your own AI, downloading it while you watch. It starts on Endpoint Essentials (free); sign in to your FBRX account, or join your organization's FBRX Command, for more. |\n| `FBRX-OS.zip` | The whole project, to build your own copy with your own license signing key (below). The link on the website always gives the newest version. |\n| `fbrx-server--amd64.iso`, `fbrx-server-.tar.gz` | FBRX Server: the installer ISO, and the bundle for a Debian 13 server you already have ([SERVER.md](SERVER.md)). |"},{"d":"docs/INSTALL.md","t":"Installing FBRX › Building your own copy","x":"Unzip `FBRX-OS.zip` somewhere permanent and outside synced folders (for example `~/FBRX` or `C:\\FBRX`), then\ndouble-click the installer for your computer. On Windows, right-click the zip and choose **Extract All…**;\ndouble-clicking `Install FBRX OS.cmd` inside the zip without extracting it also works: setup finds the zip in your\nDownloads folder, offers to unzip it to `%USERPROFILE%\\FBRX` (or a folder you type) and continues from there.\n\n| Computer | Double-click | If the computer warns you about a downloaded file |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mac | **`Install FBRX OS.command`** | System Settings → Privacy & Security → **Open Anyway**. Or open Terminal, type `bash ` and drag the file in, then press Enter |\n| Windows | **`Install FBRX OS.cmd`** | **More info → Run anyway** |\n\nThe installer does everything, in about 15–20 minutes the first time (about 2 GB of downloads), and when it is done\nFBRX OS opens straight into a chat with your own AI, running on your computer:\n\n1. downloads a private copy of Node.js if this computer doesn't have a recent one (nothing is installed system-wide),\n2. downloads what FBRX OS is made of,\n3. builds FBRX OS for this exact machine (Apple silicon or Intel, x64 or ARM), with the AI runtime inside, and creates\n   your license signing key (in `.fbrx-keys/`, with a copy in `~/.fbrx-keys`), which you should **back up**: it signs\n   every license you issue,\n4. gets your AI ready: the starter model (about 1 GB) downloads meanwhile into FBRX OS's data folder (only on a\n   computer that doesn't have FBRX OS yet),\n5. installs it in Applications (Mac) or for your user with Start menu and desktop shortcuts (Windows), with an\n   Ultra license in your name that activates by itself, and opens the app.\n\nTo update later, choose **Update now** when FBRX OS offers a new version (*Settings → Updates*), or download the new\nversion into the same place and run the installer again; your data, settings and keys are kept, and a running FBRX OS\nis closed for you. Options: `--name \"Acme Ltd\"` (the name on your license), `--no-launch`, `--no-ai` (leave the model\nto the app), `--allow-downgrade`, `--signing-key ` (below). The full log is in `.fbrx-setup/setup.log`.\n\n**Removing it.** On a Mac or Linux, *Settings → Updates → Uninstall FBRX OS*, or run the installer with `--uninstall`\n(on Linux, also right-click FBRX OS in your apps or dock → *Uninstall FBRX OS…*). It opens in a terminal window and\nasks first, closes FBRX OS, and removes the app (on Linux its entry in your apps and the build in the FBRX folder).\nYour data (chats, notes, settings, the vault) stays for a later install; `--uninstall --delete-data` erases it too.\nWindows removes it in *Settings → Apps*, or with its Setup file.\n\n**On Ubuntu and other Linux.** Installed from the FBRX folder, *Update now* runs the installer in a terminal window and\nFBRX OS opens again on the new version. Installed from the website instead: the AppImage updates itself, and a newer\n`.deb` installs over the old one with `sudo apt install ./FBRX-OS--amd64.deb`. To remove that one, use\nUbuntu's App Center, or `sudo apt remove` with the name `dpkg -l | grep -i fbrx` shows; your data in `~/.config/FBRX OS`\nstays.\n\n**With FBRX Command on another computer.** FBRX OS accepts the editions FBRX Command gives (to the organization, or to\nFBRX accounts on it) only when it was built with FBRX Command's license signing key. On one computer the two installers\nshare it by themselves. For FBRX OS on another computer, copy `~/.fbrx-keys/license-signing.pem` from the FBRX Command\ncomputer and run `Install FBRX OS` with `--signing-key `; the keys it replaces are kept beside it, dated.\nUntil then that computer runs Pro, and *Settings → Edition* says why."},{"d":"docs/INSTALL.md","t":"Installing FBRX › Giving FBRX OS to someone else","x":"For someone who isn't technical, send the ready-made installer from the website. They double-click it, and FBRX OS\ndownloads its AI when it first opens, showing the progress in the chat.\n\nOn Windows you can also make one for them: open a Command Prompt in your FBRX folder and run\n`\"Install FBRX OS.cmd\" --share`. Enter their name to build a trial license into the installer (30 days by default:\nUltra, then the free features), or leave it blank to send an update to someone who is already\nset up (`--for \"Alex\" --days 30 --yes` skips the questions). The result is one file in the `Share` folder, for example\n`Share\\FBRX-OS-Setup-2.3.2-for-Alex.exe`, plus a short *How to install* note to send along.\n\nOn their PC they double-click it. A new install opens FBRX OS straight to the chat while its AI downloads. If FBRX OS\nis already there, Setup opens on a choice instead:\n\n* **Upgrade** (when the file is newer): installs it and keeps all their data, settings, license and paired devices,\n* **Repair**: reinstalls from scratch and clears cached files, for an app that will not start or looks wrong,\n* **Uninstall**: removes FBRX OS, keeping their data unless they tick *Also delete my data*.\n\nIf FBRX OS is open, Setup asks it to quit properly first (and ends anything left over, such as the local AI runtime).\nAn older file than the installed version offers a downgrade, with a warning. The command-line switches for IT tools:\n[DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#3-the-windows-installer-upgrade-repair-and-uninstall)."},{"d":"docs/INSTALL.md","t":"Installing FBRX › FBRX Command on a computer of your own","x":"Next to the FBRX OS installers in `FBRX-OS.zip` are **`Install FBRX Command.command`** (Mac) and **`Install FBRX\nCommand.cmd`** (Windows). Double-click it on the computer that will run FBRX Command for your company, school or family\n(one that stays on, with a fixed address in your router). In about five minutes it:\n\n1. builds FBRX Command and installs it with its own copy of Node.js,\n2. gives it the same license signing key as `Install FBRX OS` on this computer, so the licenses it issues work on\n   your computers (FBRX OS built elsewhere needs `--signing-key`, above),\n3. starts it now and every time you log in, with an **FBRX Command** icon in the Start menu / Applications,\n4. opens the first-run page (Work, School or Home) with its one-time setup token already filled in.\n\nIt asks who will connect. **Computers on this network** (the default) serves `https://:8787` with\nFBRX Command's own certificate: computers check its fingerprint once when they join (FBRX shows it; FBRX Command →\nDeploy & enroll shows the same one) and then trust nothing else, so no domain name is needed. **Only this computer**\nis for trying it out. Run it again to update (data kept), or with `--uninstall` to remove it (`--delete-data` to\nerase everything). Options and details: [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#fbrx-command-on-a-computer-of-your-own)."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses","x":"What each FBRX edition includes, what it costs, and how licenses decide what a computer may run. An **FBRX Command**\n(FBRX's in the cloud, or one an organization runs for itself) keeps each organization as a **tenant** with its own\ndevices, users, configuration, credentials, plugins, backups, webhooks and audit trail, and signs the licenses its\ncomputers run under."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › Products: FBRX Endpoint Essentials, Pro, Ultra and Black Hole Edition","x":"The desktop app is **FBRX Endpoint**, in four editions (see COMMAND_CENTER.md for what each shows):\n\n* **FBRX Endpoint Essentials** (free) — where every new computer starts: the AI on its own (the chat, the AI models\n  and Settings, where it works Quick, Everyday or Thorough), Governance (what the AI may do, and the audit log) and a\n  walkthrough. It joins an organization's FBRX Command for free.\n* **FBRX Endpoint Pro** (`basic` in licenses and the API) — the everyday tools, the AI's work budget part by part\n  (steps, answer length, how much it keeps in mind) and unloading the local model when idle. Each computer on its own\n  may try it free for 7 days, once (`STANDARD_TRIAL_DAYS`).\n* **FBRX Endpoint Ultra** — expert tool sets (Terminal, FBRX/1, virtual lab, disks and partitions, Defender settings,\n  adapters, device consoles, developer tools, the Local API), Mesh and AI coordination, connections, tools and\n  plugins, the theme studio with its gradients and textures, deep space maps with duplicate files and\n  what grew, and the Wi-Fi channel planner's history and band-by-band advice.\n* **FBRX Endpoint Black Hole Edition** (`blackhole`) — everything: Ultra plus the whole Library (The Lab and the FBRX command\n  library), network environments in Network Center and the FBRX Server page. For the people who run\n  an organization's FBRX Command and its FBRX Servers, and only through FBRX Command.\n\n**Governance** comes with every edition: what the AI may do on its own and what it asks first, the folders it may\nuse, and the audit log, so whoever uses the computer stays in charge of its AI. (A second AI model reviewing risky\ncalls, the guardian model, is part of Black Hole Edition.)\n\nThe easter eggs come with every edition, once their secret key unlocks them.\n\nThe person chooses the edition; what may be chosen is decided above them. A license's **edition** is the most it allows:\nPro, Ultra or Black Hole Edition (`tier: \"basic\"`, `\"ultra\"` or `\"blackhole\"` in the key). **On its own**, a computer runs Essentials unless something was bought: a license key\n(or a purchase on the person's FBRX account) goes up to its tier, at most Ultra. A purchase on an account covers as many\nof the person's computers as its edition includes, and the further ones they add (below): each signed-in computer gets\na key of its own, for that install only, in the order they first signed in, and one past them runs Essentials until\nanother signs out or the person adds a computer (ACCOUNTS.md). A license that names an FBRX Command\ntenant belongs to that organization and counts only there. **In an organization**, a computer goes up to its\nlicense's tier, Black Hole Edition included, held lower by its group or by the computer itself in FBRX Command (*Essentials\nonly*, *Up to Pro*, *Up to Ultra*, *Up to Black Hole Edition*). The tier and the features below are separate: features\ngate services in the runtime, the tier decides how far the product the user sees can go."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › Prices","x":"One list, used by the app, FBRX Command, the website and billing. US dollars per\nmonth; the product ids are the Stripe price lookup keys and the App Store / Google Play subscription ids. These are\nwhere the prices start: FBRX Command's operator can change them in **Pricing** (below), and organizations' bills and\nStripe follow.\n\n| | On its own | Through an FBRX Command tenant | Product ids |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Essentials** | Free | Free | — |\n| **Pro** | $4.99 | $3.99 per computer (20% off) | `fbrx_standard_monthly`, `fbrx_standard_tenant_monthly` |\n| **Ultra** | $11.99 | $9.59 per computer (20% off) | `fbrx_ultra_monthly`, `fbrx_ultra_tenant_monthly` |\n| **Black Hole Edition** | — | $24.99 per computer (full price, an add-on) | `fbrx_blackhole_tenant_monthly` |\n| **FBRX Command tenant** | — | $54.99 | `fbrx_command_tenant_monthly` |\n| **MDM add-on** (not sold yet) | — | $4 per managed computer, whatever its edition (Essentials included); the operator sets it | `fbrx_mdm_tenant_monthly` |\n| **FBRX Server** (coming soon) | Free while in beta; not on sale yet | The same | `fbrx_server_iso` |\n\n**The MDM add-on** (device management: patching, remote help, device security) is one price per managed computer a\nmonth, the same whatever edition the computer runs. FBRX Command's operator sets that price and puts it on sale\n(**Pricing**); until then nothing is charged for it. An organization turns it on for a group's computers (**Profiles &\ngroups → a group → Device management**), and its bill gets a line for them: each computer in such a group at the\nadd-on's price, on top of its edition. Stripe charges what its price under `fbrx_mdm_tenant_monthly` says; the Pricing\npage checks that it agrees with the price set there, and shows what the add-on earns after card fees and the cost of\nlooking after a managed computer.\n\n**The operator's price list** (FBRX Command → **Pricing**) sets:\n\n* Pro and Ultra on their own (set the same in App Store Connect and the Google Play Console, which charge their own);\n* the **computers each edition includes** (2 for Pro, 3 for Ultra, 5 for Black Hole Edition to start; Essentials is\n  free on every computer and counts none) and the price of each **further computer** a month, never discounted (`fbrx_standard_extra_computer_monthly`, `fbrx_ultra_…`,\n  `fbrx_blackhole_…`), for a person on their own and in an organization alike. On their own, people add computers to\n  a plan bought on the website or in FBRX; the App Store and Google Play sell none;\n* the **organization fee**, never discounted: one for Work and School organizations, and optionally another for larger\n  ones (from so many computers, Essentials included, `fbrx_command_large_monthly`) and for Home ones\n  (`fbrx_command_home_monthly`);\n* each **person's price in an organization** (Pro, Ultra, Black Hole Edition; Essentials is free): a person pays once,\n  whatever number of the organization's computers they use, up to the computers their edition includes, and each\n  further computer on top. A paid computer nobody pays for (a\n  shared, lab or student computer, given its edition in Devices or Profiles & groups) takes a seat of its own at the\n  same price; student and child computers are billed as Pro;\n* the **volume discount**: up to five steps, each so much off every seat (people and shared computers) once an\n  organization has so many (the highest step reached counts). Stripe applies it as a coupon on the seats' prices,\n  never on the fee, extra computers or MDM;\n* **paying yearly**: what a year costs, in months' price (`yearlyMonths`: 10 to start, two months free; 12 for no\n  saving).\n\n**Monthly or yearly.** An organization pays monthly or yearly, one way for everything on its plan (Stripe bills a\nsubscription's items at one interval): its fee, its people, their extra computers and the MDM add-on. Paying yearly,\neach price is `yearlyMonths` months of the monthly one, under the same product id ending in `_yearly`\n(`fbrx_command_tenant_yearly`, `fbrx_ultra_tenant_yearly`, `fbrx_ultra_extra_computer_yearly`,\n`fbrx_mdm_tenant_yearly`, and so on). The volume discount comes off the same way; it is rounded on each line, so a year\ncan differ from ten months by a few cents. A person's own Pro or Ultra is monthly or yearly too, on the website\nand in FBRX (`fbrx_standard_yearly`, `fbrx_ultra_yearly`), with their extra computers at the same interval; the phone\napps sell them monthly.\n\nA tenant's bill is its organization fee, each person and shared computer at its edition's price less the volume\ndiscount, each extra computer, and the MDM add-on, for a month or a year as it pays; in the cloud,\nFBRX Command counts the people and their computers (a computer a member joined is theirs) and keeps Stripe in step\n(ACCOUNTS.md). Until the operator changes it, the list is the table above. The app and the website show the table\nabove; `GET /v1/plans` gives the operator's prices.\n\n**The Pro trial.** A computer on its own with nothing bought may ru"},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › Prices (2)","x":"n Pro free for `STANDARD_TRIAL_DAYS` (7)\ndays, once (`edition.startTrial`; Settings → Edition). Pro was free on a computer on its own until 2.0: a computer\nset up before starts its trial by itself the first time it starts 2.0. Then it runs Essentials unless Pro or\nUltra is bought. Computers in an organization have no trial.\n\n**Student and child computers** exist only in an organization's FBRX Command (the audience comes from it), run the\nlearner edition and are billed as Pro at the FBRX Command price ($3.99, 20% off).\n\n**School and Home licenses.** A license can also say what kind of tenant it is for: `vertical: \"education\"` (School)\nor `\"home\"` (Home); without it, it is a Work license. Issued from FBRX Command (*Kind of license*) or with\n`npm run license:issue -- … --vertical education` (or `home`), it also sets the tenant's kind, which you otherwise pick\nwhen the tenant is created. A School tenant starts staff computers in classroom mode, groups can hold teachers to\nPro, and student computers run **FBRX OS Education**, whatever the tier (see [EDUCATION.md](EDUCATION.md)). A Home\ntenant runs children's computers as **FBRX OS Home** (see [HOME.md](HOME.md)). A standalone computer that activates an Education key starts in classroom mode too.\n\n**FBRX Command** is the team tenant controller (fleet management with RMM-style tooling: configuration and policy,\nremote commands, credentials, packages, updates, backups, audit). A license issued there can carry the tenant's\naddress: when a computer activates that key it **joins the tenant by itself** and receives the\nproduct and settings the tenant chose for it (see *Joining FBRX Command* below)."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › What a key unlocks","x":"A key goes up to an edition, and carries the services behind the scenes that edition uses:\n\n| Service | Free (no key) | Pro | Ultra | Black Hole Edition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Built-in local AI agent (`agent.local`) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| REST / webhook / peer connectors (`connectors`) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Local automation API (`localapi`) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Cloud AI providers with your own key (`agent.cloud`) | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Third-party plugins (`plugins`) | | | ✔ | ✔ |\n| MCP connectors (`connectors.mcp`) | | | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Scheduled encrypted backups (`backup.scheduled`) | | | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Fleet management via the control plane (`fleet`) | | | | ✔ |\n| Model-assisted guardian review (`guardian.model`) | | | | ✔ |\n\nThat is a key bought on an FBRX account. Every key **FBRX Command** issues (and every key `npm run license:issue`\nsigns) carries all of them, whatever edition it goes up to: the edition decides what people see. Computers without a\nlicense run the free features (Essentials on their own; in an organization, up to Endpoint Pro)."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › License keys","x":"A key is `FBRX1..`: a JSON payload (license id, tenant, customer, edition, seats, extra\nfeatures, issue date, optional expiry, optional maximum major version, optional tier, optional FBRX Command tenant)\nsigned with Ed25519. Older apps ignore the optional fields they do not know.\n\n* **Signing** happens only on your control plane, with `FBRX_LICENSE_PRIVATE_KEY` (see\n  [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md#1-license-signing-keys)).\n* **Verification** happens offline on each workstation against the public key embedded at build time — no\n  phone-home needed, and nobody else can mint keys your builds accept.\n* **Expiry** — after `expiresAt` the workstation falls back to the free features (on its own, Essentials; data is\n  never locked away).\n* **Version cap** — `maxMajorVersion: 2` means the key works for 1.x and 2.x; 3.x needs a renewed key. Useful\n  for selling major upgrades.\n* **Seats** — enforced by the control plane at enrollment: when the tenant's active devices reach the seat count,\n  further enrollments are refused until a device is retired or the license is upgraded. `0` = seats are not counted.\n* **Fleet** — a tenant whose active license lacks `fleet` (an older key) cannot enroll new devices; its keys are meant\n  for offline activation. Tenants with no license at all may enroll devices, which then run the free features, up to\n  Pro — handy for trials and internal use."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › Issuing and delivering licenses","x":"1. **Tenants → New tenant** for the customer (name, contact, default update channel).\n2. Switch to the tenant, open **Licenses → Issue license**: the edition it goes up to (Black Hole Edition, Ultra or\n   Pro), whether computers that activate the key join this tenant automatically (on by default), seats, expiry and\n   version cap.\n3. Delivery:\n   * **Managed (recommended)** — workstations enrolled in that tenant receive the license automatically with their\n     configuration. Revoking it in the console removes it from every device on its next sync.\n   * **Offline** — copy the key from the console and paste it in the desktop app under\n     **Settings → License** (for customers who do not connect to a control plane). Offline keys cannot be revoked\n     remotely; use expiry dates.\n   * **File drop** — save the key as `fbrx-license.key` in the app's data folder\n     (`~/Library/Application Support/FBRX OS` or `%APPDATA%\\FBRX OS`) with your deployment tool; FBRX OS activates\n     it on its next start and deletes the file (a key that fails verification is kept as `fbrx-license.key.rejected`).\n     The setup wizard licenses your own laptop this way.\n\nWithout a control plane, sign keys from the command line with the same private key:\n\n```bash\nnpm run license:issue -- --customer \"Acme Ltd\" --edition ultra --seats 10 --expires 2027-12-31\n# Up to Endpoint Pro, joining a tenant on activation:\nnpm run license:issue -- --customer \"Acme Ltd\" --edition pro \\\n  --command-url https://command.acme.example --enroll-token fbrx_enr_…\n```\n\nA managed license takes precedence over an offline one; if it is revoked, a device falls back to its offline key,\nif any, and otherwise to the free features. License changes take effect immediately (licensed services start or stop\nwithout a restart)."},{"d":"docs/LICENSING.md","t":"Editions, prices and licenses › Joining FBRX Command","x":"With **Computers that activate this key join this tenant automatically** ticked, FBRX Command makes an enrollment\ntoken for the license (one use per seat, expiring with the license), signs the tenant's address and that token into\nthe key, and adds `fleet` to its features. On a computer:\n\n1. Someone pastes the key into **Settings → License** (or drops it in as `fbrx-license.key`).\n2. The key is verified offline; its features turn on at once, and Settings → Edition shows how far the computer may\n   go (Black Hole Edition only once it is in the tenant, when the license allows it).\n3. If the computer is not in a tenant yet, it enrolls itself with the token and from then on receives the tenant's\n   license, settings, policy and credentials like any enrolled device. The License page shows the FBRX Command\n   address, and the activation message says which organization it joined.\n\nIt joins once per license: if the vault is locked it waits until it is unlocked, if the server cannot be reached it\ntries again at the next start, and if someone later leaves the tenant on purpose (Organization → Disconnect) the key does\nnot join again by itself. The tenant then decides how far each license goes (up to Pro, Ultra or Black Hole Edition)."},{"d":"docs/LINEUP.md","t":"The FBRX lineup","x":"**FBRX OS** is the platform: the shared core (policy, audit, the Fabrix agent, FBRX Shield, Mesh) that every FBRX\nproduct is built on. It comes in two forms, and as an app for the computers you already have.\n\n| Product | What it is | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **FBRX Desktop** | FBRX OS as a whole operating system for computers, with FBRX Endpoint Ultra and FBRX Shield built in. | Planned |\n| **FBRX Server** | FBRX OS for servers: Debian 13 set up the FBRX way, with a web console and one or more **roles** (below): virtual machines, an AI agent on FBRX Mesh, the network's gate and its threat watch, image backups. [SERVER.md](SERVER.md) | 1.0 |\n| **FBRX Virtual** | The hypervisor FBRX Server runs: virtual machines on KVM, their screens, storage, networks, the hardware map and BIOS control. [SERVER.md](SERVER.md) | First stage |\n| **FBRX Endpoint** | The FBRX toolset on top of Windows, macOS or Linux, for organizations and homes that keep their operating system. Every computer opens in **Essentials** (free); **Pro** and **Ultra** come with an FBRX account or from your organization, and **Black Hole Edition** (everything) is an FBRX Command add-on. All come with FBRX Shield. [LICENSING.md](LICENSING.md) | Beta 2.0 |\n| **FBRX Digger** | The FBRX Network Center on its own: network scans and their comparison, ping and trace route, the speed test, the Wi-Fi channel planner, DNS and port checks, device consoles (SSH, Telnet) and network environments, for Windows, macOS and Linux. Built from the same code as FBRX Endpoint. [DIGGER.md](DIGGER.md) | 1.0 |\n| **FBRX Mobile** | The phone app: pairs with your FBRX computer to ask Fabrix, approve what it does and see how it is doing, and buys Pro or Ultra for your FBRX account. In the browser, or from the App Store and Google Play. [MESH.md](MESH.md) | Beta 2.0 |\n| **FBRX Shield** | FBRX's antivirus, in every FBRX Endpoint and FBRX Desktop, alongside the choice of Microsoft Defender or an antivirus already installed. [SHIELD.md](SHIELD.md) | Beta |\n| **FBRX Command** | The tenant controller, in the cloud or on a computer of your own: Work, School and Home tenants, people, licenses, billing and permissions; turns FBRX Endpoints into lightweight device management; controls FBRX Mesh; and (next) governs FBRX Virtual servers. [ADMIN_CONSOLE.md](ADMIN_CONSOLE.md), [ACCOUNTS.md](ACCOUNTS.md) | Beta |\n| **FBRX Mesh** | FBRX Endpoints and FBRX Servers talking to one another directly, end-to-end encrypted, with the tenant deciding who may reach whom. **Mesh Assist** lets them lend each other their AI (when one runs out of steps or credits) and lets a controller hand out work. [MESH.md](MESH.md) | Beta |\n| **FBRX Gate** | A real firewall: routing, firewall rules, VLANs, DHCP and DNS, a VPN, traffic priority and Prefer Mesh (a fast lane for FBRX computers helping each other), changed by commits that undo themselves unless kept, from the console or a router-style command line. The gate role of FBRX Server; as a virtual machine on FBRX Virtual later. [GATE.md](GATE.md) | First stage |\n| **FBRX Time** | Whole-machine image backups that prove they work: FBRX Server backs up its virtual machines and keeps the images computers send it, and checks each one by starting it as a throwaway machine with no network; FBRX Endpoint makes images of the computer on a schedule; FBRX Command starts one on any computer. The time role of FBRX Server. [TIME.md](TIME.md) | 1.0 |\n| **FBRX MiniDome** | Watches the network through FBRX Gate (names asked, connections, devices) for threats, working hand in hand with FBRX Shield on each endpoint: a computer that allows it hears what the network saw coming from it, and the network sees how the computer is protected. AI explains findings; blocking is one click on the gate. The minidome role of FBRX Server. [MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md) | First stage |"},{"d":"docs/LINEUP.md","t":"The FBRX lineup › How they fit","x":"```\n                         FBRX Command (tenants, users, licenses, policy)\n                ┌──────────────┬───────────────┬────────────────┐\n           FBRX Endpoint   FBRX Desktop    FBRX Server       FBRX Mesh\n          (Essentials to Black Hole Edition)      └ FBRX Virtual ──── virtual machines\n                └── FBRX Shield ─┘              ├ FBRX Gate (routing, firewall)\n                                                └ FBRX MiniDome (network watch) ◄──► FBRX Shield\n```"},{"d":"docs/LINEUP.md","t":"The FBRX lineup › FBRX Server roles","x":"One FBRX Server can do one job or several; an organization with several servers gives each its roles, and all of them\nstay on FBRX Mesh, so their AI helps one another (Mesh Assist) and a controller can hand work to the server whose\nroles fit.\n\n| Role | | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `virtual` | FBRX Virtual, the hypervisor (the web console comes with every role) | First stage |\n| `ai` | The FBRX core: an AI agent on FBRX Mesh with Mesh Assist, set up from the console's *Mesh & AI* | First stage |\n| `gate` | FBRX Gate: the network's router and firewall | First stage |\n| `minidome` | FBRX MiniDome: threat detection through the gate | First stage |\n| `time` | FBRX Time: image backups of virtual machines and computers, each checked by starting it | 1.0 |\n| `command` | FBRX Command on the server: the organization's computers, settings and policy, licenses and updates; servers join it too | New |\n| `files` | A file server: shared folders from the server or one of its virtual machines, priority for their traffic, backups to a NAS | New |\n| `dns` | Name service for the network | Planned |\n| `directory` | Directory and sign-in for the organization's computers (a domain controller) | Planned |\n\nWhere this is going, for example:\n\n```\n   fbrx-cmd-1 (command, ai) ── decides the settings, controller for Mesh Assist\n        │ FBRX Mesh (end-to-end encrypted)\n        ├── fbrx-dns-1 (dns, ai)\n        ├── fbrx-dc-1  (directory, ai)\n        ├── fbrx-files-1 (files, virtual, ai)\n        ├── fbrx-gate-1 (gate, minidome, ai) ── the network's edge, watching it\n        └── FBRX Endpoints (Essentials / Pro / Ultra) and FBRX Desktops\n```\n\nEach server keeps its own policy: a controller's work runs there only because that server gave it the Controller\npermission, and still under that server's rules."},{"d":"docs/LINEUP.md","t":"The FBRX lineup › Names","x":"* Say **FBRX Desktop** or **FBRX Server**, \"powered by FBRX OS\". FBRX OS on its own means the platform.\n* **FBRX Virtual** is the hypervisor (FBRX Server is the operating system it comes with).\n* **FBRX Endpoint Essentials**, **Pro** and **Ultra** are the three editions of the app (Ultra through FBRX Command).\n* Other companies' products are named only to say FBRX works with them (for example \"works with Dell iDRAC\",\n  \"Outlook / Microsoft 365 calendars\", \"Microsoft Defender\")."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile","x":"The mesh links one person's devices: this computer, their other FBRX OS computers and their phone. It is off until\nyou turn it on in **Mesh & phone**; then FBRX listens on port 47800 (configurable) on the local network."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › What paired devices can do","x":"Each device gets its own permissions on *this* computer, changeable at any time and effective immediately:\n\n| Permission | Phones (default) | Computers (default) |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| See how it is doing (health, performance, services) | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Messages (to this computer alone or to everyone) and notifications | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Ask this computer's agent | ✔ | |\n| Approve or deny waiting actions | ✔ | |\n| Tasks and notes | ✔ | |\n| Receive alerts | ✔ | |\n| Lock and sleep | | |\n| Borrow its AI (Mesh Assist, below) | — | ✔ |\n| Controller (Mesh Assist, below) | — | |\n| Network protection (a gate's FBRX MiniDome, below) | | |\n| Sync (FBRX Sync, below) | — | |\n\nEach device card offers one choice, **What it may do here**: *The usual* (the defaults above), *Full trust* (everything\nbut the two special permissions), *Messages only* or *Paused* (stays paired, can do nothing). **Choose one by one**\nopens every permission, each with an (i) that explains it; **Network protection** and **Controller** are special and\nnever part of a preset, and neither is **Sync**, which a computer's card also offers as *Turn on Sync*. *Message*\n(opens the conversation with it in **Messages**, see [MESSAGES.md](MESSAGES.md)),\n*Ask its agent* and *How is it doing?* are on the card; *Send a ping*, *Find my\nphone*, *Lock it*, *Put it to sleep* and *Remove* are under **More**.\n\nRequests from another *computer* follow **When another computer asks this computer's agent**: ask me first (an\napproval appears here), allow, or never; a device can never approve its own request. Phones you pair are yours, so\ntheir requests skip that first question (turn off *Ask the agent* for a phone to stop them). Either way the agent runs\nunder this computer's policy: each risky tool call still waits for an approval.\n\n**FBRX Mobile** is a small web app the computer serves to paired phones at `http://:47800/m/`. Add it to\nthe home screen. It shows status, alerts, tasks, messages and waiting approvals, lets you ask the agent, and can\nmake the phone ring (*Find my phone*). It refreshes while open; phones are not woken in the background.\n\nWhen the computer runs **Endpoint Essentials**, FBRX Mobile is as simple as the computer: **Home** is the split-flap\nboard (a greeting, the date, whether the AI is ready) and a few notes from FBRX: tips and tricks (each can be\ndismissed), **AI models** (the models on the computer, and *Use* to switch to one, for a phone that may ask the AI)\nand what Pro and Ultra add. Its tabs are Home, Ask, AI (and Approve when something waits). The notes are the\nsame ones as on the computer's Home (`packages/shared/src/essentials-home.ts`)."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › FBRX Sync: the same projects, tasks, notes and calendar on your computers","x":"FBRX Sync keeps your **projects, tasks, notes, snippets and FBRX calendar events** the same on two of your computers.\nIt goes straight from one computer to the other over the mesh, every request and reply sealed for that one computer\n(below), so **no FBRX server ever holds or sees any of it**: FBRX only provides the way for your computers to do it.\nAnd it happens only when you ask: nothing syncs in the background.\n\n1. Pair the two computers (*Add a device*, below), on the same network or any network where they can reach each other.\n2. On **both** computers, the other one's card: **Turn on Sync** (or *Choose one by one → Sync*). Each computer decides\n   for itself; until both have, *Sync now* says which one still has to.\n3. **Sync now** on either computer's card. Both end up with everything either had; the card says when they last\n   synced and what moved (*3 here, 2 there*), on both computers.\n\nHow it decides:\n\n* **Newer wins.** Every item carries the time it last changed, and the newer copy goes to the other computer (so both\n  computers' clocks should be right).\n* **Deleting** an item deletes it on the other computer at the next sync, unless it was changed there after you\n  deleted it: then it comes back. Deleting a project keeps what was in it, in no project, on both.\n* **Changed on both** since the two last synced: the newer one wins, and a note or snippet keeps the other version\n  beside it as *(copy from …)*, so no writing is lost.\n* With three computers, sync each pair you use; deletions and edits pass along through whichever computer has them.\n\nWhat does not sync (yet): chats, memory and skills, settings, Vault passwords, and calendars from other services\n(Outlook / Microsoft 365, calendar links), which every computer reads from the service itself. Sync is for your own\ncomputers: phones see the computer's tasks and notes instead, and a computer your organization introduced syncs only\nwhen the organization allows it."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Mesh Assist: computers lending each other their AI","x":"When the agent on one computer runs out of room, another computer on the mesh finishes the job, and the two can talk\nit through. It also lets one computer (typically a server) hand work out to the others.\n\n**When help is brought in** (each one a setting under **Mesh & phone → Share AI → Fine-tune**; *Sharing* sets asking and lending together):\n\n* **Out of steps.** The agent reached its step limit for a task: the task, a digest of what was done so far and what\n  is left go to a helper, whose answer finishes the reply (marked as coming from that computer).\n* **The AI provider stopped answering.** Out of credits, over a quota or rate limit, a billing problem, an outage or a\n  network failure: the same handover, instead of an error.\n* **The agent asks.** Mid-task, the agent can look at who could help (`mesh.helpers`) and consult another computer's\n  agent (`mesh.consult`), with follow-up questions in the same conversation on the helper.\n* **A person or a controller sends work** to the best helper, to every computer, or to one, with a priority.\n\n**Who helps.** The asking computer asks each paired computer what it can do right now (its AI and model, whether it\nruns locally, how busy it is, its processor load and the roles it announces) and picks the best one: one that helps\nright away before one that asks its person, a ready AI, a free slot, a light load.\n\n**The settings, on both sides:**\n\n| Setting | Default | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bring another computer's AI in | Ask me first | Off, ask the person here first (an approval), or automatically. |\n| When the agent runs out of steps / when the AI provider stops answering / the agent may consult | On | Which of the above may bring help in. |\n| Priority of this computer's requests | Normal | Background, normal, high (urgent is for controllers). |\n| Lend the agent to other computers | Ask me first | Off, ask the person here first, or automatically. |\n| While helping, the agent here may | Look things up | Think and answer only, read-only tools, or anything its policy allows. |\n| Help at the same time / steps per request | 1 / 12 | The rest waits in a queue, highest priority first. |\n| Urgent work from a controller may stop lower-priority help | On | The stopped help is told why. |\n| This computer's own requests go first | On | The AI on this computer answers one request at a time: your own start before help for other computers (Mesh Assist, a lent model). Help that already started finishes first. |\n\nThe **Help with AI** permission decides which paired computers may ask at all (on by default between computers, never\nfor phones).\n\n**Priorities and controllers.** Requests are background, normal, high or urgent; a helper runs the most urgent first.\nA **controller** is a computer you trust to hand out work: give it the **Controller** permission on each computer\nthat should obey it. A controller's requests (when FBRX runs as administrator there, or, on FBRX Server, when its\nconsole's administrators made it a controller) run without the helper's person being asked, may be urgent, and may\nstop lower-priority help that is running. Without the Controller permission a computer treats it like any other.\n\n**One server, many people: the work queue.** Point the computers of a school, business or home at one FBRX Server (on\nsite, or in the cloud) and its agent takes their requests one after another: as many at once as its Mesh Assist\nsettings allow, the rest waiting their turn, most important first, then first come. Each request carries the computer\nit came from (proven by the mesh pairing) and the person who asked, as that computer tells it: the name and email of\nthe FBRX account signed in there, and the system user. A computer sees its place in line (*3rd in line*) and who\nstopped a request.\n\nOn the server, *Mesh & AI*:\n\n* **Work queue:** what runs and what waits, with who asked and what. Operators run a request next, change its\n  priority, or cancel it (running or waiting, whoever asked); administrators pause the queue, so what runs finishes and\n  nothing new starts.\n* **Log** (administrators): every request, kept 180 days, with the computer, the person, the priority, how it ended, who\n  cancelled it and how long it took, tallied by person and by computer; filter by either, by status or by words, and\n  export it as CSV. What was asked is kept; the answer stays in the server's conversation, and the log notes only its\n  length. Each computer keeps the same log of what it asked (*Help asked from here*).\n\nThe queue, the log and its controls are for the people who run the computer (its console, or someone at it), never for\nits agent, plugins or the Local API. A restart cuts off what was waiting or running: the log says so, and the computer\nthat asked sees it failed.\n\n**What never changes:**\n\n* Help runs under the **helping** computer's policy and Guardian: every risky tool call there still needs its usual\n  approval, and the helper works with its own files, not the asker's.\n* Help is ne"},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Mesh Assist: computers lending each other their AI (2)","x":"ver passed along twice: a helper cannot bring a third computer in.\n* Everything is in both computers' audit logs (`assist.*`, `run.handoff`), and either side can stop a request.\n* Requests travel over the same sealed, authenticated mesh connection as everything else."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Think with your FBRX Server's AI","x":"Mesh Assist hands a task to another computer's agent. A paired computer can also lend just its **AI model**: the agent\nhere keeps working here (its tools, files, memory, approvals and audit log stay on this computer) and only the thinking\nhappens there. A laptop that runs a small model, or none, gets the answers of your FBRX Server's big one.\n\n* **On this computer:** *AI models → Your other computers' AI* lists the paired computers that would lend the AI they\n  run themselves; **Use for chats** makes one your default (it is an AI provider like any other, so it can also be\n  chosen per chat, or set up by hand with type *mesh*). Each answer in a chat shows the model, the computer, and how\n  long it took, so you can compare it with the AI on this computer.\n* **On the server:** the same setting lends it as Mesh Assist (*Lend the agent to other computers*, or *Mesh & AI* in\n  the server console) and the same **Help with AI** permission. With *Ask me first*, its person is asked once per\n  computer (until FBRX restarts there); a \"no\" holds for ten minutes.\n* **Only an AI it runs itself.** A computer whose AI is a cloud provider does not lend it, so a conversation never\n  leaves your computers this way. The server's own *Thinking* setting decides whether its model thinks first.\n* **How it travels:** the conversation goes to the server over the sealed mesh connection (up to 1 MiB a request, so\n  about 160,000 characters of conversation; pictures stay here), and the answer streams back as the model writes it.\n  Stopping the answer here stops the model there. From away, connect [FBRX VPN](VPN.md) first: the server is reached at\n  its address at home."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Prefer Mesh","x":"A fast lane for traffic between FBRX computers, so their AI helping each other does not wait behind a backup or a\ndownload. Turn it on in **Mesh & phone → Network** (on an FBRX Server: *Mesh & AI → Network*).\n\n* **Mesh networks first.** List the networks the computers should reach each other through, in order (a VLAN for AI\n  and servers, say). Computers tell each other their addresses when they say hello, and each one picks the other's\n  address inside a mesh network; if it does not answer, the usual address is used and the mesh address is tried again\n  five minutes later.\n* **TCP, kept open.** Mesh calls go over TCP connections that are kept alive and send at once (no delay), so a\n  conversation between two computers does not start a new handshake each time.\n* **Marked for priority.** Mesh traffic (TCP port 47800) is marked with a DSCP class: AF41 by default (interactive,\n  below voice calls; EF and CS5 sit higher, CS0 turns the priority off). Switches that trust DSCP, and FBRX Gate, put it\n  in a faster queue. Marking is set up on Linux with an nftables table (`inet fbrx_mesh`, as root) and on Windows with\n  a QoS policy (after an administrator prompt); macOS does not mark.\n* **Jumbo frames.** If the mesh networks run at MTU 9000, say so: the page checks each computer's mesh interface and\n  the test sends a 9000-byte packet that may not be split, to see whether every switch on the way takes it.\n* **Test the paths.** *Test* shows, for each paired computer, which address it is reached on, whether that is a mesh\n  address, the round trip, and the jumbo-frame result.\n\nFBRX Gate does the network's side: it marks mesh traffic it routes (and everything from a mesh VLAN), shapes the\ninternet line with a priority lane, and offers jumbo frames on the mesh VLAN. See [GATE.md](GATE.md#traffic-priority-and-prefer-mesh)."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Network protection: FBRX MiniDome and FBRX Shield","x":"A gate's **FBRX MiniDome** ([MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md)) watches the network. With **Network protection** allowed for the\ngate's server, this computer gets an alert when MiniDome saw something coming from it (a known-bad domain, a DNS\ntunnel, …), and MiniDome may see how this computer is protected (its antivirus and threats found). It is off until you\nallow it, per paired server."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Pairing","x":"1. **Mesh & phone → Add a device → Show a code on this computer** shows a QR code and a 20-character one-time code (100 bits), valid for five\n   minutes.\n2. A phone scans the QR code (it contains the computer's address, the code and the computer's key fingerprint), with\n   the camera (FBRX Mobile opens in the browser) or with **Scan the code** in the FBRX app;\n   another computer uses **Add a device → Enter a code from another computer** (or **Join** beside it under *FBRX\n   computers nearby*) and types the code.\n3. The joining device creates its own X25519 key pair and sends its public key with\n   `HMAC-SHA512(code, \"fbrx-pair-v1\" | its key | the computer's key)`. The computer checks it, stores the device and\n   answers with its own HMAC over both keys and the new device id, which the joining device checks before trusting\n   the computer. Both sides have proved they know the code, so a device in the middle cannot substitute its keys.\n4. The code is used once. Five wrong attempts end the pairing session (and notify you); pairing requests are also\n   rate-limited per address.\n\nCompare the fingerprints shown on both screens if you want to be certain."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Every request","x":"Requests between paired devices are JSON sealed with NaCl `box` (X25519 + XSalsa20-Poly1305) using the sender's\nsecret key and the recipient's public key, so each request is both encrypted and authenticated. Each carries a\nrandom id and a timestamp; the receiver refuses requests older or newer than two minutes and any id it has already\nseen. Replies are sealed the same way and tied to the request id. Removing a device deletes its key; its next request\nis refused.\n\nThe computer's secret key lives in the credential vault (encrypted under the OS keychain). The phone's key lives in\nthe phone's browser storage for the FBRX Mobile page."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Limits to know about","x":"* **Local network only.** The mesh is meant for your home or office network. To reach your devices from elsewhere,\n  connect with **FBRX VPN** to your FBRX Gate ([VPN.md](VPN.md)) rather than forwarding the port: the mesh then works\n  through the tunnel, your FBRX Server's AI included.\n* **The phone app arrives over plain HTTP.** Requests are end-to-end encrypted, but the web page itself is served\n  unencrypted by your computer, so someone actively attacking your local network at the moment you open FBRX\n  Mobile could tamper with the page. Pair and use the phone app on networks you trust. (A future native app removes\n  this.)\n* **Windows Firewall** asks once whether FBRX OS may accept connections; allow it on *private* networks only.\n* Clocks must be within two minutes of each other."},{"d":"docs/MESH.md","t":"Mesh and FBRX Mobile › Organizations","x":"**An organization's computers mesh by themselves.** Every computer enrolled in FBRX Command reports its mesh key,\nport, addresses and server roles in its heartbeat. FBRX Command hands each computer a roster of the organization's\nother computers, and because it vouches for their keys over the enrolled connection, they trust each other without\npairing codes. FBRX Mesh turns itself on at each computer (unless a profile locks `mesh.enabled` off). The roster\ncomes again whenever a computer joins, moves or changes roles (FBRX Command nudges every online computer), when a\ncomputer reconnects, and every ten minutes.\n\nWhat the organization's computers may do on each other is the organization's choice, not each computer's:\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| See each other and message | Always (messages only while Messages is on for the organization) |\n| Borrow a server's AI (Mesh Assist) | On a server with the **ai** role, for the groups the **hive** allows (every group by default) |\n| Network protection | A server with the **minidome** role may warn the others about threats it sees |\n| The agent, approvals, tasks, control, controller | Never through the organization; pair by hand for those |\n\nIn FBRX Command: *Profiles & groups → FBRX Mesh*: the computers mesh together (on or off), and *Who may use the\nservers' AI*: every group, or the groups chosen. Changes reach online computers at once. On each computer the\norganization's computers show *from your organization* on the Mesh page; they cannot be removed or given more\nrights there, and leaving the organization (or the organization turning its mesh off) forgets them. Computers and\nphones paired by hand stay as they are.\n\nThe mesh is otherwise per person. Administrators can switch it off for a tenant or group by locking `mesh.enabled` to\n`false` in managed settings (and Mesh Assist on its own with the `mesh.assist.*` settings); device pairing, the\norganization's introductions, requests and permission changes are written to the audit log (`category: mesh`)."},{"d":"docs/MESSAGES.md","t":"Messages","x":"**Messages** (in the sidebar, under Glass, in every edition) is chat between computers, for two kinds of\nneighbors:\n\n* **My devices**: your other computers and your phone on FBRX Mesh. Messages go straight from one device to the\n  other, encrypted end to end; nothing passes through FBRX Command or any other server.\n* **Your organization or family**: every computer in the same FBRX Command tenant. FBRX Command passes these\n  messages on at once over the connection each computer already keeps open, and keeps them for 180 days, so a\n  computer that was off finds them when it starts.\n\nA family can talk here instead of using the help desk: \"Can I play for ten more minutes?\" goes from a child's laptop\nto a parent's desktop in a second, and the answer comes straight back. On a Home tenant, the Help desk tab stays with\nEndpoint Ultra (a parent's computer that receives the children's requests, say); Messages is in every edition. Work\nand School tenants keep the help desk for tickets to IT, next to Messages."},{"d":"docs/MESSAGES.md","t":"Messages › Conversations","x":"| Conversation | Who is in it |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Everyone on my mesh** | Every paired device that takes messages (Mesh & phone → its permissions → Messages) |\n| A computer or phone on your mesh | Just the two of you |\n| **Everyone at** *your organization* | Every computer in the tenant, and FBRX Command's own people, who can post here too |\n| A computer in your organization | Just the two computers |\n\nSmall organizations (up to 12 computers) list every computer; larger ones list the computers you have talked with,\nand **New message** finds any other by name. Each conversation shows whether the other computer is online, what was\nsaid last and how many messages are new. The sidebar counts everything unread.\n\nWriting: **Enter** sends, **Shift+Enter** starts a new line, up to 4,000 characters."},{"d":"docs/MESSAGES.md","t":"Messages › Delivery","x":"* A mesh message to a computer that is switched off waits on yours and goes when that computer is back (for up to a\n  week); it shows *waiting* until then. A computer that turned Messages off answers so, and the message shows *not\n  delivered* with **Try again**.\n* A message to everyone on the mesh goes to every device that is on; it shows how many it reached.\n* Phones get messages the next time FBRX Mobile checks in.\n* Organization messages wait in FBRX Command for computers that are off.\n\nA notification pops up for each new message, with **Open**, except for the conversation already on the screen.\nPresenter-safe mode keeps them quiet and blurs Messages, like other private lists."},{"d":"docs/MESSAGES.md","t":"Messages › Settings and policy","x":"Settings → **Messages** on each computer:\n\n| Setting | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| Messages | The page itself. Off hides it and turns messages from your mesh away. |\n| Chat with *your organization* | Messages with the other computers in the tenant. Off keeps Messages to your own mesh. |\n| Notify me about new messages | The pop-ups. |\n\nIn FBRX Command:\n\n* **Profiles & groups → Organization defaults → Messages** turns organization messages on or off for the whole\n  tenant.\n* In a profile, the **Messages** area holds the same three switches (`messages.enabled`,\n  `messages.organization`, `messages.notify`), and the **No chatting** preset turns Messages off and locks it, for a\n  classroom group say. Help desk tickets keep working.\n* Student computers in a School tenant start with organization messages off; the school's own profiles can turn them\n  on. Children's computers at home have them on.\n* The **Messages** page shows the *Everyone* conversation: post to it (operators and up) and remove what does not\n  belong. Messages between two computers are not shown in the console; FBRX Command keeps them for 180 days, then\n  drops them."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome","x":"**FBRX MiniDome** watches your network through FBRX Gate ([GATE.md](GATE.md)): the names devices ask for, the\nconnections they make, and who is on the wire. What looks wrong becomes a **finding**, by device. FBRX computers on\nFBRX Mesh hear about what was seen coming from them, so **FBRX Shield** and MiniDome work hand in hand: the network sees\na laptop asking for a malware domain, the laptop gets the alert and runs a scan.\n\nIt sees and reports; changes to the network (blocking a domain, moving a device to its own network) go through the gate,\none click away and committed like any other change.\n\n> First stage (FBRX MiniDome 0.1): it runs on the gate itself, as the **minidome** role of FBRX Server."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › Install","x":"```bash\nsudo bash fbrx-server-1.0.0/install.sh --roles gate,minidome,ai --gate-wan eno1 --gate-lan eno2\n```\n\nMiniDome needs the gate role. The installer adds `conntrack` (to follow new connections) and rotates the gate's question\nlog daily. The **ai** role is optional: it explains findings and tells FBRX computers about them."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › What it listens to","x":"| Source | |\n| --- | --- |\n| The gate's DNS question log | Every name asked of the gate, and the answers \"no such name\" (followed as it is written, across rotation). |\n| New connections | conntrack's event stream on the gate (or the kernel's connection table every few seconds without the tool). |\n| Devices | The gate's neighbor table (who has which address on the wire) and its DHCP leases. |\n\n*MiniDome → Settings* shows each source and whether it works."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › What it looks for","x":"| Finding | How bad | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Known-bad name** | Serious (warning when the gate blocks it anyway) | A device asked for a domain on your threat lists. |\n| **Made-up names** | Serious | Many random-looking names in ten minutes, or names that do not exist: how some malware hunts for its servers (domain generation). Content networks and reverse lookups are left out. |\n| **Data hidden in names** | Serious | Very long names, many different long names under one domain, or many text lookups: a DNS tunnel carrying data past the firewall. |\n| **DNS around the gate** | Warning | A device asks another DNS server (port 53 or 853), so the gate's blocking and MiniDome do not see its names. |\n| **Scanning** | Serious | A device tries many ports on another device, or one port on many devices of your networks, within a minute. |\n| **Checking in like clockwork** | Warning | New connections to the same place at regular intervals: smart devices and updaters do it, so does malware calling home. Mute what you recognize. |\n| **Address fight** | Serious / warning | An address changing hands back and forth, a device answering for an address the gate gave someone else, or one device answering for many addresses (ARP spoofing; some Wi-Fi extenders do the last one innocently). |\n| **New device** | Worth knowing | A device the gate has not seen before (after a ten-minute learning period on a new MiniDome). |\n\n**Sensitivity**: *only when sure*, *balanced* or *readily* moves the thresholds. A finding speaks up at most every half\nminute per device and subject, unless it got worse."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › Findings","x":"One finding per device and subject (a domain, an address), counted, with the latest evidence and the device by name.\n**Looking into it** marks it as yours; **Resolve** closes it (it opens again if it happens again); **Mute** keeps\ncounting it but never raises it again.\n\n* **Explain with AI**: this server's AI (the ai role, *Mesh & AI*) explains the finding in plain words, how worried to\n  be, and what to do next. The finding and its evidence go to the AI provider set up there.\n* **Block on the gate**: for a domain, adds it to the gate's blocking, waiting in *Changes* for a commit."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › Threat lists","x":"Add the lists you trust in *Settings* (hosts files or one domain per line, http or https); they are fetched twice a day\nand kept between restarts. **Never report** takes domains you know are fine (their subdomains too).\n\nTo try MiniDome, ask the gate for `minidome-test.fbrx.invalid` from any device (`nslookup minidome-test.fbrx.invalid`):\nit is always on the list."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › With FBRX computers: Network protection","x":"On each FBRX computer, the **Network protection** permission for the gate's server (*Mesh & phone → the server →\nNetwork protection*) decides whether:\n\n* the computer gets an **alert** when MiniDome saw something coming from it (*The network gate saw a threat from this\n  computer*, critical for serious findings, with the evidence and the suggestion to run a scan), and\n* MiniDome's **device list** shows how it is protected (its antivirus, FBRX Shield or another, and threats found).\n\nIt is off until the computer turns it on. The message goes through the server's FBRX core (the ai role) and FBRX Mesh,\nend-to-end encrypted, only to the paired computer that has that address. *Settings → Tell FBRX computers* turns it off\non the gate's side."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › Privacy","x":"MiniDome keeps findings (with a few lines of evidence each) and the devices it has seen, in\n`/var/lib/fbrx-virtual/dome.db`. It does not keep the names asked or the connections made; the gate's question log is\nrotated daily and kept a week."},{"d":"docs/MINIDOME.md","t":"FBRX MiniDome › API","x":"Under `/v1` on the console's port:\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /dome` | Settings, open findings by severity, what it listened to, the learning period (viewers) |\n| `GET /dome/findings?status=active\\|open\\|acknowledged\\|resolved\\|muted` | Findings |\n| `POST /dome/findings/:id` `{ status }` | Looking into it, resolve, mute (operators) |\n| `POST /dome/findings/:id/explain` | This server's AI explains it (operators; ai role) |\n| `POST /dome/findings/:id/block` | Block its domain on the gate (administrators; into the candidate) |\n| `GET /dome/devices` | Devices seen, with FBRX computers' protection |\n| `PUT /dome/settings` `{ settings }`, `POST /dome/feeds/refresh` | Settings and threat lists (administrators) |"},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API","x":"There are three ways to add capabilities or connect FBRX OS to other software. All of them end up as **tools** in the\nsame registry, so everything the agent does with them is governed by policy, approvals and the audit log.\n\n| You want to… | Use | Code needed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Call an HTTP API (CRM, ticketing, ERP, an internal service) | **REST connector** | None — configure in **Connections** |\n| Use an existing MCP server (GitHub, databases, Slack, …) | **MCP connector** (stdio or HTTP) | None |\n| Push FBRX events to Slack/Teams/SIEM, or let the agent post messages | **Webhook connector** | None |\n| Hand work to another workstation's agent | **FBRX peer connector** | None |\n| Ship custom logic as an installable, versioned package | **Plugin** | JavaScript/TypeScript |\n| Drive FBRX OS from scripts or another desktop app | **Local API** | Any language |"},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Plugins › Create one","x":"```bash\nnpm run plugin:create -- order-lookup            # JavaScript; add --ts for TypeScript\n# edit plugins/order-lookup/index.mjs\nnpm run plugin:pack -- plugins/order-lookup      # → plugins/order-lookup/dist-plugin/com.fbrx.order-lookup-0.1.0.tgz\n```\n\nInstall the `.tgz` (or the folder, while developing) from **Tools & plugins → Install plugin**, push it to devices\nfrom the admin console (**Plugins → Upload plugin**, then **Deploy**), or use\n`window.fbrx.call('plugins.install', { path })` / the Local API.\n\n`plugin:pack` validates the manifest, and with `--bundle` (automatic for TypeScript entry points) bundles the entry\npoint and its npm dependencies into a single `index.mjs`, so the package needs no `node_modules` on the target."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Plugins › Manifest — fbrx-plugin.json","x":"```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"com.acme.orders\",\n  \"name\": \"ACME Orders\",\n  \"version\": \"1.2.0\",\n  \"description\": \"Look up and annotate orders in the ACME order service.\",\n  \"author\": \"ACME IT\",\n  \"namespace\": \"acme\",\n  \"main\": \"index.mjs\",\n  \"engines\": { \"fbrx\": \">=1.0.0\" },\n  \"permissions\": [\"network:orders.acme.example\", \"secrets:ACME_TOKEN\", \"storage\"]\n}\n```\n\n* `id` — reverse-DNS, unique. Installing the same id again upgrades in place (data is kept).\n* `namespace` — tool prefix; `acme` + `lookup` → the agent sees `acme.lookup`. Must be unique per workstation.\n* `permissions` — least privilege, enforced by the host:\n\n| Permission | Grants |\n| --- | --- |\n| `storage` | `ctx.storage` — persistent JSON key/value store (travels with backups) |\n| `notifications` | `ctx.notify(title, body)` |\n| `secrets:` | `ctx.secrets.get('')` — one named vault secret (set it in **Vault**, or push it from the console) |\n| `network:` | `ctx.http.fetch` to that host; `*.example.com` and `*` allowed. Requests also pass network policy |\n| `calendar` | `ctx.calendar.events(from, to)` — the person's calendar events, read-only, up to 62 days per call (no meeting links or organizers; see [CALENDAR.md](CALENDAR.md)) |"},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Plugins › Tools","x":"```js\n// @ts-check\n/** @type {import('@fbrx/plugin-sdk').PluginDefinition} */\nexport default {\n  async activate(ctx) {\n    ctx.log.info('ready');\n  },\n  tools: [\n    {\n      name: 'lookup',\n      title: 'Look up an order',\n      description: 'Fetch an order by id from the ACME order service.', // the model reads this — be specific\n      risk: 'network',\n      inputSchema: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: { orderId: { type: 'string', description: 'e.g. SO-10442' } },\n        required: ['orderId'],\n      },\n      async run({ orderId }, ctx, invocation) {\n        const token = await ctx.secrets.get('ACME_TOKEN');\n        const res = await ctx.http.fetch(`https://orders.acme.example/api/orders/${encodeURIComponent(orderId)}`, {\n          headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },\n          timeoutMs: 15000,\n        });\n        if (res.status !== 200) return `Order service returned HTTP ${res.status}`;\n        const order = JSON.parse(res.body);\n        return { output: `Order ${order.id}: ${order.status}, ${order.lines.length} lines`, data: order };\n      },\n    },\n  ],\n};\n```\n\n* `risk` drives the default policy: `read` and `network` run, `write`, `execute` and `sensitive` ask for approval\n  (administrators can change this per tool, per plugin or per origin).\n* Return a string or `{ output, data }`. `output` goes back to the model (keep it short); `data` is for programmatic\n  callers. Secret values are redacted from both.\n* `invocation.signal` is aborted when the user cancels or the call times out.\n* `deactivate()` runs before the worker stops."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Plugins › Sandbox","x":"Each plugin runs in its own Node process:\n\n* **Files** — Node's permission model limits reads to the plugin's own folder and writes to its data folder.\n* **Processes** — child processes, worker threads and native addons are refused.\n* **Network** — Node's permission model does not cover sockets, so the worker removes `fetch`, `WebSocket` and\n  `EventSource`, refuses to load `net`, `tls`, `http(s)`, `http2`, `dgram`, `dns`, `child_process`, `cluster`,\n  `worker_threads` and `inspector` (by import, `require` or `process.getBuiltinModule`), forbids installing further\n  module loader hooks, and runs without stdio pipes. The only way out is `ctx.http.fetch`, which the host checks\n  against the manifest's `network:` permissions and the workstation's network policy.\n* **Secrets, storage, notifications** — brokered by the host and checked against the manifest.\n\nCrashes are contained (the host restarts the worker with backoff) and every call is audited. This is defense in\ndepth, not a guarantee against a determined attacker with code execution, so treat plugins like any other software\nyou install: prefer packages published through your control plane, and review a plugin's permissions before\ninstalling it."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Connectors","x":"Configure in **Connections → Add connection** (or push via managed configuration)."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Connectors › REST","x":"`baseUrl`, authentication (`bearer`, `basic`, custom header) using a **vault secret reference** — the credential\nnever appears in configuration or to the model. Requests are pinned to the base URL's origin. Without named\noperations the agent gets a generic `.get` tool (and, if *allow write*, `.send` for\nPOST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE); named operations give it precise, documented tools:\n\n```json\n[\n  { \"name\": \"get_order\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"path\": \"orders/{id}\", \"description\": \"Fetch an order by id\" },\n  { \"name\": \"add_note\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"orders/{id}/notes\", \"description\": \"Append a note to an order\" }\n]\n```"},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Connectors › MCP","x":"* **stdio** — FBRX starts the server locally: `command` (`npx`, `uvx`, a binary), `args`, `env` (with\n  `${secret:NAME}` placeholders resolved from the vault at launch), working directory.\n* **HTTP** — Streamable HTTP endpoint with optional bearer/header auth from the vault.\n\nEvery MCP tool becomes `.` with a configurable default risk level, so a third-party server cannot\nbypass approvals."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Connectors › Webhook (outgoing)","x":"Sends selected events (`approval.requested`, `approval.resolved`, `agent.run.completed`, `agent.run.failed`,\n`policy.denied`, `service.failed`, `fleet.changed`, `backup.completed`, `notification`) to any URL, signed with\n`x-fbrx-signature: sha256=` when a signing secret is set. Optionally the agent gets a\n`.send` tool to post messages."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Connectors › FBRX peer","x":"Points at another workstation's Local API with that machine's **agent-scoped** token. The agent gets\n`.ask` (run a task on the peer's agent, under the peer's own policy) and `.status`."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Local API","x":"Enable in **Settings → Local API**. It listens on `http://127.0.0.1:47821` (remote access is opt-in) and accepts two\ntokens shown on that page: **full** and **agent** (agent runs and tool calls only). Rotate them there.\n\n```bash\nTOKEN=...   # from Settings → Local API, or `fbrx-headless token`\n\n# Run the agent to completion\ncurl -s localhost:47821/v1/agent/run -H \"authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"prompt\":\"Summarize the PDFs in ~/Reports into reports.md\"}'\n\n# Invoke a tool directly (still governed)\ncurl -s localhost:47821/v1/tools/fs.list_dir -H \"authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"input\":{\"path\":\"~/Documents\"}}'\n\n# Any core method (full token)\ncurl -s localhost:47821/v1/rpc -H \"authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"method\":\"backup.list\",\"params\":{}}'\n\n# Live events (server-sent events)\ncurl -N \"localhost:47821/v1/events?token=$TOKEN\"\n```\n\nErrors come back as `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\" } }` with matching HTTP status (403 for policy denials, 423\nwhen the vault is locked, …). Calls made through the Local API are audited with origin `api`."},{"d":"docs/PLUGINS.md","t":"Extending FBRX OS: plugins, connectors and the Local API › Built-in tools","x":"| Tool | Risk | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `fs.list_dir`, `fs.read_file`, `fs.search` | read | Allowed roots and deny patterns from policy |\n| `fs.write_file`, `fs.make_dir`, `fs.delete_file` | write | Guardian blocks writes into system locations |\n| `shell.run` | execute | Blocked patterns, timeout, working-directory confinement |\n| `http.request` | network | Domain allow/block lists, private-network and rebinding protection |\n| `system.info`, `system.processes`, `system.notify`, `time.now`, `fbrx.status`, `fbrx.audit_recent` | read | |\n| `memory.remember`, `memory.recall`, `memory.forget` | write / read / write | Long-term memory stored locally; allowed without approval by the built-in policy rule |"},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model","x":"FBRX OS gives an AI agent real abilities on a workstation — files, shell, network, other applications — so the\ndesign assumes the model can be wrong or manipulated and puts every action behind controls the organization owns."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Trust boundaries","x":"| Boundary | Control |\n| --- | --- |\n| Model → workstation | Every tool call passes the governance pipeline: license, schema validation, rate limit, policy (hard constraints → rules → risk defaults), guardian, human approval, redaction, audit |\n| Untrusted content → model | Tool output (files, web pages, API responses) is scanned for prompt-injection language and handed to the model explicitly marked as untrusted data; any tool call whose input carries such language, or any other guardian warning, requires human approval with the finding shown |\n| Plugin → workstation | Separate process, Node permission model (files, processes, addons), network and process-control modules removed, all capabilities brokered and permission-checked ([details](PLUGINS.md#sandbox)) |\n| Renderer → core | Sandboxed, context-isolated renderer with a strict CSP; only the `window.fbrx` bridge, which routes through the same API and origin checks as everything else |\n| Local scripts → core | Local API on 127.0.0.1 only (remote opt-in), bearer tokens (full / agent-scoped), Host-header checks against DNS rebinding |\n| Device ↔ control plane | HTTPS/WSS; device bearer token issued at enrollment and stored hashed on the server and in the device vault; idempotent, expiring commands; privileged commands need an admin role |\n| Admin → control plane | scrypt passwords, TOTP MFA, short revocable sessions, scoped API keys, RBAC, tenant isolation, rate-limited auth endpoints, full audit |\n| Paired devices → workstation | Mesh off by default; one-time pairing codes proven with HMAC over both public keys; every request sealed with NaCl box, timestamped and replay-checked; per-device permissions and instant revocation ([details](MESH.md)) |\n| Other AI apps → workstation | MCP bridge over the Local API with the agent-scoped token, so calls get the same policy, guardian, approvals and audit as Fabrix; the token is read from the data folder, never copied into other apps' configuration |\n| FBRX → network devices (device consoles) | Opened only by the person at the workstation; SSH host keys pinned on first use and a changed key refused until confirmed; saved passwords kept in the vault as internal secrets (not readable by plugins, the Local API or the agent); the agent can type into a console the person opened only through `device_console.send`, an execute-risk tool that asks for approval by default; connections audited |\n| FBRX VPN → system tunnel | Added, connected and removed only by the person at the workstation (never the agent, the Local API or FBRX Command); the gate's settings are read strictly (no `PreUp`/`PostUp`/`PreDown`/`PostDown` or unknown settings) and the tunnel's file is rebuilt from them; keys sealed with the vault key, the file on disk only while the tunnel starts or stops (Windows: while it runs), readable by the user alone ([details](VPN.md)) |\n| FBRX → Windows administrator rights | Only for actions the person starts at the workstation (never the Local API, the agent's read tools or mesh peers); scripts go to the elevated PowerShell as an encoded command, not a file, and Windows shows the UAC prompt every time |"},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Policy defaults","x":"Out of the box:\n\n* Reads and network requests run; writes, command execution and sensitive actions ask a human.\n* Files are reachable only under the user's home folder and the FBRX workspace; SSH/GPG keys, cloud credentials,\n  `*.pem`/`*.key` files and the macOS/Windows credential stores are always denied; the FBRX data folder itself is never reachable.\n* Private networks (RFC 1918, link-local, loopback, metadata endpoints) are blocked, including after DNS\n  resolution, so the agent cannot be used to probe the LAN or cloud metadata services.\n* Destructive shell patterns (`rm -rf /`, `mkfs`, `dd of=/dev/…`, fork bombs, `format C:`, `diskpart`,\n  `curl … | sh`) are blocked outright.\n* Limits: 30 model steps and 80 tool calls per run, 60 tool calls per minute, approvals time out after 5 minutes.\n* New chats start **offline**: the first network tool in a chat asks the person to put that chat online.\n* Methods that change the computer (terminal, file writes, ending processes, Defender, firewall, repairs, Hyper-V,\n  network adapters, mesh pairing, connecting AI apps) are reserved for the person at the workstation. Automation\n  through the Local API can only read files inside the folders the policy allows.\n* The link checker follows redirects itself without running page code and refuses to follow a link into a private\n  network address, so a malicious link cannot use it to reach your router or other local devices.\n\nAdministrators can tighten or relax all of this per tenant, group or device from the console and lock settings\nso users cannot change them. `mode: audit` lets you trial a stricter policy and see what it would block first."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Credentials","x":"* Vault secrets are AES-256-GCM encrypted under a data key that is protected on this computer (Windows DPAPI; the\n  Linux secret service; on macOS a key file only the user can read, see below) and optionally by a recovery\n  passphrase (scrypt).\n* **macOS does not use the Keychain.** Electron's safeStorage keeps its key in the login Keychain, and macOS asks for\n  the Mac password whenever an updated, re-signed app opens that item, which for an app that is not notarized is\n  every update. Since 1.8.1 FBRX keeps the vault key in `keychain.key` (mode 0600) in the data folder and starts\n  Chromium with its mock keychain, so nothing asks for a password. A key that 1.8.0 or earlier left in the Keychain\n  is read once, only when the person presses **Bring them over** (`vault.importMoved`, reserved for the person at\n  the computer): FBRX asks the `security` tool for the \"FBRX OS Safe Storage\" item (macOS may ask for the Mac\n  password then), decrypts the old wrapping itself (Chromium's OSCrypt format) and re-protects the key with the\n  key file. **Start over** (`vault.reset`) deletes every credential and restarts.\n* **Ask for the password at every start** (`vault.passwordOnStart`): the data key is then kept only under the\n  vault passphrase and the computer-bound copy is deleted, so the vault stays locked after every start until the\n  passphrase is entered (FBRX asks when it opens). Services that need credentials wait for the unlock instead of\n  failing.\n* The model never sees secret values. Connectors and plugins reference secrets by name; the redactor removes any\n  secret value, and every password in the password keeper, from tool output, logs and the conversation.\n* **The password keeper** (Vault → Passwords) seals each sign-in whole (site and username too) with the vault key,\n  bound to the sign-in's id. Its methods are for the person at the computer only, never the Local API or a remote\n  command. FBRX Browser's sign-in helper runs in each page's main frame in an isolated world the page cannot reach,\n  fills in only on the person's pick, only into visible boxes, and only on a page of one of the sign-in's sites (the\n  core checks the address; an `https` sign-in is never offered on `http`). See [VAULT.md](VAULT.md).\n* Organization credentials are created in the console, encrypted at rest with the control plane's master key,\n  delivered over the authenticated device channel and stored as read-only managed secrets.\n* Snapshots contain the vault key encrypted under the snapshot passphrase — protect snapshot files and passphrases\n  like the credentials they contain."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Emergency stop and the FBRX/1 console","x":"* The **emergency stop** (`ai.hardStop`) may be pressed from anywhere, including by an AI app over the Local API, so\n  that anything can pull the brake. It cancels agent runs, denies pending approvals, stops the local model and is\n  recorded in the audit log. While it is on, the agent, `aicoord.consult` and tool calls from any origin other than\n  the person at the computer are refused. **Resuming** (`ai.resume`) is reserved for the person at the computer.\n* **FBRX/1** (`cli.exec`, `cli.complete`) is reserved for the person at the computer. It calls the same core methods\n  as the app (so policy, locks, validation and audit all apply) and validates configuration commits against the\n  settings schema and the organization's locked paths before applying them.\n* The Silly Goose's pointer grab on Windows runs a small PowerShell helper (started with the goose, ended when it\n  leaves) that only moves the mouse pointer to coordinates sent by the goose's own window, for at most about three\n  seconds per grab. It is never started when Fun extras are off."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Copy & migrate","x":"* `migrate.plan`, `migrate.start` and `migrate.cancel` are reserved for the person at the computer. Robocopy and rsync\n  run from argument lists without a shell; folder paths with quotes or line breaks are refused.\n* Guard rails: no mirroring onto a drive root, a system folder or a home folder; no copying a folder into itself; no\n  moving a whole drive or a home folder away. Mirror and move ask for confirmation in the app, and Preview runs the\n  same command in list-only mode. Every start and finish is in the audit log."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Code lab, clipboard and side-panel AI","x":"* **Code lab files** live in the `codelab` folder of the data directory; names are limited to simple file names with\n  a known code extension (no paths), 512 KB each. Every `codelab.*` method is reserved for the person at the\n  computer.\n* **JavaScript** runs in a hidden, throwaway browser window on its own in-memory session: every network request is\n  cancelled, WebRTC is limited to a proxy that doesn't exist, there is no Node.js, no preload bridge, no permissions,\n  no downloads, no navigation and no pop-ups, and the page is `about:blank`. The window is destroyed when the program\n  ends or after ten seconds (an endless loop is cut off). Output is capped.\n* **PowerShell and batch** files never run on the computer itself: *Run in Sandbox* starts Windows Sandbox with\n  networking off, the code folder mapped **read-only**, and the file started inside it. Closing the sandbox discards\n  everything. Each run is in the audit log. Other languages are not run by FBRX at all.\n* **Clipboard history** is off by default (an organization can lock `clipboard.history`). When on, the desktop app\n  checks the clipboard once a second and keeps the last 50 copies of text and pictures (up to 12 pictures) **in\n  memory only**: never written to disk,\n  never synced or backed up, cleared when FBRX quits or the switch is turned off. Copies marked by password managers\n  (`ExcludeClipboardContentFromMonitorProcessing`, `CanIncludeInClipboardHistory`, `org.nspasteboard.ConcealedType`\n  and similar) are skipped. The **Ctrl+Alt+Z** window shows the same in-memory list. Picking a copy puts it on the\n  clipboard and, with *Paste right away* on, presses Ctrl+V once in the app that had the focus: on Windows through a\n  small hidden PowerShell helper (`SendKeys`) started when the history first opens, on Linux through `xdotool` if\n  installed; macOS only copies (pressing keys there would need an accessibility permission). Nothing else is typed.\n* **Side-panel answers** (`ai.quick`: the code lab, Event Viewer, Task Manager and *What if?*) use the default model\n  with **no tools**, so they can explain but never act. Known vault secrets are masked in the code, log lines or\n  process details before they are sent, the emergency stop refuses them, and they are reserved for the person at\n  the computer."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Voice and the agent's thinking","x":"* **Speech to text** runs on the computer with Whisper (transformers.js and ONNX Runtime in WebAssembly, in a Web\n  Worker of the app window). The worker is set never to fetch models itself (`allowRemoteModels` off, no browser\n  cache): it reads them from FBRX's private `fbrx-voice:` scheme, which serves only the ONNX Runtime files bundled\n  with the app and the files of an installed speech model, with path traversal refused. Recordings stay in memory and\n  are dropped once written down; nothing is saved or sent anywhere.\n* **Speech models** are downloaded only when the person at the computer asks (`voice.install`, `voice.remove` and\n  `voice.cancel` are reserved for them; `voice.models` is read-only) and only while internet access is allowed by\n  policy. They come from the named Whisper repositories on Hugging Face over HTTPS into the `voice` folder of the data\n  directory, are written to `.part` files first and marked complete only when every file arrived.\n  `FBRX_VOICE_MODEL_BASE` points downloads at an internal mirror instead.\n* **The microphone** is the only device permission the app window may get, and only for audio from FBRX's own page\n  (camera, screen capture, geolocation, MIDI and the rest are refused for every page). macOS asks once, with the\n  reason shown in the system prompt; the signed app carries the `audio-input` entitlement. The content security\n  policy allows WebAssembly compilation (`wasm-unsafe-eval`) and the `fbrx-voice:` scheme, and nothing else new.\n* **Spoken replies** use the voices installed on the operating system, or the **natural voices**: the Kokoro-82M\n  model (Apache-2.0) and its voice files, downloaded on request from `onnx-community/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX` like the\n  speech models and run in a second worker. Text becomes phonemes with a bundled copy of the CMU Pronouncing\n  Dictionary and HeadTTS's letter-to-sound rules (MIT); no GPL phonemizer (eSpeak) is included. A system voice the\n  list marks *online* belongs to a speech service that receives the text it reads. See\n  [THIRD_PARTY.md](THIRD_PARTY.md).\n* The speech workers use several processor threads, which needs `SharedArrayBuffer`: the app enables it with\n  Chromium's `SharedArrayBuffer` feature switch. Its windows only show FBRX's own pages (the code lab's JavaScript\n  runner is a separate, network-blocked window), so the cross-site timing concern behind cross-origin isolation does\n  not arise.\n* **Thinking** that a model shares (Claude's summarized thinking, `reasoning_content` from llama.cpp or vLLM,\n  OpenRouter's `reasoning`, Ollama's `thinking`, or a leading `` block) is stored with the message and shown\n  to the person, but never added to the conversation as text. (Claude's own signed thinking blocks go back to Claude\n  unchanged, as its API requires; they never go to another model.) Thinking can be hidden in Settings →\n  Agent (`ai.showThinking`). The work budget's step limit is part of the policy (`ai.maxStepsPerRun`), so an\n  organization can fix it; the answer length and context sizes are ordinary settings an organization can lock."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › The AI's memory and personal details","x":"The agent's memory (Settings → Memory) lives in FBRX's database on the computer and\nis never synced or sent anywhere. A backup made for FBRX Command leaves it out (and vacuums the copy so nothing of it\nremains in free pages); a backup the person makes, encrypted with their passphrase, keeps it, so moving to a new\ncomputer keeps it too.\n\n* **Never stored**: national ID / Social Security, driver's license, passport and tax ID numbers, bank account,\n  routing and IBAN numbers, full payment card numbers (13–19 digits passing the Luhn check) and security codes, and\n  passwords, PINs and security answers. They are found by shape and nearby words and\n  removed before a fact is saved, whether the person, the agent or the learning step wrote it; the short-term summary\n  of recent chats is scrubbed the same way.\n* **Private kinds** (addresses, location, email and phone, birthday, cards by last four digits) are stored only when\n  the person allows that kind, and are sent only to providers marked local. With a cloud provider the agent gets\n  general facts or nothing (the person's choice), never private facts and never the last 24 hours.\n* **Online tools**: when a tool goes online (network risk, a connector, or a call with URLs), the guardian blocks any\n  identity or money number in its input as **critical** (refused, never sent), and a remembered private value in its\n  input as a **warning** that needs approval.\n* Memory is off on student and child computers. Adding, changing and forgetting memories is for the person at the\n  computer (not the Local API or FBRX Command)."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Folders the AI may use","x":"`filesystem.allowedRoots` is edited from Governance on the computer (local policy only: a managed policy cannot be\nchanged there). System folders and FBRX's data folder are refused. The agent's `files.request_access` tool only asks;\nthe folder is added when the person picks it in the window, and the request lapses after the approval timeout."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Single sign-on to FBRX Command","x":"* Standard OpenID Connect authorization code flow with **PKCE**, `state` and `nonce`; nothing about the sign-in\n  travels in the browser except a one-time code (one use, one minute) that the console trades for its session.\n* The ID token's signature is checked against the provider's published keys, along with its issuer, audience\n  (the organization's own client ID) and expiry. Google sign-ins must have a verified address from the\n  organization's Workspace (`hd`); Microsoft connections are pinned to one Entra directory (never `common`).\n* The email domain must be one the connection lists. A connection signs people only into **its own organization**,\n  never the platform operator or anyone in another organization, so an organization's identity provider cannot be\n  used to reach someone else's.\n* Client secrets are encrypted at rest with the master key and never returned by the API. Only owners (and the\n  platform operator) can add or change connections, and every change, sign-in and refusal is in the audit log.\n* *Require single sign-on* turns off passwords for everyone in the organization except owners, who keep password +\n  TOTP as the way back in. Accounts created for single sign-on have no password at all."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › FBRX Command with its own certificate","x":"FBRX Command installed on a home or school computer (`Install FBRX Command`, network mode) has no domain name, so no\npublic authority can vouch for its HTTPS certificate. It makes its own (ECDSA P-256, kept in `data/tls/`, the same\nacross updates) and computers **pin** it:\n\n* A computer only joins an `https://` address whose certificate is publicly trusted, or whose SHA-256 fingerprint it\n  was given: in a provisioning file, Mac profile or Windows script from FBRX Command, in a license key that joins the\n  tenant, or by the person comparing what FBRX shows with **Deploy & enroll**. Without one, joining stops with\n  \"uses its own certificate\"; with a different one, nothing is sent.\n* After joining, every request and the live connection to FBRX Command go over a TLS socket that checks the\n  fingerprint at `secureConnect`, **before** the device credential is written. A replaced certificate stops the\n  connection (the computer reports it rather than trusting the new one).\n* Plain `http://` is accepted only for FBRX Command on the same computer (`localhost`). The console on FBRX Command's\n  own computer is on `http://127.0.0.1:`, bound to the loopback interface only.\n* The Windows script FBRX Command generates pins the same fingerprint when it downloads the installer from FBRX\n  Command. When the current release comes from FBRX's downloads instead (nothing as new uploaded to FBRX Command), it\n  downloads over ordinary HTTPS from there."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Network environments (UniFi)","x":"* The UniFi API key is stored in the vault as an internal secret (`fbrx.netenv.`): never listed, never readable by\n  the agent, the Local API or remote commands, and redacted from tool output.\n* Consoles with a self-signed certificate are trusted by SHA-256 fingerprint after the person compares it. Every later\n  connection uses a TLS socket that checks the fingerprint at `secureConnect`, **before** the request (and its key) is\n  written; a different certificate stops the connection and marks the environment with an error.\n* Only Endpoint Ultra can use environments (checked in the core, not just the UI). Reading is a `read` tool; restarting a\n  device is `execute` and making guest codes `write`, so they go through policy and approvals. Saving, removing,\n  restarting and making codes from the API are reserved for the person at the computer."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Antivirus and FBRX Shield","x":"* FBRX reads other antivirus products' state (Windows Security Center, or their files on a Mac or Linux) and only runs\n  command-line scanners it knows, with fixed arguments; it never changes another product's settings.\n* Quarantine moves a file into FBRX's data folder encrypted with AES-256-CTR under a random key per file (kept in\n  FBRX's database), so it cannot run; restoring checks it against its SHA-256 before trusting it.\n* FBRX Shield never scans FBRX's own data folder. Choosing the antivirus, starting scans and acting on findings are\n  reserved for the person at the computer (not the Local API, remote commands or mesh peers); the agent can read and\n  scan (report only), and quarantining is a `write` tool that goes through approvals.\n* The threat feed is fetched over HTTPS under the network policy; an abuse.ch key, if needed, is a vault secret.\n* FBRX Shield checks downloads and scans on demand; it does not block programs as they start, and it does not replace\n  Microsoft Defender on Windows. See [SHIELD.md](SHIELD.md)."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Calendar","x":"* Outlook / Microsoft 365 sign-in is authorization code with PKCE in the person's own browser, back to a one-time\n  loopback listener (127.0.0.1 and ::1 only, one request with the expected `state`, closed after use or 10 minutes).\n  FBRX never handles the password.\n* The refresh token, and a calendar link (which is itself a credential), are internal vault secrets\n  (`fbrx.calendar.`); access tokens live only in memory. The agent, plugins, the Local API and remote commands\n  cannot read them.\n* Reading is a `read` tool; adding an event is `write` and goes through policy and approvals; it never invites\n  anyone. Plugins need the `calendar` permission and get no meeting links or organizers. Event titles are marked\n  private for presenter-safe mode.\n* Adding, removing, signing in, deleting events and reading events through the API are reserved for the person at\n  the computer. See [CALENDAR.md](CALENDAR.md)."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Presenter-safe mode","x":"While it is on, the core drops desktop notifications (an `error` one becomes a content-free \"needs your attention\"),\nthe renderer suppresses toasts and blurs elements marked private, the clipboard history window masks entries, and the\ngoose cannot appear. It hides things from an audience; it is not access control."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Help desk","x":"* Tickets go from a computer to FBRX Command over its device credential and are stored per organization. A computer can\n  read its own tickets; only computers an admin marked as **receivers** can read the queue or change status and\n  priority (enforced by FBRX Command). Admins need `helpdesk.read` / `helpdesk.manage`.\n* The optional computer summary holds the device name, OS, app version, uptime, CPU and memory use, free space, IP,\n  gateway and DNS, and failing FBRX services; no files, chats or credentials. People can untick it.\n* Creating, replying to and updating tickets are reserved for the person at the computer; the agent's\n  `helpdesk.send_ticket` is a `write` tool that waits for approval. A computer can open at most 20 tickets an hour."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Student computers (FBRX OS Education)","x":"* The audience comes from FBRX Command (token, group or device). A computer may ask to be a student computer when it\n  enrolls, never a staff one; student settings (easter eggs, mesh, local API, AI provider and prompt) are locked.\n* On student computers the agent gets no tools, and the tool gate refuses every tool call with origin `agent`, so a\n  model cannot reach tools by naming one.\n* Safety concerns (self-harm, being hurt) are recognized locally. The student gets a caring answer with 911, 988 and the\n  Childhelp hotline; FBRX Command gets a `Student safety` device alert with the computer name and the category only.\n  The message is not transmitted. Alerts queue while offline and are delivered on reconnect."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Audit and tamper evidence","x":"Every tool decision, approval, configuration change, vault access, plugin event, backup and remote command is\nwritten to a hash-chained audit log on the device; the chain head travels with each heartbeat, so tampering is\nvisible centrally. The control plane keeps its own hash-chained log of every admin action. Audit entries can be\nstreamed to a SIEM via webhook connectors (device) and webhooks (control plane)."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Licensing integrity","x":"Licenses are Ed25519-signed by your control plane's private key; the public key is compiled into your desktop\nbuilds. A customer running their own control plane cannot mint licenses your builds accept.\n\nA license that joins its FBRX Command tenant carries that tenant's address and an enrollment token inside the signed\npayload, so treat such keys like enrollment tokens: the token is limited to one use per seat and expires with the\nlicense, and revoking the token in **Deploy & enroll** stops new joins without touching computers already enrolled.\nJoining happens only after the key verifies, only once per license, never while the computer already belongs to a\ntenant, and a deliberate *Disconnect* is respected."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Supply chain and updates","x":"* Desktop builds are code-signed (and notarized on macOS) when they are released, on Windows with a trusted signing\n  service or a certificate. A **signed** Windows copy installs an update only when Windows reports a valid\n  Authenticode signature from the same publisher (subject CN) as itself; an unsigned copy (built from source) relies on\n  the SHA-512 in the authenticated feed, as before, and accepts the first signed update.\n* Update feeds are served per device by your control plane over authenticated HTTPS; files carry SHA-512 checksums\n  verified by electron-updater.\n* **Electron's fuses** are switched when the app is packaged:\n  `NODE_OPTIONS` and `--inspect`-style arguments are ignored, so nothing can inject code into FBRX or attach a\n  debugger by starting it differently; the app's code loads only from `app.asar`, checked at start against the hash\n  built into the executable (Windows and macOS); cookies are encrypted with the system keychain. Node mode\n  (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE`) stays available because the plugin worker and the MCP bridge for other AI apps run that way.\n* **Dependencies** are checked with `npm audit` before each release; known issues in shipped packages are fixed by\n  updating them (2.2.0: the MCP SDK, nodemailer 10, systeminformation, zod, fastify and others). Electron follows its\n  newest patch release.\n* **FBRX Server updates itself only when asked.** `sudo fbrx-server update check` reads the same `release.json`\n  (its `server` entry) and downloads the bundle only from a GitHub release (any host only when the server's\n  administrator names another release list), refuses it unless it matches the SHA-256 in the list or the `.sha256`\n  published next to it, and unpacks it without changing what runs. `update commit` (root) keeps the running version\n  aside, runs the new version's installer, and goes back by itself when the console does not answer afterwards.\n* **New versions offered from the repository.** The desktop app reads `release.json` from the public FBRX repository\n  (`raw.githubusercontent.com/samfoxhuntress/FBRX/HEAD/release.json`) a minute and a half after it starts and every\n  six hours, only while internet access is allowed and **Check for new versions automatically** is on. A newer\n  version raises one alert; nothing is installed unless the person chooses **Update now** (`release.install`,\n  reserved for them). That downloads the zip named in the manifest (only from github.com or codeload.github.com),\n  checks it holds that version, copies it over the folder FBRX was installed from (recorded by the setup wizard in\n  `install-source.json`; `.fbrx-keys`, `.fbrx-setup`, `node_modules` and `.git` are never touched) and opens the\n  setup wizard there in its own window, which builds, installs and reopens the app as a manual update would.\n  `FBRX_RELEASE_MANIFEST` points the check at another manifest (an internal mirror, or tests). With **Install new\n  versions by itself** (or an organization's *Install automatically*) the same steps run without asking, once per\n  version, only when the computer has been idle for five minutes, nobody is presenting and the agent is idle. FBRX\n  Command's **Update now** (`update.install`, a privileged command) does the same from the repository, or installs the\n  release Command serves.\n* Plugin packages pushed from the console are verified against their SHA-256 before installation.\n* The local runtime downloads llama.cpp from the official `ggml-org/llama.cpp` GitHub releases and models with\n  SHA-256 verification where the catalog lists a checksum."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Operational recommendations","x":"1. Enable MFA for every console user; use API keys with the narrowest role for automation.\n2. Keep `FBRX_CP_MASTER_KEY`, `FBRX_CP_JWT_SECRET` and `FBRX_LICENSE_PRIVATE_KEY` in a secrets manager and back up\n   the control plane data directory.\n3. Push `FBRX_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE` as a tenant credential and enable scheduled backups.\n4. Set a recovery passphrase on every workstation (or rely on organization snapshots).\n5. Start new policies in `audit` mode, review **Alerts & events**, then switch to `enforce`.\n6. Restrict cloud AI providers by policy where data residency requires it; the local runtime keeps everything on\n   the device."},{"d":"docs/SECURITY.md","t":"Security model › Reporting a vulnerability","x":"Send details to your security contact (set this before distributing builds) rather than opening a public issue."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual","x":"**FBRX Server** is FBRX OS for servers: Debian 13 set up the FBRX way, running **FBRX Virtual**, the FBRX hypervisor.\nFBRX Virtual runs virtual machines on KVM (through libvirt) and is managed from a web console on port **9443**:\nvirtual machines and their screens, storage and ISOs, networks, a live **hardware map** of the server, and the server\nitself (health, power and **BIOS settings**) through its management controller.\n\nWith the **ai** role (on by default) the server also runs the **FBRX core**: an AI agent on **FBRX Mesh** that works\nwith your other FBRX computers through **Mesh Assist**, lending its AI when theirs runs out of steps or credits,\nborrowing theirs, and, as a **controller**, handing out work to them. It is managed from the same console\n(*Mesh & AI*, below).\n\nWith the **gate** role the server routes and protects your network (**FBRX Gate**: firewall, VLANs, DHCP and DNS, VPN,\ntraffic priority and Prefer Mesh, [GATE.md](GATE.md)); with **minidome** it also watches it for threats (**FBRX\nMiniDome**, [MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md)). Same console, *Gate* pages.\n\nWith the **time** role the server makes image backups (**FBRX Time**, [TIME.md](TIME.md)): its virtual machines on a\nschedule while they run, and the computers that send it their whole-computer images. It checks every image the way a\nrestore would: it opens it as a throwaway virtual machine on a private test network that reaches nothing else,\nconfirms it starts and comes online, then shuts it down and deletes it; with the ai role, the server's AI explains a\ncheck that fails. Same console, *FBRX Time* page.\n\nWith the **files** role the server is a **file server**: shared folders for your network, served by the server itself\nor by one of its virtual machines (a NAS system, Windows Server). Their traffic can go first, and their files are\nbacked up to a NAS or another server on a schedule. Same console, *File server* page.\n\nWith the **command** role the server runs your organization's **FBRX Command**: the computers that join it, their\nsettings and policy, licenses, updates, backups and help desk, with its own console on port **8787**. It runs day and\nnight, so computers stay in touch with it, and it updates with the server. A server with the ai role also joins an\nFBRX Command (its own or another) and takes its settings and policy from there (*FBRX Command* page, below).\n\nRoles can be added and turned off later, from the console's *Roles* page or `fbrx-server roles` (below).\n\n> FBRX Server 1.0. It runs real virtual machines and backs them up, and both are tested against a real hypervisor.\n> FBRX Command runs on the server, and servers join it; clusters come later (see [LINEUP.md](LINEUP.md))."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Install › A. From the FBRX Server ISO (wipes the server)","x":"1. Get the ISO: it comes with **FBRX Endpoint** for now (*Lab → FBRX Server*, with the bundle and these guides), the\n   **FBRX Server ISO** workflow in GitHub Actions builds it (artifact `fbrx-server-iso`), or build it yourself on Linux:\n   `scripts/server/bundle.sh --with-node && scripts/server/build-iso.sh` (needs `xorriso`).\n2. Boot the server from it: write it to a USB stick (`dd`, or any image writer; if the writer offers a choice, pick\n   the raw/DD image mode), or attach it as virtual media through the server's management controller (its remote\n   console), then boot once from it (the one-time boot menu, often F11 or F12). In Hyper-V, use a generation 2\n   machine.\n3. Pick **Install FBRX Server**. The installer asks only:\n   * which disk to install on, and to confirm erasing it (it asks every time it is about to write),\n   * the time zone,\n   * the administrator account's name (suggested: **`fbrx`**) and its password. There is no root password; the\n     administrator uses `sudo`.\n   Everything else is answered: US English, DHCP, host name `fbrx-server`, LVM over the whole disk, SSH server.\n4. It copies FBRX Server onto the new system and restarts. At this **first start FBRX Server finishes its setup** (the\n   hypervisor, FBRX Virtual, the AI role): a few minutes, with its progress on the screen, before the login prompt.\n   The screen then shows where to open the console, the first-time setup code, and which account to sign in with.\n\nThe ISO is Debian's own network installer with FBRX Server added, so the server needs internet access while installing\nand at its first start. If the first start could not finish (no internet, say), the login screen says so: sign in,\nfix the network, and run `sudo bash /opt/fbrx-installer/firstboot.sh` (it also tries again at every start until it\nfinishes). Everything it did is in `/var/log/fbrx-server-install.log`."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Install › B. On a Debian 13 server you already have","x":"```bash\ntar -xzf fbrx-server-1.0.0.tar.gz          # from the FBRX website, or FBRX Endpoint (Lab → FBRX Server)\nsudo bash fbrx-server-1.0.0/install.sh\n```\n\n`install.sh` installs QEMU/KVM, libvirt, UEFI firmware (OVMF) and the software TPM (swtpm), Node.js 22 (kept in\n`/opt/fbrx-server/node`, not system-wide), FBRX Virtual and (ai role) the FBRX core, turns on the IOMMU in the kernel\noptions (for device passthrough, after one reboot) and starts the `fbrx-virtual` and `fbrx-core` services.\n\n| Option | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--roles virtual,ai` | What the server does (below). `--roles virtual` installs the hypervisor alone; `--roles virtual,ai,time` adds image backups; `--roles gate,minidome,ai` makes a gate without virtual machines. Change them later with `fbrx-server roles` or the console's *Roles* page. |\n| `--gate-wan eno1 --gate-lan eno2` | The gate's internet port and the port to your network (gate role; see [GATE.md](GATE.md#install)). Your network avoids the addresses the internet side uses; `--gate-lan-address`, `--gate-wan-address` and `--gate-wan-gateway` choose them yourself. `--gate-manage ` keeps the console and SSH reachable from a private network on the internet side. |\n| `--reboot` | Restart at the end when a change waits for it (the gate taking its ports, the bridge). |\n| `--command-address command.example.lan` | The address computers use to reach FBRX Command (command role): a name in your DNS, or an address. By default the one it has, else this server's address (the gate's address in your network, on a gate). |\n| `--bridge eno1` | Makes bridge `br0` on that port so virtual machines sit straight on your network (applies after a reboot). Only for a port set up with DHCP; for a static address, see *Networks* below. |\n| `--port 9443` | The console's port. |\n| `--uninstall [--purge]` | Takes FBRX Server off again. Virtual machines and their disks, and the ai role's mesh pairings and keys, stay unless `--purge`. |"},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Install › Server roles","x":"An FBRX Server announces its roles to the other computers on FBRX Mesh (Mesh Assist shows them when it picks a\nhelper, so work can go to the right server).\n\n| Role | What it runs | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `virtual` | FBRX Virtual: the hypervisor (virtual machines) | Default |\n| `ai` | The FBRX core: the agent, FBRX Mesh and Mesh Assist (`fbrx-core` service) | Default |\n| `gate` | FBRX Gate: routing, firewall, VLANs, DHCP and DNS, VPN, traffic priority ([GATE.md](GATE.md)) | First stage |\n| `minidome` | FBRX MiniDome: threat detection through the gate ([MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md)); needs `gate` | First stage |\n| `time` | FBRX Time: image backups of virtual machines and computers, each checked by starting it ([TIME.md](TIME.md)); needs `virtual` | New in 1.0 |\n| `command` | FBRX Command: your organization's computers, their settings and policy, licenses and updates, with its own console on port 8787 ([below](#fbrx-command-on-the-server-the-command-role)) | New |\n| `files` | A file server: shared folders (SMB) from the server or one of its virtual machines, priority for their traffic, backups to a NAS ([below](#file-server-the-files-role)) | New |\n| `dns` | Name service for the network | Planned |\n| `directory` | Directory and sign-in for the organization's computers (a domain controller) | Planned |\n\nThe web console comes with every role (it is FBRX Virtual's service, with or without virtual machines). The installer\nrefuses a planned role for now and says so."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Install › Changing roles","x":"In the console, **Roles** lists every role with what it does: switch the ones you want on or off and choose *Change\nthe roles* (administrators). Adding FBRX Gate asks for its two ports first; turning it off warns that the network loses\nits router. From the server's command line:\n\n```\nfbrx-server roles                         this server's roles, and how the last change went\nsudo fbrx-server roles add time           add a role\nsudo fbrx-server roles remove ai          turn one off (its data stays)\nsudo fbrx-server roles set virtual,ai,time   exactly these roles\n```\n\nBoth run the copy of the installer the server keeps (`/opt/fbrx-server/bundle`, kept by every install and update\nfrom 1.0) with the new roles: it installs what an added role needs and stops what a removed one ran, keeping the port,\nthe settings and the gate's ports. The console restarts at the end (sign in again if it asks); the run is logged in\n`/var/log/fbrx-server-roles.log` and its last lines show on the *Roles* page. A server on 0.1.x keeps no copy yet:\nupdate it once (below), or run `install.sh --roles …` from a bundle."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Before you install: the server's own settings","x":"Any 64-bit (x86-64) PC or server works. Before (or after) installing:\n\n1. **Firmware (BIOS/UEFI).** Turn on hardware virtualization (VT-x or AMD-V; fast virtual machines) and the IOMMU\n   (VT-d or AMD-Vi; giving PCI devices to virtual machines). Boot in **UEFI** mode. If there is a power or system\n   profile, pick **performance** (processors stay at full speed). On servers with network cards that split into\n   virtual ones, turn on **SR-IOV**.\n2. **Management controller** (most rack servers have one, a small computer that runs even when the server is off):\n   give it an address on your network, change its default password, and make a separate administrator account for\n   FBRX Virtual. FBRX Virtual's *Server & BIOS* page then shows its health and changes the firmware settings above\n   for you (any Redfish controller; its *BIOS settings* list marks them **Recommended for FBRX Virtual**).\n3. **Disks.** A RAID controller can offer the disks as one volume (pick that volume in the installer) or pass them\n   through as they are (HBA or non-RAID mode, for software RAID or ZFS later)."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › First sign-in","x":"Open `https://:9443` from another computer, or in FBRX Endpoint add it under **Lab → FBRX Server →\nYour servers** and open it there (a browser built into FBRX, with its own sign-ins; see below). FBRX Virtual makes its\nown certificate, so the browser warns the first time: check that the fingerprint matches what the server shows\n(`fbrx-server fingerprint`), then continue.\nEnter the **setup code** (on the server's screen, or `sudo fbrx-server setup-code`) and make the first administrator.\n\nRoles: **administrators** do everything (users, networks, deleting machines, device passthrough, interrupt placement,\nthe BIOS); **operators** run virtual machines (create, start, screens, snapshots, ISOs); **viewers** look. Every change\nand sign-in is in the audit log."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Using FBRX Virtual","x":"* **Virtual machines.** *New virtual machine* picks sensible devices for the operating system: Windows gets SATA disks\n  and an Intel network card (installs with no extra drivers), Secure Boot and a TPM for Windows 11; Linux gets the fast\n  virtio devices. Disks are thin qcow2 files in `/var/lib/fbrx-virtual/images`.\n* **Screen.** Each running machine's screen opens in the browser (VNC relayed by FBRX Virtual; the VNC ports themselves\n  only listen on the server). Ctrl+Alt+Del and full screen are in the toolbar, and **Paste** sends text from your\n  computer: it shows your clipboard (or a box to paste into), then types it into the machine as on a US keyboard, so it\n  works anywhere, even on a login prompt or a BIOS screen. Click where the text should go first.\n* **Snapshots.** Machines with BIOS firmware are snapshotted live, memory included. UEFI machines keep their firmware\n  settings outside the disk, which QEMU cannot put in a snapshot, so they are snapshotted (and restored) while off.\n* **Changes.** Processors and memory change at the machine's next start (the page says so); the CD drive, the disk size\n  and processor placement change right away.\n* **ISOs.** Upload installers, or have the server download them from a web address (faster).\n* **Networks.** Each network is a card showing the way out: machines → the network or switch → where it leads, and\n  which machines are plugged in. `default` is a private network with internet through the server: machines reach the\n  internet, nothing on your network reaches them. To put machines **on your network** (next to your other devices,\n  reachable from them, one more device for your router or gate):\n  * **with FBRX Gate on this server**, turn on *Virtual machines can join this network* for a gate network (your LAN,\n    a VLAN) and commit: it becomes a switch machines plug into ([GATE.md](GATE.md#virtual-machines-on-your-networks));\n  * **otherwise**, a bridge on the port cabled to your network: the installer's `--bridge eno1`, or by hand.\n  Any number of machines share a switch or bridge with the server itself. Giving a network card to one machine\n  (*Hardware map*, passthrough) is different: that machine alone uses the card, at full speed.\n  Pick the network when creating a machine, or move one under *its Settings → Network* (running machines move at\n  once; the MAC address stays). For a server with a static address, add a bridge in `/etc/network/interfaces` yourself:\n\n  ```\n  auto eno1\n  iface eno1 inet manual\n\n  auto br0\n  iface br0 inet static\n      address 192.168.1.10/24\n      gateway 192.168.1.1\n      bridge_ports eno1\n      bridge_stp off\n      bridge_fd 0\n  ```"},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Mesh & AI (the ai role)","x":"The FBRX core runs as its own unprivileged account (`fbrx-core`, no login, no sudo) with only `/var/lib/fbrx-core`\nwritable, so its agent can look around the server but not change it as root; its tools follow FBRX policy and ask\nbefore anything risky, like on any FBRX computer. It listens for FBRX Mesh on port **47800** and for its console on\n`127.0.0.1:47821` only.\n\n*Mesh & AI* in the console:\n\n* **Mesh Assist:** when this server brings other computers' AI in (out of steps, AI provider out of credits or\n  unreachable, the agent consulting another), whether it lends its own (never, ask an operator here first, or\n  automatically), what its agent may do while helping, how much help at once and at what priority. **This server is a\n  controller** makes its requests run without asking on computers that gave it the Controller permission (urgent ones\n  may stop lower-priority help there). **This server's own requests go first** (on by default): its console's chats,\n  MiniDome's and What If's explanations, its automations start on its AI before help for the mesh, and the work queue\n  shows who the AI is answering and who waits. *Who can help?* lists the paired computers with their AI, load and roles;\n  *Hand out work* sends a task to the best helper, every computer, or one; requests waiting for a yes, and all help\n  asked and given (with the answers and follow-up questions), are on the same tab. See [MESH.md](MESH.md).\n* **Work queue:** the requests of every computer that asks this server's AI, as they run and wait their turn, with the\n  person and the computer that asked. Operators run one next, change its priority or cancel it; administrators pause\n  the queue.\n* **Log** (administrators): every request for 180 days, by person and by computer, with how it ended and who cancelled\n  it; filter it and export it as CSV.\n* **Computers on the mesh:** turn FBRX Mesh on or off, show a pairing code for another computer (on that computer:\n  *Mesh & phone → Add a device → Enter a code*), join another computer's code, and set what each paired computer may do here,\n  including **Help with AI** and **Controller**.\n* **Models on this server:** run the AI right here, with FBRX's built-in runtime (llama.cpp), so the server is the\n  big agent the other computers lean on (see below). Paired computers may also think with the model itself while their\n  own agent keeps working on them (*AI models → Your other computers' AI* there; see\n  [MESH.md](MESH.md#think-with-your-fbrx-servers-ai)): the same lending setting and Help with AI permission decide.\n* **AI provider:** what this server's agent runs on: a cloud provider with an API key (kept encrypted in the core's\n  vault, never shown again), or a model on your network (Ollama or any OpenAI-compatible server, by address)."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Mesh & AI (the ai role) › The AI sets itself up","x":"On the first start of the ai role, the server gets its own AI ready without anyone choosing: it installs the runtime,\npicks the best model the hardware runs well, downloads it and makes it the server's AI. With graphics cards, the\nbiggest model they hold (several cards add up); on processors alone, a model that answers quickly there (gpt-oss 120B\nwith 96 GB of memory or more, Qwen3 30B-A3B Instruct 2507 with 32 GB or more), otherwise the biggest everyday model\nthat fits; always leaving 20 GB of the disk free. *Fabrix* in the\nconsole shows the progress (and *Try again* to administrators when a download fails). It happens once, and only when\nnobody chose an AI for the server yet: a provider or model set by an administrator is never overruled, and after that\nthe choice is theirs (below)."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Mesh & AI (the ai role) › A bigger AI on the server","x":"Endpoints run small models (or a cloud provider). A server with plenty of memory, or a graphics card, can run a much\nlarger one and lend it over FBRX Mesh: a computer whose own AI is too small for a job, runs out of steps or stops\nanswering asks Mesh Assist, which hands the work to the server (*Mesh Assist* tab: who may ask, how many at once, at\nwhat priority).\n\n1. *Mesh & AI → Models on this server* shows the server's memory, threads and graphics cards, and for every model\n   whether it fits the graphics cards (llama.cpp spreads a model over all of them), fits in memory (processors only:\n   slower, fine for background work), only just fits, or is too big. Leave room for the virtual machines.\n   `sudo fbrx-server ai recommend` names the best one for this server.\n2. **Install the runtime** (a small download from the llama.cpp project's own releases; the graphics-card build when\n   the server has a card it can use).\n3. **Download** a model. Besides the desktop ones there are models *for servers*:\n   * **Qwen3 30B-A3B Instruct 2507** (~19 GB): only 3B of its 30B parameters work at a time, so it answers quickly\n     without a graphics card, and it reads long material (a 128K-token context here). The one to start with on a\n     server with 32 GB or more. (The earlier **Qwen3 30B-A3B**, which thinks before it answers, is there too.)\n   * **Qwen3 32B** (~20 GB): stronger and careful, at home on a 24 GB graphics card; slow on processors alone.\n   * **Llama 3.3 70B Instruct** (~43 GB): for 64 GB+ of memory or 48 GB of graphics memory.\n   * **gpt-oss 120B** (~63 GB): the most capable model that still answers quickly on processors (5B of its 117B\n     parameters at a time), with a 128K-token context. For 96 GB+ of memory or 80 GB of graphics memory.\n   * **Qwen3 235B-A22B Instruct 2507** (~143 GB): the largest, close to the best open models. For 192 GB+ of memory;\n     22B parameters at a time make it slow on processors alone, quick with graphics cards to help.\n\n   A download checks first that the disk has room for it and 20 GB to spare, resumes where it stopped when interrupted,\n   and is checked before use. The biggest models come in several files: they download as one.\n4. **Use it**: the model becomes this server's AI (the built-in runtime turns on and becomes the default provider).\n   The runtime starts at the first question and stops after a while unused. `sudo fbrx-server ai download  --use`\n   does both in one go.\n\n**Any GGUF model on Hugging Face.** *A model of your own → From Hugging Face* (or `sudo fbrx-server ai add`) takes the\nmodel's repository, `owner/name` or its address, with its quantization after a colon when it is not Q4_K_M\n(`bartowski/Qwen_Qwen3-14B-GGUF:Q6_K`). FBRX finds its files (every part of a model in several), adds it to the list\nand downloads it like the others, checking every file against the checksum Hugging Face publishes. Public models only:\nFBRX does not sign in to Hugging Face. Its tools work when the model's chat template supports tool calls. *Take off\nthe list* (or `fbrx-server ai delete`) removes it again.\n\nA GGUF file of your own works too: copy it to the server (not into a home folder: the core cannot see those), then\n`sudo fbrx-server ai import /srv/model.gguf \"Its name\"`.\n\n**The context window** is how much the model keeps in mind at once: the conversation, a long log, several documents.\nOn **Auto** (the default) the server gives it as much as it has room for, up to 128K tokens and never more than the\nmodel was made for: in memory, after the model and a quarter left for the system and the virtual machines; on the\ngraphics cards, what they have left after the model, so the model stays on them. Kept in 8 bits with flash attention,\nit takes about half the memory it otherwise would. *Mesh & AI → Models on this server* shows it and lets\nadministrators fix it (16K to 256K tokens; `sudo fbrx-server ai context 64k`, or `auto`); the runtime restarts with it\nat the next question. A long context window only helps models made for it: the 2507 Qwen3 models, gpt-oss and Llama 3.3\nread 128K tokens, the earlier Qwen3 models 32K.\n\nFrom the command line (as root):\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fbrx-server ai` | the runtime, the model in use, its context window, memory, graphics and disk |\n| `fbrx-server ai models` | every model, what fits this server, its context, which are downloaded |\n| `fbrx-server ai recommend` | the best model for this server, and the command that installs it |\n| `fbrx-server ai install-runtime` | install or update the runtime |\n| `fbrx-server ai download  [--use]` | download a model with its progress, speed and time left (Ctrl+C stops watching; the download goes on); `--use` then makes it this server's AI |\n| `fbrx-server ai download  [--use]` | the same for a GGUF model on Hugging Face |\n| `fbrx-server ai add  [name]` | put a Hugging Face model on the list |\n| `fbrx-server ai use ` | make it this server's AI |\n| `fbrx-server ai delete ` | delete a model (one added from Huggin"},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Mesh & AI (the ai role) › A bigger AI on the server (2)","x":"g Face also leaves the list) |\n| `fbrx-server ai context [auto\\|64k\\|128k\\|]` | the context window |\n| `fbrx-server ai import  [name]` | a GGUF file copied to the server |\n| `fbrx-server ai start`, `stop`, `logs` | the runtime itself |\n\n**Graphics cards.** The installer gives the core's account the `render` and `video` groups and, when it finds an\nAMD, Intel or NVIDIA card, installs the Vulkan libraries. AMD and Intel cards work with Debian's own drivers; an\nNVIDIA card also needs NVIDIA's driver (Debian's `nvidia-driver`, from the non-free section). Without a usable card the\nruntime uses the processors.\n\nViewers see the models; operators start and stop the runtime; administrators install the runtime, download, delete\nand choose models (they fill the disk and decide what the server's AI is).\n\n**Who may do what.** Viewers see it all. Operators answer Mesh Assist's requests for help and hand out work.\nAdministrators change the settings, pair computers, set permissions, choose the provider, save keys and answer any\napproval. Every change is in FBRX Virtual's audit log and, with the console user's name, in the core's own.\n\n**How the console reaches the core.** At every start the core writes a new random **console token** to\n`/var/lib/fbrx-core/console.token` (readable by root only); FBRX Virtual, which runs as root, reads it and calls the\ncore's Local API on `127.0.0.1` with it, as the person signed in to the console. FBRX Virtual passes on only the\ncalls the page makes, with the role each needs, and only the settings above (never the core's policy, Local API or\nsystem prompt). The console token can never read a secret back or the Local API's automation tokens."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Fabrix in the console","x":"*Fabrix* (in the console's Server section, with the ai role) is a chat with the server's own agent: ask how the\nserver is doing, which virtual machines use what, what filled the disk, or hand it a job. Its answer shows as it is\nwritten, with each step it takes; Stop ends a run. It works under the server's FBRX policy like on any FBRX computer:\nit looks things up freely, and what needs a yes waits for an administrator (in the chat, or under Mesh & AI). A new\nchat stays offline unless *May use the internet* is on.\n\n**Attach** (or drop, or paste) files and pictures for it to read, up to ten a message and 25 MB each: they go to the\nserver, where its agent reads them. What it makes (documents, drawings) shows under the step that made it, pictures as\na thumbnail, and **Download** brings a file to the computer you are on; an attached file downloads the same way. It\ngives out only what it made or was given, nothing else on the server. Drawings come as SVG (the server has no window\nto turn them into PNG); pictures from a description need a picture maker, which the server does not run yet.\n\n**Ask the AI**, all over the console, opens a new chat about what you are looking at, with its details already in the\nbox: a virtual machine, a warning on the overview, a device in the hardware map, a storage pool, the hardware event\nlog, a change of roles that did not finish, the gate and its firewall, and what is on the terminal's screen. Add your\nown question or send it as it is; nothing is sent until you do.\n\nOperators and administrators chat; administrators also delete chats. FBRX Virtual's audit log records who asked and\nhow long the question was, never what it said, and of an attached file only its name, type and size.\n\nIf a page of the console ever fails to draw, it says what went wrong, with **Try again** and **Reload**; the rest of\nthe console keeps working."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › FBRX Command on the server (the command role)","x":"The **FBRX Command** page (Server section) shows where FBRX Command is, the fingerprint of its certificate and, until\nsomeone sets it up, its **setup token** (administrators only; `sudo fbrx-server command` shows the same).\n\n1. **Set it up.** Open its address (`https://:8787`; *Open FBRX Command* on the page). FBRX Command\n   makes its own certificate, so the browser warns the first time: compare the fingerprint with the page's. Enter\n   the setup token, your organization (Work, School or Home) and the first administrator. The rest is FBRX Command\n   as in [ADMIN_CONSOLE.md](ADMIN_CONSOLE.md).\n2. **Computers join it** from *Deploy & enroll* in FBRX Command, as on any FBRX Command: an enrollment token, a\n   provisioning file, a device-manager script. They pin its certificate's fingerprint when they join.\n3. **Keep its address.** Computers keep the address they joined with: give the server a fixed address in your router,\n   or a name in your DNS (`install.sh --command-address ` sets it; computers that joined before keep the old one).\n\nOn a gate, a new gate lets your network reach FBRX Command from the start (a firewall rule, *FBRX Command for your\nnetwork*). On a gate set up before the command role, the page says when the rule is missing: add it under *Gate →\nFirewall* (from your network to the gate, TCP 8787, accept).\n\n**This server in FBRX Command.** With the ai role, the page's second card joins this server to an FBRX Command: paste an\nenrollment token from FBRX Command (*Deploy & enroll*). On a server with the command role it joins its own FBRX Command,\ntrusting its certificate by itself; otherwise enter the other FBRX Command's address and fingerprint too. The server's\nFBRX core then shows in FBRX Command as one of the organization's computers and takes its settings and policy from\nthere. *Leave FBRX Command* undoes it.\n\n**Updates and data.** FBRX Command updates with the server (`sudo fbrx-server update`; its *Releases* page says so\ninstead of offering *Update now*), and an update's rollback brings its database and settings back too. Its data\n(database, keys, certificate, uploads) is in `/var/lib/fbrx-command`; turning the role off keeps it, `--uninstall\n--purge` deletes it. Its settings are in `/etc/fbrx-command/fbrx-command.env` (the address, port and certificate; see\nDEPLOYMENT.md → Configuration) and its secrets in `/etc/fbrx-command/secrets.env`, which only root reads (the setup\ntoken; an e-mail server's address with its password goes here too). `sudo systemctl restart fbrx-command` after a\nchange."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › File server (the files role)","x":"The **File server** page (Server section) shares folders with your network. First choose where it serves from:\n\n* **This server.** FBRX Server shares folders itself (Samba, the way Windows shares folders: Windows, macOS and Linux\n  all open them). Each share is a folder in `/srv/fbrx-files`.\n* **A virtual machine.** One of the server's virtual machines is the file server: a NAS system, Windows Server, any\n  machine that shares folders. It keeps its own shares and accounts; FBRX Server gives its traffic priority, can start\n  it with the server, and backs it up. FBRX uses the address the machine reports; type one in when it has a fixed\n  address on your network.\n* **Off.** Nothing is shared. Shares and their files stay, for when you turn it back on."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › File server (the files role) › Shares and accounts (this server)","x":"1. **Add accounts.** People sign in to the shares with these, one each or one shared by a small office. They cannot\n   sign in to the server itself. Passwords are kept by Samba only: nobody can read them back, so give each person\n   theirs (*New password* sets another).\n2. **Make shares.** A name (`office`, `photos`, `scans`), a description, read only or not, and who can open it: the\n   accounts you tick, or every account when you tick none. People open it as `\\\\`; the page shows the\n   path with a *Copy* button. A Mac opens `smb:///` (Finder: Go → Connect to Server).\n3. **On a gate**, your networks need a firewall rule to reach the shares on the gate (TCP 445). The page says so and\n   *Add the firewall rule* adds one for each network to what you are editing; review it with What If on *Changes*, then\n   commit.\n\nStopping a share leaves its files in its folder. A share a backup copies cannot be stopped until that backup is\nchanged or removed."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › File server (the files role) › Priority for its traffic","x":"*Let its traffic go first* marks the file server's traffic (SMB and NFS) with a priority class, so switches that honor\npriority and FBRX Gate's traffic shaping let it go first. For this server it marks what the shares send; for a virtual\nmachine, its traffic as it passes through the server, whether it is on a NAT network, a gate network or bridged to\nyour network. **AF31** (recommended) puts file copies ahead of everyday traffic and behind calls and video meetings;\nthe page says whether the marking is in place. The marking is put back after every restart."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › File server (the files role) › Backups to a NAS","x":"Each backup copies a folder to another device on a schedule (every day, every week, or only when you start it):\n\n* **From** a share on this server, or a folder on another device: the file server machine (`\\\\data`) or\n  anything else that shares folders.\n* **To** a shared folder (`\\nas\backups`, with an account and password) or an NFS export (`nas:/volume1/backups`).\n\nThe backup gets a folder of its own there, named after it: `current` holds a full copy, and each copy after the first\nsends only what changed. Files that changed or were deleted move to `kept/` and stay for as many days as you\nchoose, so a mistake (or ransomware) can be undone: copy them back from there. 0 days keeps only the latest copy.\n\n*Back up now* starts one at once; one backup copies at a time and the others wait their turn. The *Last copy* column\nsays how each went; open it for the details. With the ai role, the server's AI looks at a backup that fails and says\nwhat most likely went wrong and what to try. Operators can start and stop backups; administrators set them up.\n\nThe account a backup signs in with needs to write to the folder on the NAS. Its password is encrypted with the\nserver's key and never shown again: leave it empty when you change the backup to keep it."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › The hardware map","x":"*Hardware map* draws the server as it is wired: the processor sockets (NUMA nodes) with their cores on top, and the\ndevices on each socket's PCIe lanes below (network ports, disk controllers, USB, display), with their link width and\nspeed. Lines show the data moving right now, in (received, read) and out (sent, written), thicker and faster as more\nmoves. Cores glow by how many device interrupts they handle.\n\n**Changing where data goes:**\n\n* **A device's interrupts.** Drag a device onto a socket or a single core (or use the buttons under it) to choose which\n  processors handle its interrupts, which is where its data is first handled. Keep a fast network card's interrupts on\n  the socket it is plugged into, and away from cores busy virtual machines run on. FBRX Virtual remembers the\n  placement and puts it back after every restart. `irqbalance` moves interrupts on its own; turn it off from the page\n  when you place them by hand.\n* **A virtual machine's processors.** Drag a pinned machine onto a socket or core, or set its processors on its\n  *Hardware & placement* tab: it then runs only there (moved right away, kept for later starts).\n* **Device passthrough.** Give a PCI device (a network card, a disk controller, a graphics card) to one virtual machine,\n  which then uses it directly at full speed. Needs VT-d and the IOMMU (the installer turns it on; reboot once). Never\n  give away the network port you reach the console through.\n\nOn a computer without a Linux hardware view (development, the simulated mode) the page shows a sample server."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Server & BIOS (Redfish management controllers)","x":"*Server & BIOS → Connect*: enter the controller's address, check the certificate it presents (compare its fingerprint\nwith the one on the controller's own web page, usually under its network or SSL settings), trust it and sign in. From then on FBRX Virtual\ntalks only to that exact controller, and keeps the password encrypted.\n\n* **Health & power:** model, service tag, BIOS and controller firmware, temperatures, fans, power use; turn the server\n  on, shut it down, restart or force it (the page reminds you every virtual machine stops with it).\n* **BIOS settings:** every setting by group, with the maker's names, choices and help. Changes are **staged** and applied\n  by the server at its next restart (some controllers do this through a configuration job, which the page lists).\n  *Apply at next restart* or *Apply and restart now*; *Throw them away* cancels staged changes. *Next start: BIOS setup*\n  boots into the setup screen once (see it through the controller's remote console).\n* **Event log:** the hardware event log (memory, power supply, fan and temperature events)."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Terminal","x":"*Server → Terminal* (administrators only) is the server's own shell in the browser: bash as root on its Debian, the\nsame as sitting at the server's keyboard or signing in over SSH. It opens in your home folder (`/root`), sees the whole\nsystem, and runs every command line tool on the server, `fbrx-server` and `fbrx-gate` included.\n\n* **Ctrl+C** stops the running command. With text selected, Ctrl+C copies it instead (Ctrl+Shift+C always copies).\n* **Paste** types the text on your clipboard into the shell; **Ctrl+Shift+V** (Cmd+V on a Mac) does the same from the\n  keyboard. Over plain HTTP some browsers keep the clipboard to themselves: then the keyboard shortcut is the way.\n* The terminal follows the size of the window; **Full screen** gives it the whole screen.\n* **End session** closes the shell, and so does `exit`. Closing the tab or signing out ends it too: nothing keeps running\n  after you leave (start long jobs with `systemd-run` or in `tmux` if you need them to carry on).\n* Up to four terminals can be open on a server at once.\n\nEvery session is in *Users & audit* (who opened it, when, and for how long), and what was on the screen is recorded on\nthe server in `/var/lib/fbrx-virtual/terminal`, readable by root only and kept 30 days. What you type without it being\nshown, such as a password, is not in the recording.\n\nThe terminal is on with FBRX Server. To turn it off, add `FBRX_V_TERMINAL=off` to the settings below and restart the\nservice; FBRX Virtual installed some other way has it off unless you set `FBRX_V_TERMINAL=on`."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Everyday commands","x":"```\nfbrx-server status                 where the console is, service and hypervisor state, certificate fingerprint\nfbrx-server doctor [--fix]         every service this server's roles need, on and running; --fix switches on what is off\nfbrx-server roles [add|remove|set] this server's roles, and changing them (above)\nfbrx-server update [check|commit|rollback]   updating FBRX Server (below)\nfbrx-server setup-code             the first-time setup code\nfbrx-server logs [-f]              FBRX Virtual's log\nfbrx-server logs ai [-f]           the FBRX core's log (agent, FBRX Mesh, Mesh Assist)\nfbrx-server restart                restart FBRX Virtual (virtual machines keep running)\nfbrx-server restart ai             restart the FBRX core\nfbrx-server command                FBRX Command (command role): address, certificate, setup token until set up\nfbrx-server logs command [-f]      FBRX Command's log\nfbrx-server restart command        restart FBRX Command (computers reconnect by themselves)\nfbrx-server users                  who can sign in\nfbrx-server reset-password   a new password for someone locked out\nfbrx-server create-admin [name]    another administrator\nfbrx-gate help                     FBRX Gate's own commands (gate role, see GATE.md)\n```"},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Updating FBRX Server","x":"```bash\nsudo fbrx-server update check     # is there a newer FBRX Server? If so: downloaded, checked, unpacked, ready\nsudo fbrx-server update           # what runs, what is ready to install, what came before\nsudo fbrx-server update commit    # install it\nsudo fbrx-server update rollback  # back to the version before the last update\n```\n\n* **check** reads the release list (`release.json` in the FBRX repository, its `server` entry) and, when it names a\n  newer version, downloads the bundle from the GitHub release, compares it with its published SHA-256 (refusing it if\n  they differ, or if there is none) and unpacks it into `/var/lib/fbrx-server-update/staged`. Nothing that runs\n  changes. `--file ` uses a bundle you brought along instead (a USB stick, or the copy inside\n  FBRX Endpoint's installer); `--force` gets a version ready that is not newer (to repair, or to go back on purpose).\n  `FBRX_SERVER_UPDATE_URL` in `/etc/fbrx-virtual/fbrx-virtual.env` points it at another release list.\n* **commit** keeps the running version aside (its programs, settings, service files and databases; virtual disks,\n  ISOs and AI models are never touched by an update), then runs the new version's own installer with this server's\n  roles, port and settings. Virtual machines keep running and the gate keeps routing; the console and the AI role\n  restart. If the installer fails, or the console does not answer within a minute and a half, the server goes back to\n  the version it had by itself, and the new one stays ready to try again. The whole run is logged in\n  `/var/log/fbrx-server-update.log`.\n* **rollback** puts the kept version back, its databases included (changes made in the console since the update go\n  back with them).\n\n`fbrx-server status` says when an update is ready. A server installed before `update` existed (FBRX Server 0.1.0)\nupdates by hand once, then with these commands from then on:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSLO https://github.com/samfoxhuntress/FBRX/releases/download/v1.9.8/fbrx-server-1.0.0.tar.gz\ncurl -fsSLO https://github.com/samfoxhuntress/FBRX/releases/download/v1.9.8/fbrx-server-1.0.0.tar.gz.sha256\nsha256sum -c fbrx-server-1.0.0.tar.gz.sha256  # says OK, or stop here\ntar -xzf fbrx-server-1.0.0.tar.gz\nsudo bash fbrx-server-1.0.0/install.sh        # keeps the roles, port and settings this server has\nsudo fbrx-server doctor\n```\n\nA server on 0.1.1 or later needs none of this: `sudo fbrx-server update check`, then `sudo fbrx-server update commit`.\n\nNo new ISO is needed for this, or for any update: the ISO is only for a server's first install."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Settings","x":"`/etc/fbrx-virtual/fbrx-virtual.env` (restart the service after a change):\n\n| Variable | Default | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `FBRX_V_PORT` | `9443` | Console port. |\n| `FBRX_V_DATA_DIR` | `/var/lib/fbrx-virtual` | Database, certificate, keys. |\n| `FBRX_V_IMAGES_DIR` / `FBRX_V_ISOS_DIR` | `…/images`, `…/isos` | Virtual disks and the ISO library (libvirt pools `fbrx-images`, `fbrx-isos`). |\n| `FBRX_V_TLS` | `self-signed` | `off` serves plain HTTP (only behind a reverse proxy). `FBRX_V_TLS_CERT` + `FBRX_V_TLS_KEY` use your own certificate. |\n| `FBRX_V_TLS_NAMES` | | Extra names for the self-signed certificate. |\n| `FBRX_V_SESSION_HOURS` | `12` | How long a sign-in lasts. |\n| `FBRX_V_MAX_UPLOAD_GB` | `32` | Largest ISO. |\n| `FBRX_V_DRIVER` | `libvirt` | `simulated` pretends (no virtual machines really run). |\n| `FBRX_V_LIBVIRT_URI` | `qemu:///system` | |\n| `FBRX_V_DOMAIN_TYPE` | | `qemu` forces software emulation (nested setups). |\n| `FBRX_V_CORE_URL` | `http://127.0.0.1:47821` | The FBRX core's Local API (ai role). |\n| `FBRX_V_CORE_TOKEN_FILE` | `/var/lib/fbrx-core/console.token` | Where the core writes the console token. |\n| `FBRX_V_ROLES` | `virtual` | The server's roles, comma separated (the installer writes it); the console shows what is there. |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE_WAN` / `FBRX_V_GATE_LAN` | `eth0` / `eth1` | The gate's ports, for its starter configuration. |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE` | `linux` | `simulated` pretends (nothing on the computer changes). |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE_FIRST` | | `commit`: put the starter configuration in place at the first start (the installer sets it). |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE_MANAGE_WAN` | | A private network on the internet side allowed to reach the console and SSH. |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE_LAN_ADDRESS` | | Your network's addresses in the starter configuration (default: 192.168.1.1/24, or the next free choice clear of the internet side's). |\n| `FBRX_V_GATE_WAN_ADDRESS` / `FBRX_V_GATE_WAN_GATEWAY` | | A fixed internet address in the starter configuration (both, or DHCP). |\n| `FBRX_V_FILES` | `linux` with FBRX Server | `simulated` pretends: shares and backups are folders in the data folder. |\n| `FBRX_V_FILES_DIR` | `/srv/fbrx-files` | The file server's share folders. |\n| `FBRX_V_COMMAND_URL` | `http://127.0.0.1:8788` | FBRX Command's local-only address (command role), where the console checks on it. |\n| `FBRX_V_TERMINAL` | `on` with FBRX Server | `off` removes the Terminal; `on` turns it on elsewhere (Linux only). |\n| `FBRX_V_TERMINAL_RECORD` | | `off`: do not record terminal sessions. |\n| `FBRX_V_TERMINAL_KEEP_DAYS` | `30` | How long terminal recordings are kept. |\n\n`/etc/fbrx-core/fbrx-core.env` (ai role; `sudo systemctl restart fbrx-core` after a change): `FBRX_SERVER_ROLES`, the\nroles this server announces (the installer writes it). Everything else about the core is set from *Mesh & AI*."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Security","x":"* HTTPS with a certificate checked by fingerprint; sessions are random tokens stored hashed; passwords are scrypt\n  hashes; sign-in is rate limited; the browser screen and the terminal use one-time tickets (30 seconds, one use).\n* The terminal is for administrators only, audited and recorded. On FBRX Server each terminal runs as a systemd service\n  of its own (outside the console's sandbox, so it sees the whole system) and is stopped when its browser tab goes.\n* The service runs as root because it drives the system hypervisor, moves interrupts and hands devices to machines;\n  systemd keeps home folders and kernel modules out of its reach.\n* virsh is called with argument lists (never a shell), and names, addresses and processor lists are checked before\n  they reach it.\n* The management controller's password is encrypted with a key kept in the data folder (`keys/master.key`, mode 600),\n  and so are the passwords backups use for other devices. File server accounts have no login on the server, and their\n  passwords are kept by Samba only.\n* The FBRX core (ai role) runs unprivileged and sandboxed by systemd (read-only system, only its data folder\n  writable); see *Mesh & AI* for how the console reaches it.\n* FBRX Command (command role) runs unprivileged and sandboxed the same way, as its own `fbrx-command` account; its\n  secrets file is readable by root only, and the console reads its status on a port only this computer reaches."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › API","x":"All under `/v1`, with `Authorization: Bearer ` from `POST /v1/auth/login`.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /setup`, `POST /setup`, `POST /auth/login`, `POST /auth/logout`, `GET /auth/me`, `POST /auth/password` | Setup and sign-in |\n| `GET/POST /users`, `PATCH/DELETE /users/:id`, `GET /audit` | Users and the audit log (administrators) |\n| `GET /host` | The server, the hypervisor and warnings |\n| `GET/POST /vms`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /vms/:id`, `POST /vms/:id/power` | Virtual machines |\n| `GET/POST /vms/:id/snapshots`, `POST …/:name/revert`, `DELETE …/:name` | Snapshots |\n| `POST /vms/:id/console` → ticket, WebSocket `/vms/:id/console/ws?ticket=` | The screen (VNC) |\n| `GET/POST /auth/app-tokens`, `DELETE /auth/app-tokens/:id` | Apps with read-only access (administrators; an app may give its own up) |\n| `GET /troubleshoot` | The network as the server sees it, for troubleshooting: the server's warnings, FBRX Gate and FBRX MiniDome (viewers) |\n| `GET /host/terminal`, `POST /host/terminal` → ticket, WebSocket `/host/terminal/ws?ticket=&cols=&rows=` | The server's shell (administrators): binary frames are the screen both ways, a text frame `{\"type\":\"resize\",\"cols\":…,\"rows\":…}` sets the size |\n| `POST /vms/:id/hostdevs`, `DELETE /vms/:id/hostdevs/:address` | Device passthrough |\n| `GET /storage/pools`, `GET/DELETE /storage/pools/:pool/volumes[/:name]` | Storage |\n| `GET /isos`, `PUT /isos/:name` (upload), `POST /isos/download`, `DELETE /isos/:name` | ISO library |\n| `GET/POST /networks`, `DELETE /networks/:name` | Networks |\n| `GET /hardware/topology`, `GET /hardware/flows`, `PUT /hardware/devices/:address/irqs`, `PUT /hardware/irqs/:irq`, `PUT /hardware/irqbalance` | Hardware map |\n| `GET/PUT/DELETE /bmc`, `POST /bmc/probe`, `GET /bmc/system`, `/sensors`, `/logs`, `POST /bmc/power`, `POST /bmc/boot-to-setup`, `GET/PATCH /bmc/bios`, `DELETE /bmc/bios/pending` | Server management |\n| `GET /files`, `PUT /files/mode`, `PUT /files/vm/autostart`, `POST /files/shares`, `PUT/DELETE /files/shares/:name`, `POST /files/accounts`, `PUT /files/accounts/:name/password`, `DELETE /files/accounts/:name`, `PUT /files/priority`, `POST /files/gate`, `POST /files/backups`, `PUT/DELETE /files/backups/:id`, `POST /files/backups/:id/run`, `POST /files/backups/:id/stop` | The file server (files role): administrators set it up, operators start and stop backups, viewers look |\n| `/gate/…` | FBRX Gate (gate role): see [GATE.md](GATE.md#api) |\n| `/dome/…` | FBRX MiniDome (minidome role): see [MINIDOME.md](MINIDOME.md#api) |\n| `GET /core`, `POST /core/call` `{ method, params }` | The FBRX core (ai role): `system.status`, `settings.get`, `ai.providers`, `mesh.status`, `mesh.assist.helpers`, `mesh.assist.sessions`, `approvals.list` (viewers); `mesh.assist.send`, `mesh.assist.followUp`, `mesh.assist.cancel`, `approvals.resolve` for Mesh Assist (operators); `settings.update` (Mesh Assist and AI provider settings), `vault.set` (provider keys), `vault.list`, `ai.models`, `mesh.setEnabled`, `mesh.rename`, `mesh.startPairing`, `mesh.cancelPairing`, `mesh.pair`, `mesh.removeDevice`, `mesh.setPermissions` (administrators) |"},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › Your servers in FBRX Endpoint","x":"*Lab → FBRX Server → Your servers* keeps the servers you manage: FBRX Server's console (port 9443) or any other web\npage a server or device has (a NAS, another hypervisor, a management controller). FBRX Servers paired on FBRX Mesh\nare offered there. *Open* shows the console inside FBRX, in a browser of its own:\n\n* its sign-ins, cookies and storage are kept apart from everything else in FBRX, and the page cannot reach FBRX\n  itself (no FBRX bridge, no Node.js, only clipboard writes and full screen allowed);\n* a certificate a public authority vouches for opens straight away. FBRX Server's own certificate is shown the first\n  time with its fingerprint to compare with the server's (`fbrx-server fingerprint`); once trusted, FBRX accepts\n  exactly that certificate at exactly that address and port, and stops with a warning if the server ever presents\n  another one;\n* links to the same server open in place (back, forward and reload in the bar); anything else opens in your own\n  browser, as does the button at the end of the bar.\n\n**Remote desktop.** An FBRX Server (a saved server on port 9443) opens as a remote desktop: its console, with the\ndashboard, the roles and every virtual machine's screen, and **Terminal**, a command line on the server over SSH under\nthe console (the host key is confirmed the first time, a password can be kept encrypted in the vault, and the session\nis also in Terminal → Device consoles, with the FBRX Server guide). **Full screen** fills the screen with it, and **In a\nwindow of its own** gives the server a window beside FBRX. Away from home, connect [FBRX VPN](VPN.md) first: the bar\nshows *FBRX VPN* when the server is reached through your gate, and a server that does not answer offers to connect it.\nWhen the server is the gate itself, its console, SSH and FBRX Mesh answer VPN devices while the gate's VPN has full\naccess (the default). The server has no graphical desktop of its own yet; the console is its screen.\n\n**Let Fabrix look.** On an FBRX Server in *Your servers*, **Let Fabrix look** lets Fabrix check the network through it\nwhen you ask it to troubleshoot: FBRX Gate (its internet side: link, address and router; your networks, DHCP and DNS;\na change waiting to be confirmed; problems it found) and FBRX MiniDome (whether its sensors work, and the worst open\nfindings). An administrator of the server signs in once. The server then gives this computer read-only access: it can\nlook at everything above but never change anything, and it never sees keys or passwords. The password you typed is\nnot kept; the access is, in your vault (unlock it if FBRX asks). **Stop** gives the access back. On the server,\n*Users & audit → Apps with access* lists every computer that may look, when each last did, and **Revoke**s any of them."},{"d":"docs/SERVER.md","t":"FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual › FBRX Server inside FBRX Endpoint (for now)","x":"Until FBRX Server has its own download page, the FBRX Endpoint installers carry it: the installer ISO, the bundle and\nthe guides, under *Lab → FBRX Server* in the app (copy them to Downloads, check them against the fingerprints taken\nwhen the installer was made, read the guides). A copy you build with **Install FBRX OS** carries the bundle and guides,\nand the ISO too when the `fbrx-server-iso` download (the zip, or the .iso) is in your Downloads folder; `--no-server`\nleaves them all out."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice","x":"**Stronghold → FBRX Shield** is where you see and choose what protects the computer from malware:\n\n* **FBRX Shield**: FBRX's own antivirus (below).\n* **Microsoft Defender**: built into Windows; also Microsoft Defender for Endpoint on a Mac or Linux computer that\n  has it.\n* **An antivirus already installed**: Sophos, CrowdStrike Falcon, SentinelOne, ESET, Bitdefender, Malwarebytes,\n  Kaspersky, Norton, McAfee/Trellix, Trend Micro, Webroot, Avast, AVG, Avira, WithSecure, Intego, Cylance, or any\n  other product registered with Windows Security Center.\n* **Automatic** (the default): an antivirus you installed that is on; otherwise Microsoft Defender when it is protecting\n  Windows; otherwise an installed antivirus even if it is off (so you hear that it is off); otherwise FBRX Shield.\n\nFBRX then shows that antivirus's status everywhere (FBRX Shield, Security, Dash, the agent, FBRX Command), runs\nits scans where it can, and raises an **Antivirus needs attention** alert when it is off, out of date or gone."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › What FBRX can see and do with each","x":"| | Status | Scans from FBRX |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| FBRX Shield | Everything | Quick, full, a folder; progress as it goes |\n| Microsoft Defender on Windows | Real-time protection, definitions age, last scan, active threats | Quick, full, a folder (`MpCmdRun`); definitions update; Defender's threat history in Security |\n| Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (Mac, Linux) | Real-time protection, definitions (`mdatp health`) | Quick, full, a folder (`mdatp scan`) |\n| Sophos on Windows / Linux | On and up to date (Windows Security Center) | A folder or the quick/full locations (`SophosInterceptXCLI scan --noui`, `avscanner`) |\n| Other products on Windows | On and up to date (Windows Security Center) | In the product's own app |\n| Other products on a Mac or Linux | Installed (FBRX cannot read their status) | In the product's own app |\n\n**FBRX Shield can always give a second opinion**: quick scan or a folder with FBRX Shield, whatever is active.\n\nWindows Security Center is not on Windows Server; there FBRX recognizes Microsoft Defender and ClamAV only."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › FBRX Shield","x":"Layers, in order:\n\n1. **The EICAR test file**, the standard harmless file antivirus products treat as malware, so you can check it works.\n2. **A threat database** of known-malware fingerprints (SHA-256), updated every day from a public feed (by default\n   abuse.ch MalwareBazaar's recent additions). If the feed asks for a key, add your free abuse.ch Auth-Key in\n   Credentials as `ABUSECH_AUTH_KEY`. You can point it at another list (one SHA-256 per line) or import a list.\n3. **FBRX's own rules** for suspicious files:\n   * programs disguised as documents (`invoice.pdf.exe`) or with hidden right-to-left characters in the name;\n   * scripts that delete backups and recovery points, turn off or exclude antivirus, download and run code, hide\n     PowerShell in Base64, decode and run hidden code, or abuse certutil, bitsadmin, mshta or regsvr32;\n   * cryptocurrency miner settings;\n   * from the internet (Downloads, the desktop, or Windows' Mark of the Web): risky file types (scripts, shortcuts,\n     disk images, screen savers…), Office documents with macros, and small archives that hide a script or a disguised\n     program.\n4. **Endpoint Ultra**: **ClamAV** (when installed) as a second engine, and **VirusTotal** for suspicious files (with a\n   `VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY` in Credentials: three or more engines calling it malicious makes it malware).\n\nWhat happens to a finding:\n\n* **Malware** (and the test file) goes into **quarantine** straight away (you can turn that off): moved into FBRX's\n  data folder, encrypted with its own key so it cannot run and other antivirus products do not trip over it.\n* **Suspicious** files wait for you: **Quarantine**, **Trust** (FBRX Shield leaves it and stops reporting it), or\n  **Delete**. **Ask Fabrix** explains a finding without opening the file.\n* From quarantine: **Restore** (put back next to its old name if something else is there now, checked against its\n  fingerprint, and trusted from then on) or **Delete** for good.\n* Each finding raises a **FBRX Shield found a threat** alert (desktop, phone, e-mail, webhooks and FBRX Command, as\n  set in Alerts). Deciding on the finding (Trust, Quarantine or Delete) takes its alert out of Alerts too.\n\n**FBRX's own files are never flagged**: its program folder, its data folder, its update downloads, `ProgramData\\FBRX`\non Windows, and any FBRX folder unpacked from the zip and built (for example in Downloads, with its `node_modules`)\nare left out of scans and download checks. Packages inside `node_modules` folders do not count as \"from the\ninternet\". After FBRX Shield's rules change (an update), it checks its suspicious list once more by itself and clears\nfindings that no longer apply; **Check the list again** does the same on demand.\n\n**One alert per scan**: a scan raises one summary alert (\"FBRX Shield found 3 suspicious files\"), not one per file,\nand download checks raise at most three alerts a minute, then one summary for the rest. **Trust all suspicious (N)**\ntrusts every suspicious finding at once (never malware), and right-clicking a finding trusts everything in its\nfolder.\n\n**Download checks** look at new files in Downloads and on the desktop as soon as they stop growing (unfinished browser\ndownloads are skipped). **Quick scan**: Downloads, the desktop, the temp folder and the startup folders. **Full scan**:\nyour home folder, the temp folder and the startup folders. Photos, music and video are listed but not fingerprinted,\nand files over 100 MB are not fingerprinted. **Skip these folders** leaves folders out of scans and download checks.\n\nFBRX Shield checks downloads, scans on demand and quarantines; it does not (yet) block programs as they start. On\nWindows, Microsoft Defender keeps running underneath, as Windows requires; on a Mac, macOS's own protection keeps\nrunning too."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › FBRX Shield › Editions","x":"Every edition (Essentials, Pro and Ultra) has FBRX Shield's download checks, scans, threat database, rules and\nquarantine, the choice of antivirus, and a **scheduled scan** (Settings on the FBRX Shield page: a quick scan every\nday or every week, at noon). **Endpoint Ultra** chooses when: picking a schedule shows *At what time?* (and *On which\nday?* for a weekly scan). A computer that is off at that time scans when it is next on, and a new or changed schedule\nwaits for its next time. Ultra also adds the second engines (ClamAV, VirusTotal). FBRX Command sets\n`protection.shield.schedule`, `scheduleTime` (\"21:30\") and `scheduleDay` (0 = Sunday) for a group like any setting. Student and child computers (FBRX OS Education and Home) run the download checks in the\nbackground without the page; the school or family chooses their antivirus from FBRX Command."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › The agent","x":"| Tool | Risk | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `shield.status` | read | Which antivirus protects the computer, its state and problems, and FBRX Shield's recent findings |\n| `shield.scan` | read | Scans a file or folder with FBRX Shield and reports (moves nothing) |\n| `shield.quarantine` | write | Quarantines a finding (with approval) |\n\nTrusting many findings at once (`shield.allowMany`) and **Check the list again** (`shield.recheck`) are for the person\nat the computer only."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › Organizations (FBRX Command)","x":"* Every computer reports its antivirus with its heartbeat: device detail shows **Antivirus** (name and state), and\n  the Health column says **Antivirus at risk** or **Antivirus needs attention**.\n* Choose the antivirus for a group in a configuration profile and lock it:\n\n  ```json\n  { \"protection\": { \"provider\": \"product:sophos\" } }\n  ```\n\n  with `protection.provider` in the locked settings. Values: `auto`, `shield`, `defender`, `product:` (`sophos`,\n  `crowdstrike`, `sentinelone`, `eset`, `bitdefender`, `malwarebytes`, `kaspersky`, `norton`, `mcafee`,\n  `trendmicro`, `webroot`, `avast`, `avg`, `avira`, `withsecure`, `intego`, `cylance`). FBRX Shield's settings live\n  under `protection.shield` (for example `\"exclusions\"` or `\"feedUrl\"`).\n* FBRX Shield findings reach FBRX Command through the alert channel for the organization."},{"d":"docs/SHIELD.md","t":"FBRX Shield and the antivirus choice › API","x":"`protection.status`, `protection.setProvider`, `protection.scan` (`engine: \"active\"` or `\"shield\"`),\n`protection.cancelScan`, `protection.job`, `shield.detections`, `shield.act` (`quarantine`, `restore`, `delete`,\n`allow`), `shield.updateSignatures`, `shield.importSignatures`, `shield.check`; events `protection.changed`,\n`protection.scan`, `shield.detected`. Choosing the antivirus, starting scans and acting on findings are for the\nperson at the computer only."},{"d":"docs/SUPPORT.md","t":"Support mode","x":"Support mode is the way an FBRX support engineer, or the person who runs an organization's FBRX Command, gets full\naccess to **one computer's** FBRX apps (FBRX OS and FBRX Browser) to troubleshoot, without that power being free for\nthe taking.\n\nIt takes a **support pass**: a short-lived token, signed by FBRX's own license key and tied to that one computer, that\nthe apps verify offline. While a valid pass is active the computer runs as Black Hole Edition with every feature on, settings\nan organization locked can be changed, and everything done is written to the audit log (and reported to FBRX Command\non a managed computer). Ending it, or the pass running out, puts the computer back exactly as it was."},{"d":"docs/SUPPORT.md","t":"Support mode › Using it","x":"1. Open **Settings → License → Support access** on the computer. It shows the computer's **install id**, with a copy\n   button: give it to whoever is helping you.\n2. They send back a support pass. Paste it into **Support pass** and choose **Start support mode**. (Pasting it into\n   the license box, Spotlight or FBRX Browser's address bar works too.) The computer goes into support mode: full\n   access, with a bar across every FBRX window saying so.\n3. When you are done, choose **End support now** (in Support access, or **End support** on the bar). The computer goes\n   back to the edition and settings it had. It also ends on its own when the pass runs out.\n\nOn a computer in an organization, its FBRX Command can also **start support mode for you**: the pass goes straight to\nthe computer, and a notification says support mode is on. Ending it works the same way.\n\nA pass is for one computer only. Nothing on a server changes."},{"d":"docs/SUPPORT.md","t":"Support mode › On an organization's computers","x":"A computer that belongs to an organization (joined to its FBRX Command) takes:\n\n- **A pass its own organization made** (in its FBRX Command) at any time. The organization already looks after the\n  computer; nothing else needs turning on.\n- **A pass from anyone else**, FBRX support included, only when the organization has turned FBRX support access on:\n  the managed setting `support.allowOnManaged`, off by default, set in FBRX Command's settings for its computers. Until\n  then such a pass is refused, so nobody outside the organization can unlock its computers, or settings it locked,\n  without its say-so. If the organization turns it off again, that support session ends at once.\n\nA computer on its own always takes a valid pass."},{"d":"docs/SUPPORT.md","t":"Support mode › Making a support pass","x":"**From FBRX Command** (an admin with the `licenses.manage` permission):\n\n- **Devices → the computer → Support pass.** Pick how long (1 to 12 hours) and what it runs as, and add a note (a\n  ticket number). **Start support now** sends it to the computer (this needs permission to send privileged commands):\n  support mode starts as soon as the computer is online. **Make a pass to paste** shows the pass instead, once, with a\n  copy button.\n- **Licenses → Support pass**, for any computer: paste its install id (Settings → License → Support access on that\n  computer), then **Make the pass**.\n\nThe same, by API:\n\n```\nPOST /v1/admin/support-passes\n{ \"deviceId\": \"\", \"hours\": 4, \"tier\": \"blackhole\", \"note\": \"Ticket 1234\" }\n```\n\n`installId` can stand in for `deviceId`. It returns `{ pass, expiresAt, tier, installId }`. To start it on the\ncomputer, send it the `support.start` command with `{ \"pass\": \"\" }`."},{"d":"docs/SUPPORT.md","t":"Support mode › Why it is safe","x":"- **A pass cannot be forged.** It is Ed25519-signed by the control plane's license key; a build only trusts passes\n  signed by the key it carries.\n- **A pass is for one computer.** It names that computer's install id; a pass for another computer is refused.\n- **A pass is short-lived.** At most 12 hours (4 by default), and it ends on its own.\n- **Everything is audited.** Starting and ending support, and every change made while it is on, are in the tamper-\n  evident audit log; on a computer in an organization, FBRX Command is told too. FBRX Command's own lists of sent\n  commands never show the pass itself.\n- **An organization decides for its computers.** Only its own FBRX Command, or someone it has allowed, can start\n  support mode on them, and its FBRX Command is told when a session starts and ends.\n- **It reaches the apps on a computer only**: FBRX OS and FBRX Browser. FBRX Command and FBRX Server have their own\n  administrator accounts; support mode does not touch them."},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"The built-in AI","x":"| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [llama.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp) (`llama-server`) | Running AI models on the computer | MIT | Official release builds, bundled with the app (or downloaded by it), run as their own program |\n| [Qwen2.5 1.5B Instruct](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), GGUF by [bartowski](https://huggingface.co/bartowski/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF) | The starter model every new computer gets | Apache-2.0 | Downloaded from Hugging Face by Install FBRX OS or on first start; not bundled |\n\nOther models in **AI models** are downloaded only when someone picks them, under their own licenses (shown beside each)."},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"Third-party components in voice","x":"Voice in FBRX OS runs entirely on the computer with these components. Models are downloaded on request from Hugging\nFace; code and data are bundled with the app.\n\n| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [Transformers.js](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers.js) | Running the speech models | Apache-2.0 | Bundled (npm `@huggingface/transformers`) |\n| [ONNX Runtime Web](https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime) | The engine under Transformers.js (WebAssembly) | MIT | Bundled (`dist/ort`) |\n| [Whisper](https://github.com/openai/whisper), ONNX conversions by [Xenova](https://huggingface.co/Xenova) | Speech to text | MIT | Downloaded (`Xenova/whisper-*`) |\n| [Kokoro-82M](https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M), ONNX conversion by [onnx-community](https://huggingface.co/onnx-community/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX) | The natural voices | Apache-2.0 | Downloaded (model, tokenizer and voice files) |\n| [HeadTTS](https://github.com/met4citizen/HeadTTS) language modules | English text to Kokoro phonemes (dictionary lookup and NRL letter-to-sound rules) | MIT, © 2025 Mika Suominen | Vendored in `apps/desktop/src/renderer/voice/kokoro/` with its license |\n| [CMU Pronouncing Dictionary](http://www.speech.cs.cmu.edu/cgi-bin/cmudict) 0.7b, in Kokoro phonemes (from HeadTTS) | Pronunciations | BSD-style, © 1993-2015 Carnegie Mellon University (notice kept at the top of the file) | Bundled (`kokoro/en-us.txt.gz`) |\n\nNo GPL component is included: the eSpeak NG phonemizer that other Kokoro front ends use is deliberately left out."},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"Third-party components in Calendar","x":"| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [ical.js](https://github.com/kewisch/ical.js) | Reading calendar links (iCalendar): repeating events, time zones | MPL-2.0 | Bundled (npm `ical.js`, unmodified) |"},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"Third-party data and components in FBRX Shield","x":"| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [abuse.ch MalwareBazaar](https://bazaar.abuse.ch/) SHA-256 exports | The default threat database feed (fingerprints only, downloaded at run time) | CC0 | Downloaded; the feed address can be changed |\n| [ClamAV](https://www.clamav.net/) | Optional second engine (Endpoint Ultra) | GPL-2.0 | Not bundled: used only when installed separately, run as its own program |"},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"Third-party components in FBRX Server and FBRX Virtual","x":"| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [noVNC](https://github.com/novnc/noVNC) | Virtual machines' screens in the browser | MPL-2.0 | Bundled in the console (npm `@novnc/novnc`, unmodified) |\n| [fast-xml-parser](https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser) | Reading libvirt's XML | MIT | Bundled (npm `fast-xml-parser`) |\n| [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) | FBRX Virtual's runtime | MIT | Downloaded by the installer (packed into the ISO) |\n| [Debian](https://www.debian.org/) 13 installer | The base of the FBRX Server ISO | DFSG-free (mostly GPL) | Downloaded from Debian when the ISO is built; FBRX adds files next to it and changes only the boot menus |\n| QEMU, libvirt, OVMF, swtpm | The hypervisor, UEFI firmware, software TPM | GPL-2.0 / LGPL-2.1 / BSD-2-Clause-Patent / BSD-3-Clause | Installed from Debian's packages, run as their own programs |\n| [PCI ID Repository](https://pci-ids.ucw.cz/) (`pci.ids`) | Device names in the hardware map | GPL-2.0+ / BSD-3-Clause | Read from the server's pciutils package at run time, not bundled |\n| nftables, dnsmasq, systemd-networkd, iproute2 (tc) | FBRX Gate: the firewall, DHCP and DNS, ports and VLANs, traffic shaping | GPL-2.0 / GPL-2.0-or-later / LGPL-2.1+ / GPL-2.0 | Installed from Debian's packages (gate role), configured by FBRX Gate, run as their own programs |\n| wireguard-tools | FBRX Gate's VPN keys and status (the VPN itself is in the Linux kernel) | GPL-2.0 | Installed from Debian's package (gate role). WireGuard is a registered trademark of Jason A. Donenfeld; FBRX Gate uses it and is not affiliated with it |\n| conntrack-tools | FBRX MiniDome: following new connections | GPL-2.0 | Installed from Debian's package (minidome role), run as its own program |\n| [qrcode](https://github.com/soldair/node-qrcode) | QR codes for VPN devices | MIT | Bundled (npm `qrcode`) |"},{"d":"docs/THIRD_PARTY.md","t":"Third-party components in Fabrix's files","x":"| Component | Used for | License | Where |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [unpdf](https://github.com/unjs/unpdf), with its build of [PDF.js](https://github.com/mozilla/pdf.js) | Reading the text of attached PDFs | MIT (unpdf), Apache-2.0 (PDF.js) | Bundled (npm `unpdf`, unmodified) |\n\nOffice documents (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx, OpenDocument) are read, and .docx documents and PDFs written, by FBRX's own code."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time","x":"**FBRX Time** makes whole-machine image backups and proves they work. An image holds everything: the operating\nsystem, the apps, the settings and the files, so a machine can be put back exactly as it was, even on a new disk.\n\nIt works in three places:\n\n| Where | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **FBRX Server** (the `time` role) | Backs up the server's virtual machines on a schedule while they run, keeps the images computers send it, and **checks every image by starting it** as a throwaway virtual machine. Restores an image as a new virtual machine. |\n| **FBRX Endpoint** (Windows, FBRX Endpoint 1.9.8+) | *Backup & restore → Whole computer*: an image of the computer, by hand or on a schedule, to a drive, a network folder or an FBRX Server. |\n| **FBRX Command** | *Image backup now* on a device's page, or the `backup.image` command (a device, a group or every device) from *Send command*. Each device's last image shows on its page. |"},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › The boot check","x":"A backup is only as good as the restore. So after FBRX Time makes or receives an image, it opens it the way a restore\nwould and watches it start:\n\n1. It makes a **throwaway copy-on-write layer** over each of the image's disks. The image itself is only read; nothing\n   the test machine writes reaches it.\n2. It starts a **temporary virtual machine** from those layers, with the same firmware as the original (BIOS or UEFI)\n   and its screen reachable only from the server itself. Its one network card is on a **private test network**\n   (`fbrx-time-check`, 10.254.254.0/24) that forwards nothing anywhere: it can ask for an address there and nothing\n   else, so a copy of a server never answers for the real one. (Settings → *See it come online* off: no network card\n   at all.)\n3. It lets the machine run (three minutes by default; *FBRX Time → Settings*), then looks at it:\n   * **Still running?** A machine that stops by itself, or never starts, **fails** the check.\n   * **The guest agent answered?** When the machine has a guest agent, its answer means the operating system is up.\n     That **passes**.\n   * **Did it come online?** A machine whose system starts its network asks the test network for an address. Getting\n     one **passes** (a server on a text console too), and FBRX Time also tries a ping (many computers' firewalls do\n     not answer one, which is fine). A machine with a fixed address does not ask, which is not held against it.\n   * **Its screen.** FBRX Time keeps a picture of the screen at the end (*Images → Details*). A desktop or sign-in\n     picture **passes**; a text console or a blank screen that did not come online gets a **warning** (it started,\n     but someone should look).\n4. It **shuts the machine down and deletes it**, and its throwaway layers with it.\n\nChecks run one at a time, after backups, so a busy server is not swamped. *Check* on an image runs one again\nwhenever you like. An image that passed reads **Tested: it starts**.\n\n**When a check fails or warns**, this server's AI (with the ai role) looks at what the check saw (how long it ran,\nthe guest agent, the network, the screen) and leaves its advice with the image: what most likely went wrong, how much\nto worry about restoring from it, and what to do next. *Images → Details* shows it; **Ask the AI again** asks anew\n(after a fix, say). Settings → *When a check fails or warns, this server's AI looks at it* turns that off."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › Virtual machines (FBRX Server)","x":"*FBRX Time → Backup jobs*: one job per virtual machine, with its schedule (every day or every week, at a time, or only\nby hand), whether to boot-check each new image, and how many images to keep (older ones are deleted after a new one is\nmade). *Back up a machine once* makes one image now, without a job.\n\nA running machine is backed up **live**. FBRX Time takes a short snapshot of its disks, copies them while the machine\nkeeps working, then merges the snapshot back so the machine ends up on its own disks as before. When the machine has a\nguest agent, it is asked to pause writes for the moment of the snapshot, so applications and databases are consistent;\nwithout one the copy is like pulling the plug (file systems recover, open databases replay their logs). A machine that\nis off is simply copied. Each image says which of these it was.\n\n**Restore** makes a **new** virtual machine from an image, stopped, with its own name and copies of the disks, on the\ndefault network. The original is never touched. Start it when you are ready (and turn the original off first if they\nwould clash on your network)."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › Computers › On the computer: FBRX Endpoint","x":"*Backup & restore → Whole computer* (Windows):\n\n1. **Where:** *A drive* (an external drive formatted NTFS; it keeps several images and only copies what changed),\n   *A network folder* (`\\\\nas\\backups`, with an account if the folder asks; Windows keeps the newest image there), or\n   *An FBRX Server* (paste the **connection code** from the server's *FBRX Time → Computers*).\n2. **When:** every day or every week at a time, or only when you ask. A computer that was off at that time makes the\n   image when it is next on.\n3. **Set up image backups.** Windows asks for administrator permission once. That makes a scheduled task\n   (`\\FBRX\\Image backup`) that runs as the system: it makes the image Windows itself makes (every volume Windows needs\n   to start, as virtual disks), even when nobody is signed in. The network folder's password is kept in a file only the\n   system and administrators can read.\n\nAfter that, *Back up now* (and FBRX Command's request) just starts the task: no more prompts. The card shows the last\nimage, when the next one is due and how many images the target holds. *Stop image backups* removes the task; the\nimages already made stay where they are.\n\nThe computer's other backup, the encrypted **snapshot**, is a different thing: it keeps FBRX itself (settings,\ncredentials, conversations, agent memory) and moves it to another computer ([BACKUP_RESTORE.md](BACKUP_RESTORE.md)).\n\nGood to know:\n\n* **Images are not encrypted** the way BitLocker protects the computer's own drive. Keep the drive somewhere safe, or\n  use the server's backup folder (only its own account can reach it).\n* If a computer's edition of Windows will not save an image to a network folder (some Home editions), it can save to a\n  drive instead.\n* Images of the whole computer are made on Windows in this version; macOS comes later. The snapshot works\n  everywhere."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › Computers › On the server: the backup folder","x":"The `time` role shares one folder for computers: `\\\\\\fbrx-time`, with an account of its own (`fbrx-time`, no\nsign-in to the server itself) and a long random password made at install. *FBRX Time → Computers* shows the folder, the\naccount and the password to administrators, and all three in one **connection code** for FBRX Endpoint. A computer\nwithout FBRX Endpoint can use Windows' own system image to the same folder.\n\nA network folder only ever holds a computer's latest image (Windows replaces it each time), so FBRX Time **keeps a copy\nof each one that finishes** in its own store (`/var/lib/fbrx-time/images`), keeps as many of each computer as\n*Settings* says, and boot-checks each new one when *Check computers' images when they arrive* is on. An image counts as\nfinished when nothing in it has changed for ten minutes; *Look now* checks straight away.\n\nOn a server that is also the gate (FBRX Gate), let your network reach the folder: a firewall rule allowing TCP 445\nto the gate from the computers' network."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › Computers › Putting a computer back","x":"* **From a drive or network folder:** start the computer from a Windows installer or recovery drive and choose\n  *Repair your computer → Troubleshoot → System Image Recovery*, then pick the image.\n* **From FBRX Server:** on *Images*, *Restore* a computer's image. FBRX Time copies it back into the backup folder\n  under `Restore\\-`; in *System Image Recovery* choose *Select a system image → Advanced → Search for a\n  system image on the network* and enter `\\\\\\fbrx-time\\Restore\\-`, signing in with the folder's\n  account."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › FBRX Command","x":"* A device's **Overview** shows *Whole-computer image*: not set up, set up with no image yet, the last image and\n  whether it worked, or *Making an image*.\n* **Image backup now** (on the device page, for devices with image backups set up) and the **`backup.image`** command\n  (*Send command*, for one device, a set, a group or the tenant) start an image on the computer, the same as its\n  *Back up now*. They need a role that may send privileged commands. A computer without image backups set up answers\n  that it is not set up; FBRX Command does not set them up for the person (that needs administrator permission on\n  the computer)."},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › Setting up the role","x":"```bash\nsudo fbrx-server roles add time        # or, in the console: Roles → FBRX Time → Change the roles\n```\n\nThe `time` role needs `virtual` (boot checks and restores run on FBRX Virtual). It installs Samba (for the backup\nfolder) and `qemu-utils`, makes `/var/lib/fbrx-time`, the `fbrx-time` account and its password\n(`/etc/fbrx-time/share.env`, root only), and shares the folder. `fbrx-server status` and `fbrx-server doctor` cover it.\nTurning the role off stops the sharing; the images stay in `/var/lib/fbrx-time`.\n\n| Path | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/var/lib/fbrx-time/images` | The images, one folder each (the disks, the machine's definition, the boot check's screen) |\n| `/var/lib/fbrx-time/incoming` | The backup folder computers write to (`\\\\\\fbrx-time`) |\n| `/var/lib/fbrx-time/lab` | Boot checks' throwaway layers while a check runs (emptied after) |\n| `/etc/fbrx-time/share.env` | The backup folder's account and password |"},{"d":"docs/TIME.md","t":"FBRX Time › API","x":"All under the console's API (`https://:9443`, a session or API token). Running backups and checks needs an\noperator; jobs, restores, deleting images and settings need an administrator.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /v1/time` | Status: space, the backup folder (password for administrators), computers, what is running |\n| `GET/POST /v1/time/jobs`, `PUT/DELETE /v1/time/jobs/:id`, `POST /v1/time/jobs/:id/run` | Backup jobs |\n| `POST /v1/time/backup` `{ vmId }` | One image now |\n| `GET /v1/time/images[?subject=]` | Images, newest first |\n| `POST /v1/time/images/:id/check` | Boot-check again |\n| `POST /v1/time/images/:id/advice` | This server's AI looks at a check that failed or warned (operators) |\n| `GET /v1/time/images/:id/screenshot` | The boot check's screen (PNG) |\n| `POST /v1/time/images/:id/restore` `{ name }` | A new virtual machine (or, for a computer, a copy in the backup folder) |\n| `DELETE /v1/time/images/:id` | Delete an image |\n| `POST /v1/time/computers/scan` | Look for finished computer backups now |\n| `PUT /v1/time/settings` | Boot-check computers, how long a check runs, images kept per computer, the online check (`checkNetwork`), the AI's advice (`aiAdvice`) |"},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault","x":"The Vault (Protect → **Vault**, Endpoint Pro and up) keeps your passwords and the keys FBRX uses, encrypted on\nthis computer. It has three parts:\n\n* **Passwords**: your sign-ins for websites and devices, which FBRX Browser fills in for you.\n* **Keys & tokens**: API keys, tokens and passwords that tools, plugins and connections refer to by name (an AI\n  service's key, an email password for alerts…).\n* **Security**: how the Vault is locked and unlocked, and how the password keeper behaves.\n\nEverything in it is encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a key only this computer, or your vault password, can open. See\n[SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md#credentials) for how the key is kept."},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault › Passwords","x":"Each sign-in has a **website** (or several), a **username**, a **password**, an optional **2-step key** and\n**notes**. All of it is encrypted, the site and username too: without the Vault, the database says nothing about\nwhere you have accounts.\n\n* **Search and filters**: All, Favorites, **Needs attention** (a weak password, one shared with another sign-in, or\n  one not changed in over a year) and **2-step codes**.\n* **Copy** the username or the password. A copied password stays out of FBRX's clipboard history and leaves the\n  clipboard after 30 seconds, unless you copied something else since (Security → *Clear a copied password…*).\n* **Show** the password (it hides again after 30 seconds), open the site in FBRX Browser, or **Edit**: the dice\n  button makes a new strong password, and the meter shows how strong one is.\n* **2-step codes**: paste the key a site shows when you turn on 2-step sign-in (or the `otpauth://` address its QR\n  code holds) and the Vault shows the six-digit code beside the password, as an authenticator app does.\n* **Earlier passwords**: when a password changes, the old one is kept (the last five), in case the change did not\n  take on the site.\n* **Import**: export your passwords as a CSV file from your browser or another password manager, then **Import** it\n  here. The columns are found by name (most browsers and password managers are understood); a sign-in the Vault\n  already has gets the file's password when it differs. Delete the exported file afterwards: it holds your passwords\n  in plain text."},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault › Save to Vault, from anywhere","x":"* **Add sign-in** on the Vault page.\n* **The password generator** (Workbench → Toolbox → Passwords): each new password has **Copy** and **Save to Vault**.\n* **The right-click menu**: on a filled password box, or on a password you picked in any text, **Save to Vault…**.\n* **Fabrix**: ask it to keep a password and it opens the Save to Vault window, filled in with the site and username,\n  for you to type the password there. Ask it to sign you up somewhere or change a password and it saves a sign-in\n  with a new strong password FBRX makes (after you say yes). Ask \"are any of my passwords weak?\" and it checks.\n* **FBRX Browser**, when you sign in to a site (below).\n\nWhen a new sign-in is for a site and username the Vault already has, saving updates that one instead of keeping two."},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault › FBRX Browser fills them in","x":"FBRX Browser has the Vault built in, like a password manager's browser extension:\n\n* Click into a site's sign-in box and your sign-ins for that site show beside it: pick one and FBRX fills in the\n  username and password. The key in the address bar does the same for a page with a sign-in form.\n* On a sign-up or change-password form, **Use a strong password** puts a new strong password in both boxes.\n* After you sign in with something the Vault does not have, FBRX offers to **Save** it (or to **Update** the\n  password it has for that site and username). **Never for this site** stops asking there (Security lists those\n  sites, to undo it).\n* The side panel's **Saved sign-ins** (the menu) lists this site's sign-ins first, with Fill in, Copy username and\n  Copy password, and **Add a sign-in**.\n\nHow it stays safe:\n\n* The sign-in helper in each page runs in a world of its own, which the page's scripts cannot see or call, and in\n  the page's main frame only (never in an embedded frame).\n* A sign-in goes only to a page on one of its sites (the core checks the page's address, not the page's word), and\n  one saved for a secure (`https://`) page is never offered on an insecure one. Sites on shared hosting (each name\n  under `github.io`, for example) count as different sites.\n* Nothing is filled in by itself: you pick the sign-in. It goes only into boxes you can see.\n* Private tabs can fill in sign-ins but never offer to save one."},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault › Fabrix and your passwords","x":"Fabrix never sees a password or a username. Its tools find sign-ins by name and site and report their health\n(*passwords.find*), open the Save to Vault window for you to type a password (*passwords.offer_save*), and save a\nsign-in with a password FBRX makes without showing it (*passwords.create*, which asks you first). Every password in\nthe Vault is also scrubbed from anything a tool hands back to a model, as the keys are."},{"d":"docs/VAULT.md","t":"The Vault › Locking","x":"The Vault unlocks by itself on this computer, unless **Ask for it every time FBRX starts** is on (Security). **Lock\nnow** clears the key from memory: sign-ins cannot be listed, filled in or saved until you unlock it with the vault\npassword or FBRX starts again. A backup carries the Vault, protected by the backup passphrase."},{"d":"docs/VPN.md","t":"FBRX VPN","x":"**FBRX VPN** (Orbit → **FBRX VPN**, Endpoint Ultra and up) connects this computer to your **FBRX Gate** from anywhere,\nso home is as close as when you are there: your FBRX Server and its console, its AI over FBRX Mesh (Mesh Assist and\nthe chats you send it, see [MESH.md](MESH.md)), and the devices on your network. It is the gate's own VPN (WireGuard),\nrun by FBRX on this computer."},{"d":"docs/VPN.md","t":"FBRX VPN › Set it up","x":"1. **On the gate** (your FBRX Server's console): *Gate → DNS & VPN → Add a device*. Name it after this computer and\n   choose what goes through home: **Only home** (your networks, and nothing else) or **Everything** (safer on public\n   Wi-Fi). The gate makes the device's keys and shows its settings **once**: copy them, or download the settings file.\n   Commit the change (the banner at the top) so the gate lets the device in.\n2. **On this computer**: *FBRX VPN → Add your gate*, paste the settings (or *Pick the file…*). FBRX keeps them\n   encrypted with the Vault, keys and all; delete the downloaded file afterwards.\n3. **Connect.** The system asks for administrator rights each time a tunnel starts or stops.\n\nAt home, behind another router (your provider's box), forward UDP **51820** to the gate. The gate's settings name its\naddress on the internet; if that address changes, give the gate a name that follows it (a dynamic DNS name) and put it\nafter `Endpoint =` before you paste.\n\n**Check home** pings the gate and each paired FBRX computer with an address behind the tunnel, and asks them over FBRX\nMesh: when your FBRX Server answers there, its AI can help this computer as at home, and your chats can think with it\n(*AI models → Your other computers' AI*, see [MESH.md](MESH.md#think-with-your-fbrx-servers-ai)). *Your servers* opens\nthe server as a remote desktop (its console and a command line) through the tunnel, see\n[SERVER.md](SERVER.md#your-servers-in-fbrx-endpoint). Pair your computers with the\nserver at home first (*Mesh & phone*); they find each other by the addresses they had there."},{"d":"docs/VPN.md","t":"FBRX VPN › What it needs on this computer","x":"| System | The tunnel runs with | Installing it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Windows | WireGuard for Windows (free): FBRX installs a tunnel service for it | *Install them* on the page (through `winget`) |\n| macOS | `wg-quick` from Homebrew's `wireguard-tools` | *Install them* (`brew install wireguard-tools`; needs Homebrew) |\n| Linux | `wg-quick` from `wireguard-tools`, started through `pkexec` | *Install them* (`apt-get` or `dnf`), or your package manager |\n\nOn Linux the gate's name server is used only when the system has `resolvconf`; without it, names at home are not\nlooked up through the tunnel, but every address works.\n\nOn Linux the tunnel runs by its name, `fbrxvpn`, from `/etc/wireguard/fbrxvpn.conf` (readable by root alone): Ubuntu\n26.04 confines `wg-quick` to that folder. The file is there while the tunnel runs, and goes when it stops or does not\nstart."},{"d":"docs/VPN.md","t":"FBRX VPN › How it stays safe","x":"* The settings are read strictly: only what a tunnel needs. A file that runs commands (`PreUp`, `PostUp`, `PreDown`,\n  `PostDown`) or carries other settings is refused, since the tunnel starts with administrator rights; the file the\n  tunnel runs from is rebuilt from what was read.\n* The keys stay sealed with the vault key. The settings file the system needs exists only while a tunnel starts or\n  stops, in FBRX's machine-local folder, readable by you alone, and never in a backup; while it runs, Windows keeps it\n  for its tunnel service and Linux in `/etc/wireguard`, readable by root alone.\n* Only you, at this computer, can add, connect, disconnect or remove a tunnel (not the agent, the Local API or FBRX\n  Command).\n* On the gate, a device reaches what its *access* allows (your networks, or the internet only); with full access it\n  may also reach the gate's console, SSH and FBRX Mesh (the server's own FBRX core).\n\nA tunnel keeps running when FBRX closes, like any VPN; *Disconnect* (or restarting the computer, on macOS and Linux)\nends it."}];
